(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1c.1fabe550c1.js"]={"16":[{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q01_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Atelectasis - lung collapse and effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man develops atelectasis of the right middle lobe 48 hours after abdominal surgery. A large mucous plug completely obstructs the lobar bronchus. Within 2-3 hours of the obstruction, the previously air-filled alveoli distal to the blockage begin collapsing. What mechanism explains this rapid alveolar collapse following complete airway obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Surfactant production immediately ceases when airways are obstructed, increasing surface tension","B":"Air trapped beyond the obstruction is absorbed into pulmonary capillary blood, removing alveolar gas","C":"Smooth muscle in alveolar walls contracts reflexively in response to obstruction","D":"Inflammatory cells migrate into alveoli and consume the oxygen, creating negative pressure","E":"Blood flow to the obstructed region increases dramatically, pulling air into circulation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Following complete airway obstruction, atelectasis develops rapidly because air trapped distal to the obstruction is absorbed by blood flowing through the pulmonary capillaries. The alveolar air contains oxygen (~100 mm Hg) and CO₂ (~40 mm Hg) that create concentration gradients favoring diffusion into the relatively deoxygenated mixed venous blood in pulmonary capillaries. Oxygen is absorbed much more rapidly than nitrogen because of its higher solubility and the large alveolar-capillary PO₂ gradient. Within minutes to hours, this gas absorption progressively removes air from the trapped alveoli. If the lung tissue is pliable (normal compliance), this air removal simply causes alveolar collapse—atelectasis. If the lung tissue is rigid or fibrotic and cannot collapse easily, the absorption creates very negative pressures within the alveoli (Guyton Ch 43), which then pulls fluid out of pulmonary capillaries into the alveolar spaces, filling them with edema fluid. This edema-filled pattern is typical of massive collapse of an entire lung. Immediate surfactant loss is incorrect—surfactant persists for hours to days. Alveolar smooth muscle contraction is wrong—alveoli lack smooth muscle. O₂ consumption by inflammatory cells and increased blood flow don't describe actual mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Obstructive atelectasis — air trapped beyond a blocked bronchus is absorbed within minutes to hours by blood flowing through the pulmonary capillaries, and pliable lung simply collapses; rigid fibrotic lung instead fills with edema fluid. FA: obstructive (resorptive) atelectasis from foreign body, mucous plug or tumor; prevented with incentive spirometry or PEEP. COMLEX: FA — atelectasis is dull to percussion with ↓ breath sounds and ↓ fremitus, trachea deviated toward the lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — the types of atelectasis and the causes of each","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"O₂ consumption by inflammatory"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q02_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Atelectasis - lung collapse and effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A premature infant born at 28 weeks gestation develops respiratory distress syndrome (hyaline membrane disease) due to surfactant deficiency. Chest X-ray shows diffuse bilateral atelectasis. How does lack of surfactant lead to alveolar collapse in this infant?","choices":{"A":"Surfactant normally prevents bacterial colonization; without it, infection causes collapse","B":"Surfactant normally stimulates respiratory muscle contraction; without it, breathing effort is inadequate","C":"Surfactant blocks pulmonary capillaries; its absence causes excessive blood flow that compresses alveoli","D":"Surfactant normally decreases surface tension 2-10 fold; its absence allows surface forces to collapse alveoli","E":"Surfactant normally increases airway diameter; without it, bronchial obstruction occurs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Surfactant is secreted by type II alveolar epithelial cells and coats the inner surface of alveoli, where it decreases surface tension by 2- to 10-fold compared to pure water. Surface tension creates inward-directed forces (following LaPlace's law: P = 2T/r) that tend to collapse alveoli, especially small ones. In premature infants with inadequate surfactant production, the surface tension of alveolar fluid becomes several times greater than normal, creating powerful collapsing forces. Without surfactant's surface tension-reducing effects, small alveoli collapse into larger alveoli or collapse completely. The increased surface tension also makes it extremely difficult to inflate the lungs—requiring much higher inspiratory pressures. This is why these infants experience severe respiratory distress and why exogenous surfactant administration (along with assisted ventilation and antenatal corticosteroids) has dramatically improved survival in respiratory distress syndrome. The more premature the infant, the less mature the surfactant system, and the more severe the atelectasis and respiratory distress. The other options incorrectly describe surfactant's functions—its primary role is surface tension reduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surfactant lowers alveolar surface tension 2- to 10-fold; in hyaline membrane disease of premature infants so little is secreted that surface tension rises several-fold and large areas collapse. FA: NRDS — surfactant deficiency → ↑ surface tension → alveolar collapse ('ground-glass' lungs); risk ↑ with prematurity, maternal diabetes and C-section. COMLEX: FA — an amniotic L/S ratio ≥ 2 indicates mature lungs, < 1.5 predicts NRDS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — surfactant's function, mechanism and developmental timing","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q03_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Atelectasis - lung collapse and effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman develops massive atelectasis of the entire right lung following aspiration of a foreign body that completely obstructs the right main bronchus. Arterial blood gases show PO₂ of 62 mm Hg despite the left lung functioning normally. Pulmonary angiography reveals markedly increased vascular resistance in the collapsed right lung. What mechanisms cause increased pulmonary vascular resistance in the atelectatic lung?","choices":{"A":"Surfactant accumulation in blood vessels increases blood viscosity and resistance","B":"Inflammatory mediators cause systemic vasodilation, shunting blood away from the lungs","C":"Lung collapse compresses and folds vessels, and alveolar hypoxia causes vasoconstriction","D":"Increased blood flow to the collapsed region overwhelms vessel capacity","E":"Air emboli from collapsed alveoli obstruct pulmonary arterioles"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Atelectasis increases pulmonary vascular resistance through two mechanisms: (1) Mechanical compression: as lung volume decreases during collapse, pulmonary vessels are compressed and folded, increasing resistance to blood flow through the collapsed region. (2) Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction: the collapsed, unventilated alveoli become hypoxic (low PO₂), triggering local vasoconstriction of pulmonary arterioles—a protective reflex that diverts blood away from poorly ventilated regions toward better-ventilated areas to optimize V/Q matching. The combination of mechanical compression and hypoxic vasoconstriction substantially increases vascular resistance in atelectatic lung tissue. However, this compensation is incomplete, so some blood still flows through the collapsed lung without adequate oxygenation, creating physiological shunt. This shunted, poorly oxygenated blood mixes with well-oxygenated blood from the normal left lung, causing arterial hypoxemia (PO₂ 62 mm Hg). The left lung cannot fully compensate because even healthy alveoli cannot \"super-oxygenate\" blood beyond ~98% saturation due to hemoglobin saturation plateau. The other options describe mechanisms that don't occur in atelectasis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Collapse raises resistance in the atelectatic lung two ways — the shrinking lung compresses and folds its vessels, and alveolar hypoxia adds vasoconstriction; in Guyton's example five-sixths of the blood then passes through the aerated lung, so desaturation stays mild. COMLEX: FA — chronic hypoxic vasoconstriction raises pulmonary vascular resistance and can lead to pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — pulmonary hypertension, including hypoxic causes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q04_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Asthma - immunologic process with mast cells","batch":1,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with known pollen allergy experiences an acute asthma attack during spring. She has previously developed high levels of IgE antibodies specific to tree pollen. When she inhales pollen, these IgE antibodies trigger mast cells in her lung interstitium near the bronchioles. What substances are released by activated mast cells during this allergic asthma attack?","choices":{"A":"Insulin, glucagon, and growth hormone causing metabolic changes","B":"Surfactant, mucin, and elastase that increase airway patency","C":"Acetylcholine, norepinephrine, and dopamine causing neurotransmitter effects","D":"Histamine, leukotrienes (SRS-A), eosinophil chemotactic factor, bradykinin","E":"Complement proteins, immunoglobulins, and interferons for immune defense"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When pollen antigens bind to IgE antibodies attached to mast cells in asthmatic patients, the mast cells degranulate and release multiple inflammatory mediators: (1) Histamine—causes immediate bronchoconstriction, increases vascular permeability causing edema, and stimulates mucus secretion; (2) Slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis (a mixture of leukotrienes, particularly LTC₄, LTD₄, LTE₄)—causes prolonged, powerful bronchoconstriction lasting hours (hence \"slow-reacting\"); (3) Eosinophilic chemotactic factor—attracts eosinophils to the airways, perpetuating inflammation; (4) Bradykinin—causes bronchoconstriction, vasodilation, and increased vascular permeability. The combined effects of these mediators produce: localized edema in bronchiolar walls, thick mucus secretion into bronchiolar lumens, and spasm of bronchiolar smooth muscle. These effects greatly increase airway resistance. The bronchiolar diameter becomes further reduced during expiration compared to inspiration because the increased expiratory effort compresses bronchioles externally, creating the characteristic expiratory difficulty in asthma. The other options list substances not released by mast cells during allergic reactions. Complement proteins, immunoglobulins, and interferons are not among the mast cell releases Guyton lists; IgE is the antibody bound to the mast cell, not a mediator it secretes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pollen binding IgE on interstitial mast cells releases histamine, slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis (leukotrienes), eosinophilic chemotactic factor and bradykinin, producing bronchiolar wall edema, thick mucus and smooth-muscle spasm. FA: type I hypersensitivity — immediate (histamine, tryptase, leukotrienes) and late (chemokines, eosinophils) phases. COMLEX: FA — Curschmann spirals and Charcot-Leyden crystals in sputum mark the eosinophilic airway inflammation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 6 — asthma's fundamental abnormality, an exaggerated Th2 response","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q05_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Asthma - immunologic process with mast cells","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with allergic asthma develops severe bronchospasm after exposure to ragweed pollen. Pulmonary function tests during the attack show normal inspiratory flow rates but markedly reduced expiratory flow rates (maximum expiratory flow reduced by 60%). Why does asthma cause greater difficulty with expiration than inspiration?","choices":{"A":"Positive pleural pressure in expiration compresses the already-narrowed bronchioles","B":"Inspiratory muscles are stronger than expiratory muscles, overcoming airway resistance more easily","C":"Pollen particles physically block airways only during expiration, not inspiration","D":"Mast cell mediators selectively paralyze expiratory muscles while sparing inspiratory muscles","E":"Inspiratory negative pressure dilates airways while expiratory positive pressure has no effect"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Asthma causes disproportionately severe expiratory obstruction due to dynamic airway collapse during forced expiration. In asthmatic patients, the bronchioles are already partially occluded by smooth muscle spasm, mucosal edema, and mucus secretions (from mast cell mediator effects). During expiration, especially forced expiration, the positive pleural pressure required to drive air out also compresses the outside of the bronchioles. Since asthmatic bronchioles are already narrowed, this external compression causes further severe narrowing or complete collapse, markedly increasing airway resistance during expiration. By contrast, during inspiration, the negative pleural pressure needed to expand the lungs actually \"pulls\" airways open, helping maintain airway patency despite the underlying bronchoconstriction. This explains why asthmatic patients often inspire quite adequately but have great difficulty expiring, leading to air trapping, increased functional residual capacity and residual volume, and over years, development of \"barrel chest\" from chronic hyperinflation. Clinical measurements show greatly reduced maximum expiratory rate and reduced FEV₁/FVC ratio, with relatively preserved inspiratory function. The other options don't explain the expiratory-predominant obstruction characteristic of asthma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In asthma the bronchiolar diameter shrinks more in expiration than inspiration, because expiratory effort compresses the outsides of already narrowed bronchioles; air enters but is trapped. COMLEX: FA — between attacks spirometry may be normal; in an exacerbation it shows an obstructive pattern that improves with a bronchodilator.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — the principle of maximum expiratory flow and dynamic compression","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q06_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Asthma - immunologic process with mast cells","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman with long-standing asthma visits her physician. She reports that about 70% of her asthma attacks are triggered by exposure to specific plant pollens. What immunologic mechanism explains why only certain allergens trigger her asthma attacks while other airborne particles do not?","choices":{"A":"Her airways have developed mechanical scarring that physically traps only pollen particles","B":"She has pollen-specific IgE bound to mast cells, which degranulate on antigen contact","C":"Pollen particles are larger than other particles and directly obstruct her bronchioles","D":"Her lung macrophages selectively recognize and attack pollen while ignoring other substances","E":"Pollen produces toxins that directly damage bronchiolar smooth muscle"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Allergic asthma involves a specific type I hypersensitivity reaction mediated by IgE antibodies. This patient has been previously sensitized to certain plant pollens, causing her B cells to produce IgE antibodies specifically directed against proteins (antigens) in those pollens. These IgE antibodies attach to Fc receptors on mast cells located in the lung interstitium near bronchioles and small bronchi. The IgE antibodies remain attached to mast cells, waiting for their specific antigen. When this patient inhales the specific pollen to which she is sensitized, the pollen antigens bind to the IgE antibodies on mast cell surfaces. This antigen-antibody binding cross-links IgE molecules, triggering mast cell degranulation and release of inflammatory mediators (histamine, leukotrienes, bradykinin, eosinophilic chemotactic factor). Other airborne particles don't trigger reactions because she lacks IgE antibodies specific to those particles—there's no antigen-antibody recognition and no mast cell activation. This explains the specificity of allergic asthma: only antigens matching existing IgE antibodies cause attacks. About 70% of younger asthmatic patients have this allergic pattern; older patients more commonly develop asthma from nonallergenic irritants. The other options don't explain the immunologic specificity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Allergic asthma (about 70% of patients younger than 30, per Guyton, mostly to plant pollens) depends on specific IgE bound to mast cells near the bronchioles; only the sensitizing antigen triggers release. In older patients the cause is almost always nonallergenic irritants such as smog. COMLEX: FA — test with skin prick or serum allergen-specific IgE.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — the etiology and phenotypes of asthma","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q07_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Tuberculosis - late-stage effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with untreated tuberculosis for 5 years develops progressive dyspnea. Chest CT shows multiple areas of lung tissue destruction with large abscess cavities and extensive fibrosis throughout both lungs. Pulmonary function testing reveals reduced vital capacity (2.5 L, predicted 4.8 L) and reduced breathing capacity. What pathophysiological mechanism explains his reduced lung volumes?","choices":{"A":"Tubercle bacilli directly paralyze the respiratory muscles, preventing full lung expansion","B":"Abscess cavities fill with air that cannot be expelled, paradoxically reducing measurable volumes","C":"Tuberculosis causes bronchospasm similar to asthma, trapping air and preventing full inspiration","D":"Extensive fibrosis stiffens the lungs and destroys functional tissue, lowering compliance","E":"The \"walling off\" process creates flexible tissue that hyperinflates the lungs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Late-stage tuberculosis causes progressive lung destruction with extensive fibrosis (scar tissue formation) throughout the lungs, along with formation of large abscess cavities and loss of functional lung parenchyma. Fibrotic tissue is stiff and non-compliant, requiring much greater respiratory muscle work to expand the lungs during inspiration. This increased \"work\" of breathing, combined with the reduced total amount of functional lung tissue, causes reduced vital capacity and reduced total breathing capacity. The fibrosis makes lungs less distensible (reduced compliance), limiting how much the lungs can expand even with maximal inspiratory effort. The tubercle bacilli cause this damage through an initial protective response: infected tissue is invaded by macrophages and \"walled off\" by fibrous tissue to form tubercles, limiting bacterial spread. However, when tuberculosis is untreated (occurs in ~3% of cases), this walling-off process fails, tubercle bacilli spread throughout the lungs, causing extreme destruction with abscess cavities. The combination of destroyed tissue, fibrosis, and reduced functional lung volume progressively impairs pulmonary function. The other options don't accurately describe tuberculosis pathophysiology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Late TB leaves many areas of fibrosis and less functional lung, increasing the work of breathing and reducing vital capacity and breathing capacity; it also thins the membrane area, thickens the membrane and disturbs V/Q. COMLEX: Guyton lists tuberculosis among the fibrotic 'constricted lung' diseases with reduced TLC and RV.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 49 → Obj 4 — primary versus reactivation tuberculosis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q08_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Tuberculosis - late-stage effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with advanced pulmonary tuberculosis undergoes pulmonary function testing. Her diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide (DLCO) is 45% of predicted. Arterial blood gases show PO₂ of 62 mm Hg and PCO₂ of 48 mm Hg. What mechanisms in late-stage tuberculosis cause reduced pulmonary diffusing capacity?","choices":{"A":"Tuberculosis increases surfactant production, which blocks gas diffusion","B":"Bronchospasm prevents oxygen from reaching alveoli","C":"Excessive mucus production dissolves the respiratory membrane","D":"Tubercle bacilli consume all available oxygen before it can diffuse to blood","E":"Fibrosis reduces membrane surface area and increases membrane thickness"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Late-stage tuberculosis causes reduced pulmonary diffusing capacity through two mechanisms related to the extensive tissue destruction and fibrosis: (1) Reduced total respiratory membrane surface area—the widespread lung tissue destruction with abscess cavity formation and loss of functional alveoli substantially decreases the total surface area available for gas exchange. (2) Increased thickness of the respiratory membrane—the inflammatory process and fibrosis (scar tissue deposition) increase the thickness of the alveolar-capillary membrane, increasing the diffusion distance for gases. Fick's law states that gas diffusion is directly proportional to surface area and inversely proportional to membrane thickness, so both these changes markedly reduce diffusing capacity. Additionally, tuberculosis causes abnormal ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) ratios: some areas are well-ventilated but poorly perfused (high V/Q, dead space), while other areas are poorly ventilated but still perfused (low V/Q, shunt), further reducing overall pulmonary gas exchange efficiency for both O₂ and CO₂. This explains her hypoxemia and mild hypercapnia. The other options don't describe actual tuberculosis pathophysiology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In late TB, reduced total respiratory membrane surface area and increased membrane thickness progressively diminish diffusing capacity, and abnormal V/Q further reduces O2 and CO2 exchange. COMLEX: FA — fibrotic (interstitial-type) restriction lowers DLCO and raises the A-a gradient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 9 — diffusing capacity, its definition and measurement","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q09_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Tuberculosis - late-stage effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A physician evaluates a 55-year-old man with late-stage tuberculosis who reports severe dyspnea with minimal exertion. The patient's lung tissue shows extensive fibrosis and multiple abscess cavities. Which pathophysiological change most directly explains his increased work of breathing?","choices":{"A":"Tubercle bacilli release toxins that directly stimulate the respiratory center, forcing rapid breathing","B":"Fibrotic tissue has low compliance, so the muscles must work harder to ventilate","C":"Abscess cavities create excessive dead space, paradoxically improving gas exchange","D":"Tuberculosis increases lung compliance, making lungs too elastic and difficult to control","E":"The \"walling off\" process increases airway diameter, causing turbulent flow"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The extensive pulmonary fibrosis characteristic of late-stage tuberculosis dramatically increases the work of breathing by reducing lung compliance (distensibility). Fibrotic scar tissue is stiff and non-elastic, making it much more difficult for respiratory muscles to expand the lungs during inspiration. Normal lungs require relatively modest inspiratory muscle force to create the negative pleural pressure needed for adequate tidal volumes. In fibrotic lungs, the same tidal volume requires much greater muscle force and energy expenditure because the stiff tissue resists expansion. This increased work of breathing is a major component of the dyspnea (air hunger) experienced by tuberculosis patients. Additionally, the reduced total amount of functional lung tissue means that to achieve adequate minute ventilation and gas exchange, the patient must work harder to ventilate the remaining functional alveoli. Over time, this increased work leads to respiratory muscle fatigue, further compromising ventilation. The reduced compliance also explains the reduced vital capacity seen on pulmonary function testing—even with maximal inspiratory effort, the patient cannot fully expand the stiffened lungs. The other options don't accurately describe the mechanical effects of pulmonary fibrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's first late-TB effect is increased work by the respiratory muscles, from widespread fibrosis and less functional tissue, with reduced vital and breathing capacity. FA: fibrosis lowers compliance (lung harder to fill). COMLEX: The pulmonary OMM lab — obstructive or restrictive disease raises work of breathing from about 2–3% of energy expenditure to as much as 30–35%; OMT aims to improve rib and diaphragm mechanics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — the respiratory muscles and chest-wall mechanics that do the work of breathing","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q10_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Causes of hypoxia - oxygenation, transport, cellular utilization","batch":1,"stem":"A physician encounters five patients with hypoxia from different causes: Patient A has COPD with V/Q mismatch; Patient B has carbon monoxide poisoning; Patient C has cyanide poisoning; Patient D lives at high altitude; Patient E has severe anemia. Which patients have hypoxia due to inadequate oxygen transport to tissues rather than problems with oxygenation or cellular utilization?","choices":{"A":"Patients B and E (transport problems)","B":"Patients A and D (pulmonary causes)","C":"Patient B only (hemoglobin dysfunction)","D":"Patient C only (cellular enzyme poisoning)","E":"All five patients (hypoxia always involves transport problems)"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hypoxia can be classified into five major categories: (1) Inadequate oxygenation (atmospheric deficiency like Patient D, or hypoventilation); (2) Pulmonary disease (V/Q mismatch like Patient A, impaired diffusion, or increased shunt); (3) Venous-to-arterial shunts; (4) Inadequate O₂ transport to tissues by blood; and (5) Inadequate tissue capability to use O₂ (cellular enzyme poisoning like Patient C). Patients B and E both have inadequate O₂ transport problems: Patient B with carbon monoxide poisoning has hemoglobin that is bound to CO instead of O₂ (forming carboxyhemoglobin), drastically reducing the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity even though arterial PO₂ may be normal. Patient E with severe anemia has insufficient hemoglobin available to carry adequate oxygen to tissues, again reducing oxygen delivery despite potentially normal arterial PO₂. In contrast, Patient A has a pulmonary oxygenation problem (V/Q mismatch preventing adequate blood oxygenation), Patient C has impaired cellular utilization (cyanide blocks cytochrome oxidase, preventing cells from using delivered oxygen), and Patient D has atmospheric deficiency (low inspired PO₂ at altitude). The distinction is important because oxygen therapy helps oxygenation and some transport problems but not cellular utilization defects. The other options incorrectly categorize these hypoxia types.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's hypoxia classification — (1) inadequate oxygenation in the lungs (low atmospheric O2, hypoventilation, pulmonary disease, venous-to-arterial shunt), (2) inadequate O2 transport (anemia or abnormal hemoglobin, circulatory deficiency, edema), (3) inadequate tissue use (cyanide, enzyme deficiency). COMLEX: FA — CO lowers SaO2 and O2 content with a normal PaO2; anemia lowers content with normal SaO2 and PaO2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 11 — carbon monoxide's effect on hemoglobin and O2-carrying capacity","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"In contrast, Patient A has a p","C":"Patient E with severe anemia h","E":"In contrast, Patient A has a p"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q11_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Causes of hypoxia - oxygenation, transport, cellular utilization","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old man is exposed to cyanide gas in an industrial accident. He develops severe symptoms despite his arterial PO₂ being 95 mm Hg (normal) and his hemoglobin saturation being 98% (normal). He remains profoundly hypoxic at the cellular level. What mechanism explains his cellular hypoxia despite adequate oxygen delivery?","choices":{"A":"Cyanide causes bronchoconstriction, preventing oxygen from reaching alveoli","B":"Cyanide blocks cytochrome oxidase, so tissues cannot use the delivered oxygen","C":"Cyanide binds hemoglobin more tightly than carbon monoxide, preventing oxygen loading","D":"Cyanide causes severe anemia by destroying red blood cells","E":"Cyanide increases oxygen consumption so rapidly that delivery cannot keep up"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cyanide poisoning causes a unique form of hypoxia: inadequate tissue capability to use oxygen. Cyanide binds to and blocks cytochrome oxidase (also called cytochrome c oxidase or complex IV), the terminal enzyme in the electron transport chain located in mitochondria. This enzyme is essential for cellular respiration—it catalyzes the final step of oxidative phosphorylation where electrons are transferred to oxygen to form water, with ATP production. When cytochrome oxidase is blocked, tissues cannot utilize oxygen for aerobic metabolism even when plenty of oxygen is delivered to cells. This explains why arterial PO₂ and hemoglobin saturation remain normal (oxygen is loaded onto hemoglobin normally and delivered to tissues normally), but cellular hypoxia is severe (cells cannot extract or use the delivered oxygen). The result is that venous blood returns with abnormally high oxygen content because tissues couldn't extract it, and cells must rely on anaerobic metabolism, producing lactic acidosis. This type of hypoxia is also called \"histotoxic hypoxia\" because the toxin directly impairs cellular oxidative machinery. Oxygen therapy provides no benefit because the problem isn't oxygen availability but rather the cellular machinery to use it. The other options don't describe cyanide's mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cyanide blocks cytochrome oxidase so tissues cannot use O2 even when plenty is available — the classic inadequate-utilization hypoxia. FA: cyanide inhibits complex IV; PaO2 normal, curve normal, ↑ lactate with anion gap metabolic acidosis; bright red venous blood and bitter almond breath. COMLEX: FA — 100% O2 is ineffective; give hydroxocobalamin, nitrites or sodium thiosulfate, and suspect it in fire victims.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 6 — intracellular PO2, metabolic rate and the O2 cells actually use","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q12_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Causes of hypoxia - oxygenation, transport, cellular utilization","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student categorizes different causes of hypoxia to understand when oxygen therapy will be effective. She creates a list including: atmospheric hypoxia at altitude, hypoventilation from neuromuscular disease, V/Q mismatch from pneumonia, severe anemia, carbon monoxide poisoning, and cyanide poisoning. For which conditions would supplemental oxygen therapy be LEAST effective?","choices":{"A":"Atmospheric hypoxia and hypoventilation (can increase inspired PO₂)","B":"V/Q mismatch and hypoventilation (can improve alveolar oxygenation)","C":"Atmospheric hypoxia and V/Q mismatch (can increase alveolar PO₂)","D":"Hypoventilation and cyanide poisoning (a ventilation problem and a cellular utilization problem)","E":"Severe anemia and cyanide poisoning (transport and utilization problems with normal PO₂)"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Understanding hypoxia classifications helps predict oxygen therapy effectiveness. Oxygen therapy is LEAST effective for: (1) Severe anemia—the problem is insufficient hemoglobin to carry oxygen; increasing inspired O₂ doesn't add more hemoglobin. Arterial PO₂ may already be normal, and hemoglobin that exists is already ~98% saturated, so supplemental O₂ adds negligible oxygen content (only tiny amount dissolved in plasma). (2) Cyanide poisoning—tissues cannot use delivered oxygen due to blocked cytochrome oxidase; supplemental O₂ provides essentially no benefit. Guyton (Ch 43) groups abnormal hemoglobin transport of O₂, as in carbon monoxide poisoning, with anemia as conditions in which O₂ therapy is of much less value, although CO poisoning is still treated with 100% or hyperbaric O₂ to speed displacement of CO from hemoglobin (Guyton Ch 41; First Aid 2025). In contrast, O₂ therapy is highly effective for atmospheric hypoxia (directly corrects low inspired PO₂), hypoventilation (increases alveolar PO₂ despite low ventilation), and V/Q mismatch (increases alveolar PO₂ gradient, compensating for diffusion/ventilation problems). Understanding these distinctions is crucial for appropriate therapy selection. Atmospheric hypoxia with hypoventilation, V/Q mismatch with hypoventilation, and atmospheric hypoxia with V/Q mismatch list conditions that respond well. Hypoventilation with cyanide poisoning pairs the utilization defect with hypoventilation, in which a person breathing 100% O₂ moves five times as much O₂ into the alveoli with each breath (Guyton Ch 43).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton — O2 therapy is of much less value in anemia, abnormal hemoglobin, circulatory deficiency or physiological shunt, because alveolar O2 is already normal (though 7–30% extra O2 can travel dissolved), and gives no measurable benefit when tissues cannot use O2. COMLEX: FA — in cyanide poisoning give antidotes, since 100% O2 is ineffective.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — hemoglobin's O2-carrying capacity and O2 delivery","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Atmospheric hypoxia with hypov","D":"Hypoventilation with cyanide p"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q13_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Oxygen therapy value in different types of hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with pulmonary edema has arterial PO₂ of 52 mm Hg due to impaired alveolar-capillary diffusion. Her physician starts oxygen therapy, increasing her inspired oxygen fraction from 21% (room air) to 100%. How does supplemental oxygen improve oxygenation in impaired diffusion?","choices":{"A":"Oxygen therapy directly removes fluid from alveoli, restoring normal diffusion distance","B":"Oxygen stimulates respiratory muscles to breathe faster, improving ventilation","C":"High inspired O₂ raises alveolar PO₂ from ~100 to ~600 mm Hg, widening the gradient","D":"Supplemental oxygen decreases pulmonary capillary blood flow, allowing more time for diffusion","E":"High oxygen concentrations directly repair the damaged alveolar-capillary membrane"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Oxygen therapy is extremely beneficial in diffusion-impaired hypoxia because it dramatically increases the pressure gradient driving oxygen diffusion from alveoli to blood. Normally, breathing room air (21% O₂) produces alveolar PO₂ of ~100 mm Hg, with a diffusion gradient of ~60 mm Hg (difference between alveolar PO₂ 100 mm Hg and mixed venous PO₂ 40 mm Hg entering pulmonary capillaries). When a patient breathes 100% O₂, alveolar PO₂ increases to ~600 mm Hg, creating a diffusion gradient of ~560 mm Hg—an increase of more than 800% (9-fold). By Fick's law, the rate of gas diffusion across a membrane is directly proportional to the pressure gradient. This massively increased gradient means that even though the alveolar-capillary membrane is thickened or damaged (in pulmonary edema, interstitial lung disease, or pulmonary fibrosis), the much steeper pressure gradient can compensate, driving enough oxygen across the impaired membrane to significantly improve arterial oxygenation. Clinical data shows blood in pulmonary edema patients picks up O₂ three to four times as rapidly with 100% O₂ therapy compared to room air. The other options describe mechanisms that either don't occur or aren't the primary benefit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In diffusion hypoxia O2 therapy can raise alveolar PO2 from about 100 to as high as 600 mm Hg, lifting the alveolar-to-blood gradient from about 60 to as high as 560 mm Hg — more than 800%; in pulmonary edema blood picks up O2 three to four times as rapidly. COMLEX: Supplemental O2 buys time while the cause of edema (often cardiogenic) is treated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — the categories of pulmonary edema and their causes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q14_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Oxygen therapy value in different types of hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A physician manages four hypoxic patients and must decide who will benefit most from oxygen therapy: Patient A has atmospheric hypoxia from altitude; Patient B has hypoventilation from opioid overdose; Patient C has severe anemia (hemoglobin 6 g/dL); Patient D has cyanide poisoning from smoke inhalation. Which patient(s) will show minimal or no improvement with supplemental oxygen?","choices":{"A":"Patients A and B (atmospheric and ventilation problems benefit most)","B":"Patient C only (anemia limits carrying capacity regardless of PO₂)","C":"All patients benefit equally from supplemental oxygen","D":"Patients C and D (transport and utilization defects, not low PO₂)","E":"Patient D only (cellular enzyme poisoning prevents oxygen use)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Oxygen therapy effectiveness depends on the type of hypoxia. Patients C (anemia) and D (cyanide poisoning) will show minimal improvement: Patient C with severe anemia has insufficient hemoglobin to carry oxygen. Her arterial PO₂ might be normal (~100 mm Hg), and what little hemoglobin exists is already ~98% saturated breathing room air. Supplemental O₂ cannot increase saturation meaningfully (already near maximum), and the small increase in dissolved plasma oxygen (from ~0.3 to ~2 mL O₂/dL blood) doesn't compensate for the massive deficit from low hemoglobin. She needs blood transfusion, not oxygen. Patient D with cyanide poisoning has blocked cytochrome oxidase—tissues cannot use oxygen for cellular respiration regardless of how much is delivered. His arterial PO₂ and saturation may be normal; the problem is cellular utilization. Supplemental O₂ provides no benefit. In contrast, Patients A and B benefit dramatically: Patient A's atmospheric hypoxia is completely corrected by O₂ therapy (directly increases inspired PO₂). Patient B with hypoventilation can move 5× more O₂ into alveoli with each breath when breathing 100% O₂ versus room air, markedly improving oxygenation despite reduced ventilation. Understanding these principles guides appropriate therapy. The other options don't correctly identify both non-responsive cases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: O2 therapy is 100% effective in atmospheric hypoxia and extremely beneficial in hypoventilation (about five times as much O2 per breath on 100% O2, though it does nothing for the CO2), but of much less value in anemia and none in tissue-utilization hypoxia. COMLEX: Guyton stresses O2 does not correct the hypercapnia of hypoventilation — reversing the cause (e.g., an opioid) does.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — hyperbaric O2, which raises the dissolved O2 that limited hemoglobin cannot","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"In contrast, Patients A and B "}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q15_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Oxygen therapy value in different types of hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with COPD and severe V/Q mismatch has PO₂ of 55 mm Hg on room air. His physician starts him on supplemental oxygen at 2 L/min via nasal cannula, increasing his inspired oxygen fraction from 21% to approximately 28%. His repeat PO₂ is 72 mm Hg. What mechanism explains the improvement despite continued V/Q mismatch?","choices":{"A":"Supplemental oxygen corrects the underlying V/Q mismatch by improving ventilation to low V/Q regions","B":"Oxygen therapy increases respiratory rate, correcting the V/Q mismatch","C":"Supplemental oxygen causes vasoconstriction in high V/Q regions, redistributing blood flow","D":"Higher oxygen concentrations directly dissolve the mucus plugs causing V/Q mismatch","E":"Higher inspired O₂ raises alveolar PO₂ even in poorly ventilated regions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In V/Q mismatch, some lung regions have low ventilation relative to perfusion—blood flows past poorly ventilated alveoli without adequate oxygenation. Supplemental oxygen improves oxygenation by increasing the PO₂ even in these poorly ventilated regions. Even though ventilation is reduced, the higher oxygen fraction in inspired gas means that what little gas does reach those alveoli has higher PO₂. This benefit occurs across all regions: normally ventilated areas reach even higher PO₂, and poorly ventilated areas achieve better (though still suboptimal) PO₂. The net effect is improved overall arterial oxygenation despite the V/Q mismatch persisting. This explains why oxygen therapy is moderately to highly effective in most pulmonary diseases causing V/Q abnormalities, including pneumonia, pulmonary edema, and COPD. However, care must be taken in COPD patients not to eliminate their hypoxic respiratory drive with excessive supplemental oxygen. Correcting the V/Q mismatch is incorrect—O₂ doesn't fix the ventilation problem. Increased respiratory rate, vasoconstriction in high V/Q regions, and dissolving mucus plugs don't explain the improvement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In V/Q mismatch, raising inspired O2 raises alveolar PO2 even in poorly ventilated units, so blood leaving them carries more O2; only true shunt units (no ventilation) are unaffected. FA: shunt (V/Q = 0) does not respond to 100% O2, whereas regions with some ventilation do. COMLEX: A good response to low-flow O2 favors V/Q mismatch over anatomic shunt.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 12 — VA/Q and its effects on alveolar PO2 and PCO2","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Correcting the V/Q mismatch is","B":"Increased respiratory rate, va"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q16_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Physiological consequences of systemic hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with chronic severe hypoxia (PO₂ chronically 50-55 mm Hg) from interstitial lung disease presents with progressive cognitive difficulties and memory problems. His family reports he seems confused at times and has difficulty concentrating. What physiological mechanism explains his neurological symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Low oxygen delivery causes depressed neuronal metabolic activity and impaired cerebral function","B":"Hypoxia causes brain tissue to produce excessive neurotransmitters, overstimulating neurons","C":"Chronic hypoxia increases cerebral blood flow so much that it causes intracranial hypertension","D":"Hypoxia stimulates excessive ATP production, depleting glucose stores in the brain","E":"Low oxygen levels cause neurons to switch to more efficient aerobic metabolism"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Systemic hypoxia causes depressed mental activity because brain neurons are exquisitely sensitive to oxygen deprivation. The brain has extremely high metabolic demands, consuming ~20% of total body oxygen despite being only ~2% of body weight. Neurons rely almost exclusively on aerobic metabolism (oxidative phosphorylation) to produce the ATP needed for maintaining ion gradients, neurotransmitter synthesis and release, and other energy-dependent processes. When oxygen delivery is insufficient (hypoxia), neuronal oxidative metabolism becomes impaired, reducing ATP production. This energy deficit compromises cellular functions including synaptic transmission, action potential propagation, and maintenance of membrane potentials. The clinical manifestations range from subtle (difficulty concentrating, memory problems, slowed mental processing) in mild-to-moderate hypoxia, to severe (confusion, altered consciousness) in moderate-to-severe hypoxia, to coma and ultimately brain death in severe hypoxia. The brain cannot store oxygen or significantly increase anaerobic metabolism to compensate, making it particularly vulnerable to hypoxic injury. This is why hypoxic patients often present with neurological complaints, and why restoring adequate oxygenation is critical for preserving brain function. Chronic hypoxia can trigger partial compensatory mechanisms (increased cerebral blood flow, increased hemoglobin), but these are often insufficient. The other options incorrectly describe neuronal responses to hypoxia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton — less severe hypoxia mainly causes depressed mental activity, sometimes culminating in coma, and reduced work capacity of muscles; severe hypoxia kills cells throughout the body. COMLEX: FA lists headache, confusion and coma among CO poisoning features — the same CNS sensitivity to low O2 delivery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — the acute effects of hypoxia on major organ systems","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q17_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Physiological consequences of systemic hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old man experiences acute severe hypoxia after near-drowning. Rescue breathing restores adequate oxygenation within 3 minutes. However, he initially exhibits reduced muscle work capacity and weakness. What cellular mechanism in skeletal muscle explains the reduced work capacity during hypoxia?","choices":{"A":"Inadequate oxygen impairs aerobic ATP production, forcing reliance on less efficient anaerobic glycolysis","B":"Hypoxia causes muscles to preferentially use fat metabolism, which is more efficient than glucose","C":"Low oxygen directly paralyzes the contractile proteins actin and myosin","D":"Hypoxia increases ATP production in muscle, causing muscle stiffness and reduced flexibility","E":"Hypoxia stimulates excessive muscle contraction, causing immediate fatigue"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Skeletal muscle work capacity is reduced during hypoxia because muscles must rely on anaerobic glycolysis for ATP production when oxygen is insufficient for aerobic metabolism. Normally, skeletal muscle generates ATP primarily through oxidative phosphorylation in mitochondria, which is highly efficient: complete oxidation of one glucose molecule via the citric acid cycle and electron transport chain produces ~30-32 ATP molecules. When oxygen is limited, muscle must rely on anaerobic glycolysis, which produces only 2 ATP molecules per glucose—a ~15-fold reduction in efficiency. This means muscles must consume glucose much more rapidly to maintain the same ATP production rate, quickly depleting local glucose and glycogen stores. Additionally, anaerobic glycolysis produces lactate, which accumulates in muscles and blood, contributing to metabolic acidosis and further impairing muscle contractile function. The result is rapid fatigue, reduced force generation, and decreased work capacity. Athletes at high altitude or patients with cardiopulmonary disease limiting oxygen delivery experience this as exercise intolerance and early muscle fatigue. Once adequate oxygenation is restored (as in this patient after rescue breathing), aerobic metabolism resumes, lactate is cleared, and muscle function gradually recovers. The other options don't accurately describe muscle metabolism during hypoxia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reduced muscle work capacity is one of Guyton's two main effects of less severe hypoxia; without enough O2, muscle cannot sustain aerobic ATP production. COMLEX: The pulmonary OMM lab notes that higher respiratory-muscle workload shifts toward anaerobic metabolism, which is less efficient and can contribute to acidosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 4 — exercise and the rise in O2 demand of working muscle","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q18_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Physiological consequences of systemic hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A physician lectures medical students about the physiological consequences of systemic hypoxia. She emphasizes two major effects that occur before death: depressed mental activity (sometimes culminating in coma) and reduced work capacity of muscles. Why are the brain and skeletal muscles particularly vulnerable to hypoxia?","choices":{"A":"Brain and muscle have minimal blood flow, making them first to experience oxygen deprivation","B":"Brain and muscle have very high metabolic demands requiring constant aerobic ATP production","C":"Brain and muscle lack mitochondria, preventing any aerobic metabolism","D":"Brain and muscle preferentially use anaerobic metabolism, which is impaired by hypoxia","E":"Brain and muscle store excess oxygen, which depletes rapidly during hypoxia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The brain and skeletal muscles are particularly vulnerable to hypoxia because both tissues have very high metabolic demands and depend heavily on aerobic metabolism for ATP production. The brain has the highest metabolic rate relative to mass of any organ, consuming ~20% of total body oxygen despite being only ~2% of body weight. Neurons require constant ATP production to maintain ion gradients (via Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase), synthesize and release neurotransmitters, and support synaptic transmission—processes that rely almost exclusively on oxidative phosphorylation. Skeletal muscles, especially during activity, also have high energy demands. During exercise, muscle oxygen consumption can increase 20-fold above resting levels. While muscle can temporarily use anaerobic glycolysis, this is inefficient (producing only 2 ATP per glucose versus ~30-32 from aerobic metabolism) and quickly leads to lactate accumulation and fatigue. Tissues with lower metabolic rates (adipose tissue, bone) or better anaerobic capacity (some glycolytic muscle fibers) tolerate hypoxia better. The sensitivity of brain and muscle to hypoxia explains why patients with systemic hypoxia present with confusion, cognitive impairment, muscle weakness, and exercise intolerance. In severe hypoxia, neurological decline progresses to coma, and muscle function is severely compromised. The other options are factually incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's two hallmark effects of systemic hypoxia are depressed mental activity (to coma) and reduced work capacity of muscles; both organs depend on continuous oxidative metabolism. COMLEX: Acute mental change in a hypoxemic patient is a red flag for respiratory failure — the OMM lab adds persistent accessory muscle use as a sign of impending failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — acute effects of hypoxia on the major organ systems","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q19_b1","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Hypoxia vs hypercapnia - coexistence and independence","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student reviews four patients: Patient A (COPD with hypoventilation) shows PO₂ 52 mm Hg, PCO₂ 68 mm Hg; Patient B (high altitude) shows PO₂ 55 mm Hg, PCO₂ 32 mm Hg; Patient C (anemia) shows PO₂ 95 mm Hg, PCO₂ 40 mm Hg; Patient D (cyanide poisoning) shows PO₂ 96 mm Hg, PCO₂ 38 mm Hg. Which patient(s) demonstrate(s) hypoxia and hypercapnia coexisting?","choices":{"A":"Patients A and B (both have low PO₂ and abnormal PCO₂)","B":"Patients B, C, and D (every type of hypoxia also causes hypercapnia)","C":"Patient A only (hypoventilation impairs O₂ intake and CO₂ removal)","D":"All four patients (hypoxia always causes hypercapnia)","E":"No patients (hypoxia and hypercapnia never coexist)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hypoxia and hypercapnia coexist primarily in hypoventilation and circulatory deficiency, but NOT in most other types of hypoxia. Patient A with COPD hypoventilation demonstrates both: hypoxia (PO₂ 52 mm Hg, normal ~80-100) because inadequate ventilation fails to bring sufficient O₂ into alveoli, and hypercapnia (PCO₂ 68 mm Hg, normal 35-45) because inadequate ventilation fails to eliminate CO₂. When alveolar ventilation is reduced, both gas exchange processes are impaired proportionally. Patient B at high altitude has hypoxia (low PO₂ from low atmospheric pressure) but hypocapnia (low PCO₂ 32 mm Hg) because the hypoxia stimulates hyperventilation through peripheral chemoreceptors, increasing CO₂ elimination. Patient C with anemia has hypoxia at the tissue level (inadequate O₂ delivery due to low hemoglobin) but normal arterial PO₂ and PCO₂—the blood gas tensions are normal even though oxygen content is reduced. Patient D with cyanide poisoning has cellular hypoxia (tissues cannot use O₂) but normal arterial PO₂ and PCO₂. This distinction is crucial: hypercapnia indicates that the problem involves ventilation or perfusion, not just oxygen delivery or utilization. CO₂ diffuses 20× faster than O₂, so impaired diffusion alone typically causes hypoxia without hypercapnia. The other options misunderstand when these conditions coexist.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton — hypercapnia accompanies hypoxia only when hypoxia is caused by hypoventilation or circulatory deficiency; low inspired O2, too little hemoglobin or poisoned enzymes do not raise CO2, and in diffusion defects any early rise in CO2 drives ventilation that corrects it. COMLEX: FA — hypoxemia at altitude drives hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis, so PaCO2 is low rather than high.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 5 — how O2, CO2 and H+ drive the control of respiration","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Patient B at high altitude has","B":"Patient B at high altitude has","E":"Patient A with COPD hypoventil"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q20_b1","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Hypoxia vs hypercapnia - coexistence and independence","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with pulmonary fibrosis has arterial blood gases showing PO₂ of 58 mm Hg and PCO₂ of 38 mm Hg (normal 35-45). Despite significant hypoxia, she does not have hypercapnia. What property of CO₂ versus O₂ explains why impaired diffusion causes hypoxia without hypercapnia?","choices":{"A":"CO₂ is smaller than O₂, allowing it to diffuse through pores that O₂ cannot access","B":"O₂ requires active transport while CO₂ diffuses passively","C":"CO₂ is produced faster than O₂ is consumed, maintaining normal PCO₂ despite diffusion impairment","D":"O₂ binds hemoglobin more tightly than CO₂, slowing its release for diffusion","E":"CO₂ diffuses about 20 times faster than O₂ because it is far more soluble"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Carbon dioxide diffuses approximately 20 times more rapidly than oxygen across biological membranes, including the alveolar-capillary membrane, because CO₂ is much more soluble in tissue fluids and lipid membranes than O₂. This difference in diffusibility means that in conditions causing impaired diffusion (pulmonary fibrosis, interstitial lung disease, pulmonary edema), oxygen transfer becomes significantly impaired (causing hypoxia) while CO₂ transfer often remains adequate (preventing hypercapnia). Even when the respiratory membrane is thickened or surface area is reduced, the high diffusibility of CO₂ allows adequate elimination. If hypercapnia begins to develop, even slightly elevated PCO₂ immediately stimulates central chemoreceptors, increasing ventilation, which corrects the CO₂ level while often being insufficient to correct the O₂ level. This explains the common clinical pattern: patients with diffusion-limited diseases or V/Q mismatch develop hypoxemia relatively early in disease progression, while hypercapnia appears only in very advanced disease when impairment is so severe that even CO₂'s high diffusibility cannot compensate. When hypercapnia does develop, it indicates severe pulmonary impairment. Guyton (Ch 43) also notes that the blood's transport capacity for CO₂ is more than three times that for O₂. The other options don't explain the diffusibility difference.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In diffusion hypoxia serious hypercapnia usually does not occur because CO2 diffuses 20 times as rapidly as O2; any rise in CO2 stimulates ventilation, correcting the hypercapnia but not necessarily the hypoxia. COMLEX: FA — diffusion-limiting disease such as pulmonary fibrosis causes early hypoxia rather than hypercapnia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 8 — diffusion rates of O2 versus CO2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q21_b1","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Hypoxia vs hypercapnia - coexistence and independence","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with severe COPD has PCO₂ of 85 mm Hg (normal 35-45 mm Hg). He is drowsy but arousable. His physician knows that further increases in PCO₂ could be life-threatening. At what PCO₂ levels do specific dangerous effects occur?","choices":{"A":"PCO₂ 60-75: air hunger; 80-100: lethargy/semicoma; 120-150: coma and death","B":"PCO₂ 50-60 mm Hg causes coma; 60-75 mm Hg causes respiratory arrest and death","C":"PCO₂ levels above 60 mm Hg cause hyperventilation that cannot be suppressed","D":"PCO₂ 45-50: lethargy; 50-60: semicoma; above 60 mm Hg: apnea and death","E":"PCO₂ levels do not affect consciousness; only PO₂ matters"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Progressive hypercapnia produces characteristic clinical effects at predictable PCO₂ levels: (1) At PCO₂ 60-75 mm Hg, patients experience severe dyspnea (air hunger) and are breathing as rapidly and deeply as possible. The elevated CO₂ maximally stimulates central and peripheral chemoreceptors, driving respiratory effort to its limit. (2) At PCO₂ 80-100 mm Hg, patients become lethargic and sometimes semicomatose. The very high CO₂ level begins to have direct depressant effects on the central nervous system, impairing consciousness. (3) At PCO₂ 120-150 mm Hg, anesthesia, coma, and death occur. At these extreme levels, CO₂'s depressant effects on the CNS, including the respiratory center, become dominant. A dangerous vicious cycle develops: high CO₂ depresses respiration → ventilation decreases → CO₂ rises further → respiration depressed more → culminating in respiratory arrest and death. This biphasic response (stimulation at moderate elevations, depression at very high levels) is critical to understand. In chronic CO₂ retention (like this COPD patient), renal compensation (bicarbonate retention) partially buffers pH changes, allowing tolerance of higher PCO₂ levels, but the progression described still occurs with acute increases or very severe chronic elevation. This patient at 85 mm Hg is in the lethargy zone and at risk of further deterioration. The other options don't accurately describe the PCO₂-effect relationship.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's hypercapnia ladder — alveolar PCO2 60–75 mm Hg: breathing as hard as possible with severe dyspnea; 80–100: lethargy, sometimes semicoma; 120–150: anesthesia and death, as CO2 now depresses respiration in a vicious cycle. COMLEX: Rising PCO2 with falling alertness signals impending respiratory failure and the need for ventilatory support.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 10 — composite effects of PCO2, pH and PO2 on alveolar ventilation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q22_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Atelectasis - lung collapse and effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman undergoes bronchoscopy that reveals a large mucous plug completely obstructing her right lower lobe bronchus. The bronchoscopist explains that if the plug isn't removed, the lobe will collapse within hours. The patient asks why the air doesn't just stay in the blocked lobe. What physiological process causes the trapped air to disappear?","choices":{"A":"The mucous plug physically compresses the air, forcing it back into the bloodstream","B":"Bacterial enzymes in the mucus chemically break down oxygen and nitrogen molecules","C":"Trapped gases diffuse down their gradients into capillary blood and are carried away","D":"The diaphragm creates negative pressure that actively sucks air out through the lung parenchyma","E":"Surfactant absorbs the trapped air and converts it into liquid form"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When an airway is completely obstructed, gases trapped in distal alveoli diffuse into pulmonary capillary blood down their concentration gradients. Oxygen diffuses readily because alveolar PO₂ (~100 mm Hg) greatly exceeds mixed venous PO₂ (~40 mm Hg) in blood entering pulmonary capillaries. CO₂ also diffuses but adds little to total volume. Nitrogen, though less soluble, slowly diffuses as well. The net effect is progressive removal of alveolar gas into the bloodstream, which carries it away systemically. This absorption occurs within minutes to hours depending on initial gas composition and blood flow. Once sufficient gas is absorbed, the alveoli either collapse (if tissue is compliant) or fill with transudated fluid (if tissue is stiff). The rate of absorption is faster if the patient was breathing high oxygen concentrations before obstruction because oxygen is absorbed much more rapidly than nitrogen. This principle explains why preoxygenation before anesthesia induction can accelerate atelectasis if airway obstruction occurs. The other options describe mechanisms that don't occur physiologically. The diaphragm cannot pull gas out through lung tissue; Guyton describes the trapped air as absorbed by the blood flowing in the pulmonary capillaries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trapped alveolar gas is absorbed by the blood flowing through the pulmonary capillaries within minutes to hours; pliable lung then collapses, while rigid fibrotic lung develops very negative alveolar pressure that pulls edema fluid in. COMLEX: FA — incentive spirometry and PEEP reduce postoperative obstructive atelectasis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — atelectasis types and their causes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q23_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Atelectasis - lung collapse and effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A neonatologist treats a 26-week premature infant with respiratory distress syndrome. Despite mechanical ventilation, the infant's lungs show diffuse atelectasis on chest X-ray. Analysis of tracheal aspirate fluid reveals surface tension 5 times higher than normal. Which cellular deficiency most directly explains the elevated surface tension?","choices":{"A":"Type I pneumocytes fail to provide adequate structural support for alveolar walls","B":"Alveolar macrophages don't produce enough surfactant proteins","C":"Pulmonary capillary endothelial cells have immature tight junctions","D":"Clara cells in the bronchioles secrete excessive mucus instead of surfactant","E":"Type II pneumocytes produce insufficient surfactant due to developmental immaturity"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Type II alveolar pneumocytes are the cells responsible for synthesizing and secreting pulmonary surfactant, a complex mixture of phospholipids (primarily dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine) and proteins that reduces alveolar surface tension. In premature infants, type II pneumocytes are developmentally immature and produce inadequate amounts of surfactant; surfactant production accelerates only after about the 35th week of gestation (Robbins Ch 10). This deficiency leaves the surface tension of the alveolar lining fluid high (about 50 dynes/cm, several times the 5–30 dynes/cm with surfactant), creating powerful inward collapsing forces that overcome the structural support of alveolar walls. The resulting widespread atelectasis characterizes respiratory distress syndrome (hyaline membrane disease). Fetal lung maturity can be assessed by measuring surfactant components in amniotic fluid. Treatment includes exogenous surfactant administration (derived from animal or synthetic sources) instilled directly into airways, along with antenatal corticosteroids given to mothers to accelerate fetal lung maturation. Type I pneumocytes provide surface area for gas exchange but don't produce surfactant. Alveolar macrophages perform phagocytosis, not surfactant production. Endothelial tight junctions and Clara cells incorrectly identify the surfactant-producing cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Surfactant comes from type II alveolar epithelial cells; in premature infants secretion is so depressed that alveolar surface tension becomes several times normal. FA: type II pneumocytes secrete surfactant and serve as precursors of type I cells; NRDS risk rises with prematurity, maternal diabetes and C-section. COMLEX: Antenatal corticosteroids and surfactant therapy have greatly improved survival (Guyton).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — alveolar cell types and their roles","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Alveolar macrophages perform p"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q24_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Atelectasis - lung collapse and effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with complete right lung atelectasis from bronchial carcinoma has arterial blood gases showing PO₂ of 58 mm Hg. A pulmonary angiogram demonstrates that blood flow through his collapsed right lung is only 15% of normal despite the lung receiving normal perfusion before collapse. What dual mechanism explains the markedly reduced blood flow through atelectatic lung?","choices":{"A":"Surfactant deficiency causes direct vasoconstriction independent of oxygen levels","B":"Inflammatory mediators from the tumor directly paralyze pulmonary smooth muscle","C":"Mechanical vessel compression from collapse plus hypoxic vasoconstriction reduce flow","D":"Atelectasis stimulates excessive nitric oxide production, causing paradoxical vasodilation","E":"Blood viscosity increases dramatically in regions of atelectasis, slowing flow"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Atelectatic lung experiences markedly increased pulmonary vascular resistance through two complementary mechanisms. First, mechanical compression: as lung volume decreases during collapse, the pulmonary vessels (arteries, capillaries, veins) are compressed, folded, and kinked as they are no longer held open by normal lung architecture. This physical compression increases resistance to blood flow. Second, hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (Euler-Liljestrand mechanism): collapsed alveoli are not ventilated, so alveolar PO₂ approaches mixed venous levels (~40 mm Hg). This regional hypoxia triggers vasoconstriction of small pulmonary arterioles in the affected area—a protective reflex that diverts blood away from poorly ventilated regions toward better-ventilated areas to optimize V/Q matching. In Guyton's example (Fig. 43.7), only about one-sixth of the blood still passes through the collapsed lung, close to this patient's value. However, compensation is incomplete—some blood still perfuses the collapsed lung without oxygenation, creating physiological shunt that contributes to systemic hypoxemia. This explains why his PO₂ is only 58 mm Hg despite having a functional left lung. The other options don't describe the actual mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Collapse compresses and folds the vessels of the atelectatic lung, and alveolar hypoxia adds vasoconstriction; most blood is diverted to the aerated lung (five-sixths in Guyton's example), so aortic desaturation is only mild despite losing a whole lung's ventilation. COMLEX: FA — hypoxic vasoconstriction shifts blood away from poorly ventilated regions, limiting the shunt.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 13 — physiological shunt and dead space","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q25_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Asthma - immunologic process with mast cells","batch":1,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with acute asthma exacerbation after exposure to cat dander. She reports her breathing difficulty began within 5 minutes of exposure and has progressively worsened over 30 minutes. Which mediator released by mast cells is primarily responsible for the prolonged bronchoconstriction lasting several hours?","choices":{"A":"Histamine causes the majority of prolonged bronchoconstriction lasting hours","B":"Leukotrienes (slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis) sustain constriction","C":"Bradykinin is the sole mediator responsible for all asthmatic bronchospasm","D":"Eosinophilic chemotactic factor directly constricts bronchiolar smooth muscle","E":"Prostaglandin E2 is the primary bronchoconstrictor in allergic asthma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The prolonged bronchoconstriction characteristic of asthma is primarily caused by slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis (SRS-A), which is a mixture of leukotrienes (LTC₄, LTD₄, LTE₄). When mast cells are activated by antigen binding to IgE, they release multiple mediators with different time courses. Histamine causes immediate bronchoconstriction within seconds to minutes but its effect is brief. In contrast, leukotrienes produce powerful, sustained bronchoconstriction lasting several hours—hence the name \"slow-reacting.\" Leukotrienes are synthesized from membrane phospholipids via the 5-lipoxygenase pathway and are released more gradually than preformed histamine. This sustained effect explains why asthma attacks can persist for hours despite removal from allergen exposure and why leukotriene receptor antagonists (montelukast) or 5-lipoxygenase inhibitors (zileuton) are effective controller medications. Leukotrienes also increase vascular permeability (causing bronchiolar wall edema) and stimulate mucus secretion, further contributing to airway obstruction. The combination of histamine's rapid effects and leukotrienes' prolonged effects creates the biphasic response in allergic asthma. The histamine option underestimates histamine's duration. Bradykinin, eosinophilic chemotactic factor, and prostaglandin E2 incorrectly identify the primary mediator of sustained bronchospasm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton singles out slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis — a mixture of leukotrienes — as the most important mast-cell product in the asthmatic bronchiole, causing edema, thick mucus and smooth-muscle spasm. FA: leukotrienes are released in the immediate type I phase. COMLEX: FA — montelukast and zafirlukast block CysLT1 receptors and are especially good for aspirin-induced and exercise-induced asthma; zileuton blocks 5-lipoxygenase.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 1 — the drug classes, including leukotriene modifiers, used in asthma","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The histamine option underesti"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q26_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Asthma - immunologic process with mast cells","batch":1,"stem":"A 19-year-old college student with allergic asthma undergoes skin prick testing to identify his specific allergens. Testing reveals positive reactions to ragweed, dust mites, and mold spores. His immunologist explains that he produces abnormally high levels of a specific antibody class. Which immunoglobulin mediates his allergic asthma responses?","choices":{"A":"IgE binds to mast cells and mediates type I hypersensitivity reactions","B":"IgG is the primary antibody responsible for allergic asthma","C":"IgM provides the initial response to allergens in asthmatic patients","D":"IgA in airway secretions triggers mast cell degranulation","E":"IgD on B cell surfaces directly causes bronchospasm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Allergic asthma is mediated by IgE antibodies in a classic type I hypersensitivity reaction. Individuals with allergic tendencies (atopy) have a genetic predisposition to produce abnormally large amounts of IgE in response to environmental antigens (allergens). When first exposed to allergens like ragweed pollen, their B cells differentiate into plasma cells that produce allergen-specific IgE antibodies. These IgE molecules bind via their Fc portions to high-affinity Fc receptors on mast cells located in lung interstitium near bronchioles. The mast cells become \"sensitized\"—coated with allergen-specific IgE, waiting for subsequent allergen exposure. Upon re-exposure, allergen binds to and cross-links the IgE molecules on mast cell surfaces, triggering degranulation and release of mediators (histamine, leukotrienes, bradykinin, eosinophilic chemotactic factor) that cause bronchospasm, edema, and mucus secretion. Serum IgE levels are typically elevated in allergic individuals, and allergen-specific IgE can be measured to identify sensitizations. Anti-IgE therapy (omalizumab) binds free IgE, preventing it from attaching to mast cells—an effective treatment for severe allergic asthma. IgG mediates type II and III hypersensitivity but not allergic asthma. IgM, IgA, and IgD don't play primary roles in allergic asthma pathophysiology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The allergic person forms abnormally large amounts of IgE, which bind mast cells in the lung interstitium near bronchioles and small bronchi. FA: type I hypersensitivity, tested by skin test or serum allergen-specific IgE; allergic asthma and anaphylaxis are its classic examples. COMLEX: FA links asthma with atopy and triggers such as allergens, viral URIs and stress.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 7 — genetic susceptibility and environmental factors in asthma","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"IgM, IgA, and IgD don't play p"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q27_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Asthma - immunologic process with mast cells","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist counsels an asthmatic patient about the difference between allergen sensitivity and actual allergen exposure. The patient asks why she doesn't have asthma symptoms constantly if her mast cells are always \"primed\" with IgE antibodies. What prevents continuous mast cell degranulation in the absence of allergen?","choices":{"A":"Mast cells automatically degranulate every 24 hours regardless of allergen presence","B":"IgE antibodies spontaneously detach from mast cells within minutes, requiring constant re-binding","C":"Cross-linking of IgE by allergen binding is required to trigger mast cell degranulation","D":"Mast cells are only present in lungs during active allergen exposure","E":"Histamine levels are too low to cause symptoms without allergen stimulation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mast cell degranulation requires cross-linking of surface-bound IgE antibodies by multivalent allergen molecules. In the sensitized state, allergen-specific IgE antibodies are already attached to mast cells via Fc receptors, but this binding alone doesn't trigger degranulation. The mast cells remain stable and quiescent. Degranulation only occurs when allergen (antigen) molecules bind to the Fab portions of multiple adjacent IgE antibodies simultaneously, physically cross-linking them together on the cell surface. This cross-linking brings together intracellular signaling components, activating phospholipase C, increasing intracellular calcium, and triggering the degranulation cascade that releases preformed mediators and initiates synthesis of leukotrienes. A single allergen molecule with multiple epitopes can bind and cross-link multiple IgE antibodies. Without allergen present, the IgE antibodies sit harmlessly on mast cells without triggering degranulation, which is why sensitized individuals only experience symptoms during allergen exposure. This requirement for cross-linking provides specificity—only the allergen that matches the bound IgE can trigger that particular mast cell. Automatic degranulation is incorrect—degranulation is stimulus-dependent. Spontaneous IgE detachment is wrong—IgE stays bound to mast-cell Fc receptors. Mast cells present only during exposure and low baseline histamine are factually incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FA — in the immediate type I reaction, antigen crosslinks preformed IgE on presensitized mast cells, causing degranulation; IgE bound alone does not trigger release. COMLEX: FA — omalizumab binds mostly unbound serum IgE and blocks its binding to FcεRI, used in allergic asthma with ↑ IgE resistant to inhaled glucocorticoids and LABAs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — the therapeutic rationale for each asthma drug class","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Mast cells present only during"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q28_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Tuberculosis - late-stage effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"In tuberculosis, macrophages invade the infected tissue and fibrous tissue walls off the lesion to form a tubercle, limiting spread of the bacilli. According to Guyton, in approximately what fraction of people with untreated tuberculosis does this walling-off process fail, allowing spread through the lungs with formation of large abscess cavities?","choices":{"A":"About 50%","B":"About 30%","C":"About 90%","D":"About 15%","E":"About 3%"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Guyton describes the tubercle as the product of macrophage invasion and fibrous walling-off, a process that protects against extension of the infection; if tuberculosis is not treated, the walling-off fails in about 3% of people in whom the disease develops, and the bacilli spread throughout the lungs, often destroying lung tissue and forming large abscess cavities. Late-stage disease then shows widespread fibrosis and reduced functional lung tissue, with increased work of breathing, reduced vital capacity, reduced diffusing capacity, and abnormal ventilation-perfusion ratios. Values of 15%, 30%, 50%, and 90% all greatly overstate the failure rate Guyton gives.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Macrophage invasion and fibrous walling-off form the tubercle; if TB is untreated this fails in about 3% of people in whom disease develops, and bacilli spread with extreme destruction and large abscess cavities. COMLEX: Late TB therefore leaves fibrosis, lost lung tissue, more work of breathing and reduced diffusing capacity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 49 → Obj 4 — primary versus reactivation TB pathophysiology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q29_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Tuberculosis - late-stage effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with healed tuberculosis but extensive residual fibrosis exercises on a treadmill during cardiopulmonary exercise testing. Her oxygen saturation drops from 96% at rest to 84% with moderate exercise. What mechanism explains exercise-induced desaturation in fibrotic lung disease?","choices":{"A":"Shorter capillary transit time in exercise leaves too little time for O₂ to cross thick membranes","B":"Exercise causes bronchospasm that physically blocks air from entering the lungs","C":"Increased cardiac output during exercise preferentially shunts blood away from functioning lung tissue","D":"Exercise suppresses respiratory drive, causing hypoventilation and reduced alveolar oxygen","E":"Skeletal muscles consume all available oxygen before it reaches the lungs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Exercise-induced desaturation in pulmonary fibrosis comes from the combination of a thickened alveolar-capillary membrane and a shortened capillary transit time. At rest blood spends about 0.8 second in the pulmonary capillaries, and normally it is almost saturated after only the first third of that time (Guyton Ch 39, Ch 41), so there is a large reserve. A fibrotic membrane slows O₂ transfer and uses up much of that reserve, but at rest the time is still enough to reach reasonable saturation (96%). In exercise the transit time can fall to as little as 0.3 second; across the thickened membrane this is no longer enough for equilibration, so blood leaves the capillaries incompletely oxygenated and saturation falls (84%). This diffusion limitation is a hallmark of interstitial lung disease, which is why these patients tolerate rest but desaturate with exertion. Supplemental oxygen helps by widening the alveolar-to-capillary PO₂ gradient. Exercise does not cause bronchospasm or suppress respiratory drive in fibrosis, rising cardiac output does not divert blood away from functioning lung, and muscles do not take up O₂ before it reaches the lungs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normally blood saturates in the first third of the capillary, leaving a threefold time reserve; a thickened membrane uses up that reserve, so the shortened transit of exercise leaves blood incompletely oxygenated. COMLEX: FA — in fibrosis O2 is diffusion limited and fails to equilibrate by the end of the capillary.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 12 — capillary transit time and gas exchange efficiency","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Exercise does not cause bronch"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q30_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — Tuberculosis - late-stage effects on pulmonary function","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist examines lung tissue from a patient who died from complications of tuberculosis. The tissue shows dense fibrosis, calcified granulomas, and areas of cavitation. Compared with normal lungs, these tuberculosis-affected lungs are markedly heavier. What accounts for the increased lung weight?","choices":{"A":"Increased air trapping causes lung hyperinflation, increasing total mass","B":"Fibrotic scar tissue and calcification add mass to the lung parenchyma","C":"Pulmonary edema fluid accumulation is the primary cause of weight increase","D":"Tubercle bacilli themselves contribute significantly to total lung mass","E":"Compensatory hypertrophy of remaining functional alveoli increases tissue mass"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The increased lung weight in chronic tuberculosis results from extensive deposition of fibrotic (scar) tissue throughout the lung parenchyma. Fibrosis involves replacement of normal delicate lung architecture with dense collagen and other extracellular matrix proteins. Each tubercle (granuloma) begins with macrophage infiltration and becomes surrounded by fibrous tissue attempting to wall off the infection. In untreated or extensive disease, large areas of lung become replaced by fibrotic tissue. Additionally, many tubercles undergo dystrophic calcification—deposition of calcium salts in necrotic tissue—further increasing mass. Dense fibrous tissue and calcified granulomas are much heavier than normal air-filled alveoli and thin-walled capillaries. While cavitation (abscess formation) destroys some tissue, the overall balance favors mass increase from fibrosis. This increased tissue density is visible on chest CT as areas of increased attenuation. The heavy, stiff, fibrotic lungs are difficult to inflate, explaining the restrictive physiology. Importantly, this fibrotic tissue is non-functional—it doesn't participate in gas exchange, contributing to reduced diffusing capacity despite increased total tissue mass. Air trapping is incorrect—TB causes restriction, not air trapping. Edema fluid might contribute acutely but isn't the chronic cause. The bacilli themselves and compensatory hypertrophy don't explain the weight increase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Late tuberculosis is characterized by many areas of fibrosis throughout the lungs with reduced functional lung tissue — fibrous scar replaces the delicate air-filled parenchyma. COMLEX: FA — primary TB forms a Ghon complex (Ghon focus plus hilar nodes) that heals by fibrosis and calcification, while reactivation produces fibrocaseous cavitary upper-lobe lesions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 49 → Obj 4 — primary and reactivation TB pathophysiology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q31_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Causes of hypoxia - oxygenation, transport, cellular utilization","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with long-standing alcohol use disorder and poor nutrition is diagnosed with beriberi from vitamin B₁ deficiency. His arterial PO₂ and hemoglobin are normal. Guyton cites beriberi as a special example within his classification of hypoxia. Into which category does it fall?","choices":{"A":"Inadequate tissue capability to use O₂","B":"Inadequate oxygenation from hypoventilation","C":"Inadequate O₂ transport from anemia","D":"Venous-to-arterial shunting of blood","E":"Localized circulatory deficiency"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton's third category of hypoxia is inadequate tissue capability of using O₂, with cyanide poisoning of cytochrome oxidase as the classic cause; he adds that deficiencies of cellular oxidative enzymes can do the same, and that in beriberi several important steps in tissue use of O₂ and formation of CO₂ are compromised because of vitamin B deficiency. Hypoventilation and venous-to-arterial shunting fall under inadequate oxygenation of the blood in the lungs, which would lower arterial PO₂. Anemia and localized circulatory deficiency fall under inadequate O₂ transport to the tissues; his hemoglobin is normal, and nothing points to a regional perfusion defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's inadequate-utilization hypoxia — cyanide is classic, but deficient oxidative enzymes do the same; in beriberi, vitamin B1 deficiency compromises tissue O2 use and CO2 formation. FA: thiamine (TPP) is a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase and α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase. COMLEX: Give thiamine before glucose in malnourished patients with alcohol use disorder to avoid precipitating Wernicke encephalopathy (FA).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 6 — intracellular PO2 and cellular O2 use","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q32_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Causes of hypoxia - oxygenation, transport, cellular utilization","batch":1,"stem":"A toxicology fellow asks why cyanide poisoning causes rapid death while chronic anemia causes gradual functional decline, even though both can result in similar reductions in oxygen availability to tissues. What fundamental difference in pathophysiology explains the different time courses?","choices":{"A":"Cyanide causes immediate hemoglobin destruction while anemia develops gradually","B":"Anemia allows oxygen delivery through increased cardiac output while cyanide paralyzes the heart","C":"Cyanide halts ATP production at once; anemia allows compensation through higher extraction","D":"Cyanide poisoning causes irreversible brain damage while anemia causes reversible changes","E":"Anemic patients have normal venous oxygen content while cyanide patients have low venous oxygen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The fundamental difference is that cyanide causes immediate cellular energy crisis by blocking ATP production, while anemia allows compensatory mechanisms time to partially adapt. Cyanide binds cytochrome oxidase (complex IV of the electron transport chain), immediately preventing cells from using oxygen for oxidative phosphorylation. Mitochondrial ATP production ceases within seconds, causing rapid cellular dysfunction, especially in high-energy-demand tissues (brain, heart). Cells must rely on anaerobic glycolysis alone, producing ~15-fold less ATP per glucose molecule, quickly depleting energy stores and causing rapid organ failure and death (minutes to hours). In contrast, chronic anemia develops gradually, allowing compensatory mechanisms: increased cardiac output delivers more blood (and thus more total oxygen) despite low hemoglobin concentration; rightward shift of the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve (via increased 2,3-DPG) enhances oxygen unloading at tissues; increased capillary density in some tissues improves extraction efficiency; tissues develop increased tolerance to lower oxygen tensions. While these mechanisms are incomplete, they allow survival and function at hemoglobin levels that would be poorly tolerated if they developed acutely. Additionally, in anemia, tissues can extract proportionally more oxygen from delivered blood, resulting in low venous oxygen content. In cyanide poisoning, tissues cannot extract oxygen despite adequate delivery, resulting in paradoxically high venous oxygen content. The other options contain factual errors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cyanide blocks cytochrome oxidase so cells cannot use the O2 they receive; in anemia O2 transport falls but tissues can extract a larger fraction (utilization coefficient normally about 25%). FA: cyanide raises lactate with an anion gap acidosis and needs antidotes, since 100% O2 is ineffective. COMLEX: Anemic patients do not become cyanotic — there is not enough hemoglobin for 5 g/dL to be deoxygenated (Guyton).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 4 — how O2 extraction rises to meet demand","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"In cyanide poisoning, tissues "}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q33_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Causes of hypoxia - oxygenation, transport, cellular utilization","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student creates a diagram categorizing causes of hypoxia to predict which patients benefit from supplemental oxygen. She groups: (Group 1) high-altitude exposure, hypoventilation from opioids, pneumonia with V/Q mismatch; (Group 2) severe anemia, carbon monoxide poisoning; (Group 3) cyanide poisoning, beriberi. What distinguishes these three groups?","choices":{"A":"Group 1 involves cardiac problems; Groups 2 and 3 involve primary lung problems","B":"All three groups benefit equally from supplemental oxygen therapy","C":"Group 1 has low tissue PO₂; Groups 2 and 3 have normal tissue PO₂","D":"Group 1 affects only children; Groups 2 and 3 affect only adults","E":"Group 1 lacks oxygenation, Group 2 lacks transport, Group 3 lacks utilization"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This categorization correctly separates hypoxia types by the level at which oxygen delivery fails. Group 1 (altitude, hypoventilation, pneumonia) represents inadequate oxygenation of blood—the problem is getting oxygen from atmosphere into arterial blood. Altitude reduces inspired PO₂; hypoventilation reduces alveolar PO₂; pneumonia causes V/Q mismatch and impaired diffusion. All result in low arterial PO₂. These respond excellently to supplemental O₂ which directly corrects the oxygenation problem. Group 2 (anemia, CO poisoning) represents inadequate oxygen transport—blood reaches lungs and may achieve normal or near-normal PO₂, but inadequate hemoglobin (anemia) or non-functional hemoglobin (carboxyhemoglobin from CO) prevents adequate oxygen content in blood reaching tissues. Anemia responds poorly to supplemental O₂ because the problem is carrying capacity, not oxygenation; CO poisoning is the exception — high-concentration O₂ speeds displacement of CO from hemoglobin. Group 3 (cyanide, beriberi) represents inadequate cellular utilization—oxygen is delivered to tissues adequately (normal arterial PO₂ and content), but cellular enzyme systems cannot use it (cyanide blocks cytochrome oxidase; beriberi/thiamine deficiency impairs multiple oxidative enzymes). These don't respond to supplemental O₂ at all—increasing delivered oxygen doesn't help if cells cannot use it. This classification is clinically crucial for predicting treatment response and selecting appropriate interventions. The other options incorrectly characterize these groups.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's three tiers — inadequate oxygenation of blood in the lungs, inadequate O2 transport by the blood, inadequate tissue capability to use O2 — predict O2 therapy: great value in the first, much less in the second, none in the third. COMLEX: FA — CO lowers SaO2 and content, methemoglobin lowers both, and cyanide leaves all O2 values normal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 11 — carbon monoxide as a hemoglobin-level transport defect","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Anemia responds poorly to supp"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q34_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Oxygen therapy value in different types of hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with COPD and chronic hypercapnia (baseline PCO₂ 58 mm Hg) is admitted with pneumonia. His PO₂ is 48 mm Hg on room air. The intern wants to start high-flow oxygen to normalize his PO₂. Why does the attending physician recommend cautious low-flow oxygen instead?","choices":{"A":"High-flow oxygen will cause immediate oxygen toxicity and pulmonary fibrosis","B":"Removing hypoxic drive in chronic CO₂ retainers can worsen hypoventilation and PCO₂","C":"Low oxygen saturation is beneficial and should be maintained to stimulate erythropoiesis","D":"High-flow oxygen causes bronchoconstriction in COPD patients, worsening airflow obstruction","E":"Oxygen therapy has no benefit in patients with chronic lung disease"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Guyton Ch 42 explains why a chronic CO₂ retainer's breathing leans on O₂ lack: CO₂ excites the respiratory center strongly for the first few hours, but over the next 1 to 2 days that effect falls to about one-fifth, because the kidneys raise blood HCO₃⁻ and HCO₃⁻ diffuses into the cerebrospinal fluid, returning H⁺ near normal around the respiratory neurons — so a high PCO₂ has a potent acute but only a weak chronic effect on drive. Guyton makes the matching point for altitude: once the center has lost much of its CO₂/H⁺ sensitivity, low O₂ can drive ventilation far more strongly. Abolishing that hypoxic stimulus with high-flow O₂ can therefore let ventilation fall and PCO₂ rise; Guyton Ch 43 adds that O₂ therapy in hypoventilation provides no benefit for the excess CO₂, and that at a PCO₂ of 120 to 150 mm Hg the excess CO₂ begins to depress respiration in a vicious cycle. The aim is enough O₂ to relieve dangerous hypoxemia, given cautiously at low flow while PCO₂ and mental status are watched. ('Hypoxic drive' is the clinical label; neither First Aid 2025 nor the course chapters use the term.) Immediate O₂ toxicity and fibrosis overstate an acute risk; deliberately leaving saturation low to stimulate erythropoiesis is dangerous; O₂ does not cause bronchoconstriction; and O₂ therapy clearly benefits hypoxemic lung disease (Guyton Ch 43).","boardPrep":"Step 1: In hypoventilation O2 therapy moves about five times as much O2 into the alveoli per breath but provides no benefit for the excess CO2 (Guyton); at very high levels CO2 itself begins to depress respiration. COMLEX: Because O2 corrects the hypoxemia but not the CO2 retention, the blood gas, not pulse oximetry, is what tracks ventilation in a chronic CO2 retainer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 7 — how central and peripheral chemoreceptors drive respiration","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Immediate O₂ toxicity and fibr"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q35_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Oxygen therapy value in different types of hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis has arterial blood gases showing PO₂ 52 mm Hg on room air. After starting oxygen at 4 L/min via nasal cannula (FiO₂ ~36%), her repeat PO₂ is 78 mm Hg. Why does oxygen therapy effectively improve her hypoxemia despite her lung disease?","choices":{"A":"Oxygen directly dissolves the fibrotic tissue, restoring normal lung architecture","B":"Supplemental oxygen stimulates her respiratory muscles to breathe more deeply","C":"Higher oxygen concentration reduces pulmonary vascular resistance, improving perfusion","D":"Raising alveolar PO₂ widens the O₂ gradient across the thickened membranes","E":"Oxygen prevents further fibrosis progression, allowing existing tissue to heal"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Oxygen therapy is highly effective for hypoxemia caused by impaired diffusion (as in pulmonary fibrosis, interstitial lung disease, or pulmonary edema) because it dramatically increases the alveolar-to-capillary PO₂ gradient that drives oxygen diffusion. In pulmonary fibrosis, the alveolar-capillary membrane is thickened by collagen deposition, increasing diffusion distance. On room air (FiO₂ 21%), alveolar PO₂ is ~100 mm Hg, creating a gradient of ~60 mm Hg (alveolar PO₂ 100 - mixed venous PO₂ 40). This limited gradient, combined with increased diffusion distance, results in incomplete oxygenation and hypoxemia (PO₂ 52 mm Hg). When FiO₂ increases to 36%, alveolar PO₂ rises to ~200 mm Hg, creating a gradient of ~160 mm Hg—nearly tripling the driving force for diffusion. With 100% O₂, alveolar PO₂ reaches ~600 mm Hg, gradient ~560 mm Hg. By Fick's law, diffusion rate is directly proportional to the concentration gradient. This increased gradient compensates for the increased diffusion distance, allowing adequate oxygen to cross the thickened membrane and achieve acceptable arterial oxygenation (PO₂ 78 mm Hg). This principle explains why diffusion-impaired patients often require only modest supplemental oxygen to achieve significant improvement. The other options describe mechanisms that don't occur or aren't the primary benefit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In diffusion-impaired hypoxia O2 therapy raises alveolar PO2 several-fold, multiplying the gradient that drives O2 across the thickened membrane. COMLEX: FA — IPF shows progressive dyspnea, dry cough, velcro crackles and clubbing; supplemental O2 treats the hypoxemia while the fibrosis itself progresses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and other restrictive diseases","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q36_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Oxygen therapy value in different types of hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A critical care physician manages three hypoxic ICU patients and must allocate one remaining oxygen concentrator: Patient X (massive pulmonary embolism with V/Q mismatch, PO₂ 50 mm Hg); Patient Y (severe anemia from GI bleeding, hemoglobin 5 g/dL, PO₂ 95 mm Hg); Patient Z (methemoglobinemia from dapsone, PO₂ 88 mm Hg). Which patient would benefit MOST from supplemental oxygen?","choices":{"A":"Patient Y needs oxygen most urgently because anemia causes the most severe hypoxia","B":"Patient Z needs oxygen most because methemoglobinemia is immediately fatal without treatment","C":"Patient X would benefit most because V/Q mismatch responds well to increased inspired oxygen","D":"All three patients benefit equally from supplemental oxygen","E":"None of these patients benefit from supplemental oxygen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Patient X with pulmonary embolism causing V/Q mismatch would benefit most from supplemental oxygen. Large pulmonary emboli create areas of high V/Q (ventilated but not perfused, increasing dead space) and may cause areas of low V/Q in remaining perfused regions (from atelectasis, edema, or redistribution of blood flow). The low V/Q regions have reduced alveolar PO₂, causing blood to pass without adequate oxygenation. Supplemental O₂ increases alveolar PO₂ even in these poorly ventilated regions, improving oxygen uptake and raising arterial PO₂. This should raise his arterial PO₂ and improve tissue oxygen delivery. Patient Y with severe anemia has normal arterial PO₂ (95 mm Hg) and hemoglobin saturation (~97%), meaning the limited hemoglobin present is already fully saturated. Supplemental O₂ won't significantly increase oxygen content because you can't increase saturation beyond ~100%, and dissolved O₂ contributes minimally (~0.3 to ~2 mL/dL, vs 15-20 mL/dL normally carried by hemoglobin). Patient Y needs blood transfusion. Patient Z with methemoglobinemia (oxidized hemoglobin unable to carry oxygen) has moderate PO₂ (88 mm Hg), somewhat responsive to supplemental O₂, but primary treatment is methylene blue to reduce methemoglobin back to normal hemoglobin. While all three have tissue hypoxia, only Patient X's problem is directly addressable with oxygen therapy. The other options don't reflect differential response to O₂ therapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: O2 therapy helps most where the problem is getting O2 into blood (V/Q mismatch, diffusion), and much less where O2 is already normal in the alveoli but transport fails (anemia, abnormal hemoglobin). FA: with dead space (V/Q = ∞, e.g., PE), 100% O2 improves PaO2 as long as dead space is < 100%; methemoglobin cyanosis does not improve with O2. COMLEX: Treat methemoglobinemia with methylene blue and anemia from bleeding with transfusion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — the pathophysiology of pulmonary embolism","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Supplemental O₂ won't signific","D":"While all three have tissue hy","E":"While all three have tissue hy"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q37_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Physiological consequences of systemic hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A mountain climber ascends rapidly to 14,000 feet (atmospheric PO₂ ~95 mm Hg) without acclimatization. Within hours, he develops confusion, difficulty with simple calculations, and poor judgment—symptoms of acute mountain sickness affecting the central nervous system. What property of neurons makes them exceptionally vulnerable to acute hypoxia?","choices":{"A":"Neurons can switch to anaerobic metabolism more efficiently than other cells","B":"The brain has minimal oxygen requirements compared to other organs","C":"Neurons contain large oxygen reserves that deplete slowly during hypoxia","D":"Cerebral blood flow decreases during hypoxia, reducing oxygen delivery","E":"Neurons depend almost exclusively on aerobic metabolism and cannot store oxygen"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Neurons are exceptionally vulnerable to hypoxia because they depend almost exclusively on aerobic metabolism (oxidative phosphorylation) for ATP production and cannot store oxygen. The brain accounts for ~20% of total body oxygen consumption despite being only ~2% of body weight, reflecting the enormous energy demands of neuronal function. Neurons require constant ATP production to maintain transmembrane ion gradients (via Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase), synthesize and release neurotransmitters, support action potential propagation, and maintain synaptic transmission. Unlike skeletal muscle, which can temporarily rely on anaerobic glycolysis (though inefficient at 2 ATP per glucose vs 30-32 from aerobic metabolism), neurons have very limited anaerobic capacity. The brain stores minimal glucose and essentially no oxygen, so it depends on continuous delivery. During acute hypoxia (as at altitude), inadequate oxygen delivery rapidly impairs ATP-dependent processes, causing neuronal dysfunction manifesting as cognitive impairment, confusion, poor judgment, memory problems, and in severe cases, altered consciousness or coma. Chronic hypoxia triggers partial compensation (increased cerebral blood flow, increased hemoglobin), but acute hypoxia overwhelms these mechanisms before they can develop. This explains why hypoxic patients present with neurological symptoms and why the brain is the limiting organ in severe hypoxia. The other options are factually incorrect—neurons cannot switch to anaerobic metabolism efficiently, the brain has high oxygen demands, there are no oxygen reserves, and cerebral blood flow actually increases during hypoxia (attempting compensation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypoxia first depresses mental activity (Guyton), the CNS being among the tissues most dependent on continuous O2 delivery. FA: ascent lowers PIO2 and PaO2, causing hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis; altitude sickness brings headache, nausea, fatigue, lightheadedness and sleep disturbance. COMLEX: FA — renal HCO3− excretion compensates for the alkalosis and can be augmented with acetazolamide.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 6 — high-altitude cerebral edema, its mechanisms and presentation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The other options are factuall","D":"The other options are factuall"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q38_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Physiological consequences of systemic hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"A 65-year-old man with severe COPD has chronic hypoxemia (PO₂ typically 55-60 mm Hg). His family reports progressive personality changes, memory loss, and difficulty with complex tasks over recent months. Brain MRI shows diffuse cortical atrophy without focal lesions. What mechanism explains progressive brain dysfunction from chronic hypoxia?","choices":{"A":"The brain adapts to hypoxia by reducing its metabolic rate, causing reversible dysfunction","B":"Chronic hypoxia increases cerebral oxygen consumption, overwhelming available supply","C":"Sustained low O₂ delivery causes cumulative neuronal injury and cell death","D":"Hypoxia causes excessive ATP production that is toxic to neurons","E":"Chronic hypoxia has no effect on brain function; his symptoms must have another cause"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Chronic hypoxia causes progressive neuronal injury and death through sustained inadequate oxygen delivery despite compensatory mechanisms. While acute compensatory responses include increased cerebral blood flow (via vasodilation), increased hemoglobin concentration (via erythropoietin stimulation), and increased capillary density, these adaptations are often insufficient to fully normalize oxygen delivery in severe chronic hypoxia (PO₂ 55-60 mm Hg represents ~85-88% saturation). Neurons experiencing chronic energy insufficiency develop cumulative damage: impaired maintenance of ion gradients leads to chronic partial depolarization; reduced ATP availability impairs protein synthesis and cellular repair; increased oxidative stress from struggling mitochondria produces reactive oxygen species; accumulation of intracellular calcium triggers apoptotic pathways. Over months to years, this results in progressive neuronal death, particularly in high-demand regions like cerebral cortex and hippocampus, explaining personality changes, memory loss, and executive dysfunction. The cortical atrophy on MRI reflects actual loss of neuronal tissue. This progressive cognitive decline in chronic lung disease is called \"hypoxemic encephalopathy\" and may partially improve with long-term oxygen therapy (LTOT) if initiated early, but established neuronal loss is irreversible. The cognitive impairment is distinct from acute confusion (which reverses with oxygenation) because it represents structural brain damage. The other options don't explain the progressive neurodegeneration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sustained hypoxia depresses mental activity and work capacity (Guyton) and, if severe, causes cell death. FA: chronic hypoxemia raises erythropoietin and hematocrit, and chronic hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction leads to pulmonary hypertension and RVH. COMLEX: Robbins lists oxygen therapy with smoking cessation and bronchodilators among COPD treatments.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 5 — chronic mountain sickness, the model of chronic hypoxic injury","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q39_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Physiological consequences of systemic hypoxia","batch":1,"stem":"An exercise physiologist studies healthy volunteers during maximal exercise. He notes that despite skeletal muscles increasing oxygen consumption 20-fold, subjects maintain adequate muscle function. However, when the same subjects exercise at high altitude, they experience rapid muscle fatigue at much lower workloads. What explains muscle fatigue during hypoxic exercise?","choices":{"A":"High altitude causes direct muscle fiber destruction, reducing contractile capacity","B":"Hypoxia increases the efficiency of aerobic metabolism, causing excessive ATP accumulation","C":"Exercise at altitude increases oxygen delivery above sea-level values","D":"Anaerobic glycolysis in hypoxic exercise yields less ATP and more lactate","E":"Muscle blood flow decreases during hypoxic exercise, reducing substrate delivery"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Muscle fatigue during hypoxic exercise results from forced reliance on anaerobic glycolysis, which is dramatically less efficient than aerobic metabolism. At sea level during exercise, even with 20-fold increased oxygen consumption, adequate oxygen delivery allows muscles to primarily use aerobic metabolism: complete oxidation of glucose via glycolysis, citric acid cycle, and electron transport chain produces ~30-32 ATP molecules per glucose. This high efficiency supports sustained muscle work. At altitude, reduced atmospheric PO₂ limits oxygen delivery despite compensatory increases in ventilation and cardiac output. When oxygen availability becomes insufficient for aerobic demand, muscles shift toward anaerobic glycolysis, which produces only 2 ATP per glucose molecule—a ~15-fold reduction in efficiency. To maintain ATP production, muscles must metabolize glucose ~15× faster, rapidly depleting local glucose and glycogen stores. Additionally, anaerobic glycolysis produces lactate as the end product. Lactate accumulation in muscles and blood causes metabolic acidosis (reducing intracellular pH), which directly impairs contractile protein function and further reduces ATP production from glycolysis (by inhibiting rate-limiting enzymes). The combination of inadequate ATP production and lactate accumulation causes rapid muscle fatigue, reducing exercise capacity at altitude. Acclimatization partially improves this through increased hemoglobin, improved oxygen delivery, and enhanced muscle oxidative capacity. The other options don't explain hypoxic muscle fatigue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reduced work capacity of muscles is a principal effect of hypoxia (Guyton); at altitude lower arterial O2 limits aerobic ATP, so fatigue comes at lower workloads. COMLEX: FA — altitude acclimatization adds ventilation, erythropoietin-driven Hct, 2,3-BPG and mitochondria, gradually restoring exercise capacity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — the acute effects of hypoxia on organ systems","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q40_b1","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Hypoxia vs hypercapnia - coexistence and independence","batch":1,"stem":"A physician evaluates three patients with different pulmonary conditions: Patient L (ARDS with severe shunt) shows PO₂ 50 mm Hg, PCO₂ 35 mm Hg; Patient M (neuromuscular weakness with hypoventilation) shows PO₂ 55 mm Hg, PCO₂ 65 mm Hg; Patient N (isolated diffusion defect from mild interstitial disease) shows PO₂ 68 mm Hg, PCO₂ 38 mm Hg. What explains the presence or absence of hypercapnia in these three patients?","choices":{"A":"All three patients have hypercapnia because hypoxia always causes CO₂ retention","B":"None have hypercapnia because CO₂ diffuses slowly and accumulates despite adequate ventilation","C":"Only Patient L has hypercapnia because shunt prevents CO₂ elimination","D":"All three have the same PCO₂ because pulmonary disease affects O₂ and CO₂ identically","E":"Only Patient M, because hypoventilation impairs CO₂ elimination; the others keep normal PCO₂"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The presence or absence of hypercapnia depends on whether the underlying problem affects ventilation. Patient M with neuromuscular weakness causes hypoventilation—reduced minute ventilation impairs both oxygen intake and CO₂ elimination proportionally. The alveolar gas equation shows that reduced ventilation decreases alveolar PO₂ (causing hypoxia) and increases alveolar PCO₂ (causing hypercapnia). This patient demonstrates the classic coexistence of hypoxia and hypercapnia. Patient L with ARDS has severe shunt (blood bypassing ventilated alveoli). While this causes severe hypoxia (PO₂ 50 mm Hg), it doesn't cause hypercapnia: shunted blood never meets alveolar gas, so CO₂ diffusibility is irrelevant here; instead, hypoxia stimulates increased ventilation (via peripheral chemoreceptors), which further enhances CO₂ elimination, potentially causing hypocapnia (low PCO₂). Patient L's PCO₂ 35 mm Hg actually represents slight hypocapnia. Patient N with diffusion defect develops mild hypoxia (PO₂ 68 mm Hg) because oxygen transfer is impaired. However, CO₂'s much higher diffusibility means it crosses the thickened membrane adequately, maintaining normal PCO₂ (38 mm Hg). Any tendency toward CO₂ retention would immediately stimulate increased ventilation, correcting PCO₂ while being insufficient to correct PO₂. Only hypoventilation and circulatory failure cause both hypoxia and hypercapnia to coexist. The other options misunderstand when hypercapnia occurs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypercapnia joins hypoxia when ventilation itself falls (hypoventilation) or flow fails (circulatory deficiency); in shunt or diffusion problems any rise in CO2 stimulates ventilation, which keeps PCO2 normal or low. COMLEX: FA — shunt and diffusion limitation raise the A-a gradient, while pure hypoventilation keeps it normal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — ARDS pathogenesis, the classic shunt physiology","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"While this causes severe hypox"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q41_b1","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Hypoxia vs hypercapnia - coexistence and independence","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student asks why patients with mild-to-moderate COPD typically have hypoxia without hypercapnia, while patients with severe COPD often have both. The attending physician explains that this relates to disease progression. At what point in COPD progression does hypercapnia typically develop?","choices":{"A":"Hypercapnia appears immediately with any degree of airway obstruction","B":"Hypercapnia appears late, when ventilatory capacity is overwhelmed","C":"Hypercapnia occurs first, before hypoxia, in COPD progression","D":"COPD never causes hypercapnia because CO₂ diffuses too rapidly","E":"Hypercapnia and hypoxia always appear simultaneously in COPD"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hypercapnia in COPD develops late in disease progression when ventilatory capacity is overwhelmed. In early-to-moderate COPD, patients develop hypoxia from V/Q mismatch, impaired diffusion, and increased dead space, but maintain normal or even low PCO₂ because they can compensate by increasing minute ventilation (breathing faster/deeper). Although breathing requires increased work due to airway obstruction, their respiratory muscles can still generate adequate ventilation to eliminate CO₂, which diffuses 20× more readily than O₂. As COPD progresses to severe disease, several factors eventually cause hypercapnia: (1) Respiratory muscle fatigue—chronic increased work of breathing exhausts respiratory muscles, reducing ventilatory capacity; (2) Overwhelming dead space—when most of each breath ventilates dead space, even maximum respiratory effort cannot maintain adequate alveolar ventilation; (3) Severe airway obstruction with extreme flow limitation; (4) Air trapping—dynamic hyperinflation places respiratory muscles at mechanical disadvantage, reducing efficiency. When ventilatory capacity becomes insufficient to eliminate CO₂ despite maximum effort, hypercapnia develops. The appearance of hypercapnia (PCO₂ >45 mm Hg) in COPD indicates advanced disease and worse prognosis. Some patients become chronic CO₂ retainers with compensated respiratory acidosis (elevated bicarbonate). The other options don't accurately describe COPD progression. This understanding guides prognosis and interventions like mechanical ventilation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Because any rise in CO2 drives ventilation that corrects it, hypercapnia appears only when ventilation can no longer keep up — late in COPD. Robbins: the bronchitis-dominant 'blue bloater' shows hypercapnia, hypoxemia and mild cyanosis, while the emphysema-dominant 'pink puffer' has near-normal resting gases. COMLEX: Robbins — cor pulmonale is common in the bronchitic pattern, uncommon until end stage in emphysema.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 3 — the pink puffer and blue bloater phenotypes of COPD","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"In early-to-moderate COPD, pat","C":"In early-to-moderate COPD, pat","E":"In early-to-moderate COPD, pat"}},{"id":"sdl16_ex1_q42_b1","objective":14,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 14 — Hypoxia vs hypercapnia - coexistence and independence","batch":1,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with COPD exacerbation has PCO₂ of 92 mm Hg and appears lethargic but arousable. His respiratory rate is 28 breaths/min. The resident notes this seems contradictory—high CO₂ should stimulate breathing, yet the patient seems sedated. What explains this paradox?","choices":{"A":"The patient is comfortable and relaxed despite the elevated CO₂","B":"High CO₂ always causes increased alertness and anxiety, never sedation","C":"At PCO₂ above ~80 mm Hg, CO₂ directly depresses the CNS despite chemoreceptor drive","D":"The tachypnea (rapid breathing) indicates the patient is fully alert and responding normally","E":"Sedation is impossible with elevated CO₂ because stimulation always dominates"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient demonstrates the biphasic dose-response relationship between PCO₂ and CNS effects. At moderate elevations (PCO₂ 45-75 mm Hg), CO₂ stimulates central and peripheral chemoreceptors, increasing respiratory drive and causing dyspnea, anxiety, and increased respiratory rate. The patient is hyperventilating in an attempt to \"blow off\" CO₂. However, at very high PCO₂ levels (>80 mm Hg), CO₂'s direct depressant effects on the central nervous system begin to dominate. High CO₂ causes CNS acidosis (CO₂ readily crosses the blood-brain barrier and forms carbonic acid), which depresses neuronal function, causing lethargy, confusion, and eventually coma. This patient at PCO₂ 92 mm Hg is in the transition zone where chemoreceptor stimulation still drives tachypnea (respiratory rate 28) but CNS depression causes altered consciousness (lethargy). Further CO₂ increases can trigger a dangerous vicious cycle: CNS depression → reduced respiratory drive → worsening hypoventilation → more CO₂ retention → more CNS depression → respiratory arrest → death. At PCO₂ 120-150 mm Hg, anesthesia and death typically occur. This patient requires urgent intervention (non-invasive ventilation or intubation) to support ventilation and reduce CO₂. Simply stimulating the patient to \"breathe harder\" is ineffective because respiratory muscle fatigue and CNS depression prevent adequate response. The other options don't explain the paradoxical CNS depression with high CO₂.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton — CO2 stimulates breathing only up to a point; at very high levels the excess CO2 begins to depress the CNS and respiration rather than stimulate it, a vicious cycle of more CO2 and less breathing that ends in respiratory death. COMLEX: A drowsy COPD patient with a high PCO2 needs ventilatory support rather than reassurance from a fast respiratory rate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 10 — composite effects of PCO2, pH and PO2 on alveolar ventilation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"This patient at PCO₂ 92 mm Hg ","E":"However, at very high PCO₂ lev"}}],"17":[{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q01_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Etiology, Pathology, Morphology, and Clinical Features of Major Obstructive Pulmonary Diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with a 45 pack-year smoking history presents with progressive dyspnea on exertion and chronic productive cough. Spirometry shows FEV₁/FVC ratio of 0.62. Which pathophysiologic mechanism best explains the airflow limitation in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Increased elastic recoil of alveolar walls causing premature airway closure","B":"Restrictive fibrosis reducing total lung capacity","C":"Dynamic expiratory airway collapse from loss of radial traction","D":"Bronchial smooth muscle contraction causing reversible obstruction","E":"Decreased respiratory drive from chronic hypercapnia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The patient has COPD (FEV₁/FVC < 0.7), which causes airflow obstruction through multiple mechanisms. In emphysema, destruction of alveolar walls leads to loss of elastic recoil and loss of radial traction that normally keeps airways open. During expiration, when intrathoracic pressure exceeds intraluminal airway pressure, unsupported airways collapse prematurely. In chronic bronchitis, mucous gland hyperplasia, inflammation, and fibrosis directly increase airway resistance. These processes cause dynamic airway collapse and prolonged expiration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — small airways are held open by the elastic recoil of the lung parenchyma; loss of elastic tissue in the alveolar walls around respiratory bronchioles reduces radial traction, so they collapse in expiration. COMLEX: FA — tobacco is the most important risk factor; chronic bronchitis and emphysema often coexist, and exacerbations are often triggered by respiratory infections.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — the principle of maximum expiratory flow and dynamic airway compression","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"In emphysema, destruction of a"}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q02_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Etiology, Pathology, Morphology, and Clinical Features of Major Obstructive Pulmonary Diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with chronic bronchitis undergoes bronchial biopsy. Pathologic examination reveals mucous gland hyperplasia. Which measurement would best quantify the severity of this morphologic change?","choices":{"A":"FEV₁/FVC ratio on spirometry","B":"Degree of squamous metaplasia in bronchial epithelium","C":"Number of goblet cells per high-power field","D":"Reid index (gland layer thickness / wall thickness)","E":"Extent of alveolar wall destruction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Reid index is a pathologic measurement used to quantify mucous gland hyperplasia in chronic bronchitis. It is calculated as the ratio of the thickness of the submucosal mucous glands to the thickness of the wall between the epithelium and cartilage. Normally the Reid index is approximately 0.4 and it increases in chronic bronchitis, usually in proportion to disease severity and duration (Robbins Ch 15); First Aid 2025 gives a Reid index > 50% in chronic bronchitis. This objective measurement correlates with the clinical finding of chronic productive cough. Goblet cells are epithelial cells, and their increase in small airways is a separate change from enlargement of the submucosal glands, which is what the Reid index measures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reid index = thickness of the mucous gland layer ÷ thickness of the wall between epithelium and cartilage; Robbins gives about 0.4 as normal, increased in chronic bronchitis in proportion to severity; FA cutoff is > 50%. COMLEX: FA — chronic bronchitis is diagnosed clinically by productive cough for ≥ 3 months in ≥ 2 consecutive years.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — bronchial histology, including the submucosal glands","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Goblet cells are epithelial ce"}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q03_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Etiology, Pathology, Morphology, and Clinical Features of Major Obstructive Pulmonary Diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist is educating medical students about distinguishing obstructive from restrictive lung diseases. Which of the following best differentiates obstructive diseases (COPD, asthma) from restrictive diseases (pulmonary fibrosis, sarcoidosis)?","choices":{"A":"Obstructive diseases show FEV₁/FVC < 0.7; restrictive show ≥ 0.7 with reduced TLC","B":"Obstructive diseases cause hypoxemia without hypercapnia, while restrictive diseases cause both","C":"Obstructive diseases result from alveolar wall thickening, while restrictive diseases result from airway narrowing","D":"Obstructive diseases primarily affect upper lobes, while restrictive diseases affect lower lobes","E":"Obstructive diseases show increased diffusing capacity, while restrictive diseases show normal diffusing capacity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The fundamental spirometric distinction between obstructive and restrictive lung diseases is the FEV₁/FVC ratio. Obstructive diseases (COPD, asthma, bronchiectasis) cause disproportionate reduction in expiratory flow, resulting in FEV₁/FVC < 0.7. In contrast, restrictive diseases (pulmonary fibrosis, sarcoidosis) cause proportional reduction in both FEV₁ and FVC, maintaining FEV₁/FVC ≥ 0.7, but with reduced total lung capacity due to stiff lungs or restricted chest expansion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — an FEV1/FVC below 0.7 generally indicates obstruction; restrictive disease lowers FVC and TLC with a normal (or ↑) ratio. Guyton gives about 80% as the normal ratio. COMLEX: FA — obstructive loops shift left with ↑ RV and TLC; restrictive loops shift right with reduced volumes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 4 — FEV1, FVC and FEV1/FVC with worsening obstruction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q04_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Etiology, Pathology, and Morphology of Four Main Types of Emphysema","batch":1,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with COPD dies of respiratory failure. Autopsy reveals emphysematous destruction primarily affecting the respiratory bronchioles in the central portions of the acini, with relative sparing of distal alveoli. The lesions are most severe in the upper lobes. Which type of emphysema does this represent?","choices":{"A":"Centriacinar emphysema associated with heavy cigarette smoking","B":"Panacinar emphysema associated with α₁-antitrypsin deficiency","C":"Paraseptal emphysema causing spontaneous pneumothorax","D":"Irregular emphysema adjacent to scarring","E":"Bullous emphysema with large airspace cysts"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This patient has centriacinar (centrilobular) emphysema, the most common form constituting more than 95% of clinically significant cases. In centriacinar emphysema, the central or proximal parts of the acini (formed by respiratory bronchioles) are preferentially affected, while distal alveoli are initially spared. This creates a pattern where both emphysematous and normal airspaces coexist within the same acinus and lobule. Centriacinar emphysema occurs predominantly in heavy smokers and characteristically affects the upper lobes, particularly the apical segments.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Centriacinar emphysema — more than 95% of clinically significant cases, in heavy smokers — involves the central acinus (respiratory bronchioles) and spares distal alveoli; lesions are worse in the upper lobes, especially the apical segments. COMLEX: FA — centriacinar = upper lobes and smoking; panacinar = lower lobes and α1-antitrypsin deficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — bronchioles, including the respiratory bronchioles centriacinar disease destroys","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q05_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Etiology, Pathology, and Morphology of Four Main Types of Emphysema","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with α₁-antitrypsin deficiency and a 10 pack-year smoking history develops progressive dyspnea. CT scan shows uniform enlargement of airspaces throughout the acini, from the level of the respiratory bronchioles to the terminal alveoli, predominantly in the lower lung zones. Which pathophysiologic mechanism best explains the distribution pattern of this patient's emphysema?","choices":{"A":"Upper lobe preferential exposure to inhaled tobacco smoke particulates","B":"Gravity-dependent accumulation of neutrophil elastase in lower zones","C":"Genetic antiprotease deficiency throughout the lung, worsened by smoking","D":"Centriacinar pattern progressing to involve distal alveoli in severe disease","E":"Lower lobe mechanical stress from diaphragmatic contraction during breathing"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient has panacinar (panlobular) emphysema, characteristically associated with α₁-antitrypsin deficiency. α₁-Antitrypsin is a serine protease inhibitor (serpin) that protects lung tissue from neutrophil elastase and other proteases. Genetic deficiency creates a protease-antiprotease imbalance throughout the lungs, allowing unopposed proteolytic destruction of alveolar walls. This results in uniform acinar involvement. Panacinar emphysema preferentially affects lower zones and anterior lung margins, with severity typically greatest at the lung bases. Smoking exacerbates the condition by increasing oxidative stress, inflammation, and protease activity while inactivating residual α₁-antitrypsin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Panacinar emphysema is associated with α1-antitrypsin deficiency and exacerbated by smoking — acini uniformly enlarged from respiratory bronchiole to terminal alveoli, worst in the lower zones and bases. FA: AAT is the antiprotease that inhibits neutrophil elastase; its deficiency leaves elastase unopposed. COMLEX: FA — AAT deficiency also causes liver disease, and it is among the causes of cirrhosis that raise hepatocellular carcinoma risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — the pathophysiology of hypoxia and hypercapnia in chronic emphysema","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q06_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Etiology, Pathology, and Morphology of Four Main Types of Emphysema","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old previously healthy man presents to the emergency department with sudden onset right-sided chest pain and dyspnea. Chest X-ray reveals a large right-sided pneumothorax. CT scan performed after chest tube placement shows multiple cyst-like airspaces (0.5-2.5 cm in diameter) in the peripheral lung adjacent to the pleura, particularly in the upper lobes. Which type of emphysema is most likely responsible for this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Centriacinar emphysema from undiagnosed smoking history","B":"Panacinar emphysema from α₁-antitrypsin deficiency","C":"Irregular emphysema associated with previous tuberculosis scarring","D":"Paraseptal emphysema causing spontaneous pneumothorax","E":"Compensatory emphysema from congenital lung hypoplasia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Paraseptal (distal acinar) emphysema is the likely cause of spontaneous pneumothorax in young, otherwise healthy adults. In this type, the proximal acinus is normal but the distal part is predominantly involved. The characteristic finding is multiple enlarged airspaces (0.5 cm to >2 cm) that can form cyst-like structures. The emphysema is most striking adjacent to the pleura, along lobular connective tissue septa, and at lobular margins. It typically occurs in the upper lung regions and adjacent to areas of fibrosis or atelectasis. The subpleural location makes these areas prone to rupture, causing spontaneous pneumothorax.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distal acinar (paraseptal) emphysema spares the proximal acinus and involves the distal part next to the pleura and septa, mostly in the upper half of the lungs; enlarged spaces from under 0.5 to over 2 cm can form cysts and probably underlie many spontaneous pneumothoraces in young adults. COMLEX: FA — pneumothorax is hyperresonant with ↓ breath sounds; tension pneumothorax deviates the trachea away from the lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 2 — spontaneous, secondary and tension pneumothorax","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q07_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and Contrast Clinical Features of COPD (Pink Puffers and Blue Bloaters)","batch":1,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with long-standing COPD presents to clinic. He appears thin and barrel-chested, sits leaning forward, and breathes through pursed lips with prolonged expiration. He reports minimal cough with scant sputum. Pulse oximetry shows 94% on room air. Pulmonary function tests reveal severely reduced diffusing capacity (DLCO 35% predicted) with marked lung hyperinflation. Which additional finding is most consistent with this clinical presentation?","choices":{"A":"Early development of cor pulmonale with peripheral edema and jugular venous distention","B":"Frequent respiratory infections requiring antibiotic therapy","C":"Weight loss raising concern for underlying malignancy","D":"Hypercapnia (PaCO₂ >50 mm Hg) and chronic cyanosis at rest","E":"Copious sputum production (>30 mL daily) and productive cough as the cardinal symptom"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient is a classic \"pink puffer,\" in whom emphysema dominates the clinical picture. Pink puffers characteristically present with severe dyspnea early in disease, barrel chest from hyperinflation, pursed-lip breathing, prolonged expiration, forward-leaning posture, minimal cough, low diffusing capacity from alveolar wall destruction, and relatively well-preserved oxygenation at rest (hence \"pink\"). Weight loss is common and can be severe enough to suggest occult malignancy. The weight loss results from increased work of breathing, chronic inflammation, and hypermetabolism. These patients maintain relatively normal blood gases through increased ventilatory effort until end-stage disease. Frequent infections point the other way: Robbins lists infections as common in chronic bronchitis but only occasional in emphysema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins' 'pink puffer' (emphysema-dominant) — barrel chest, prolonged expiration, hunched forward, pursed lips, slight cough, severe overdistention, low diffusion capacity and relatively normal resting gases; weight loss is common and can be severe enough to suggest occult cancer. COMLEX: Smoking history plus weight loss in COPD warrants evaluation for lung cancer, which shares the same risk factor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 2 — tobacco, industrial hazards and air pollution as lung cancer risk factors","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"These patients maintain relati","E":"Pink puffers characteristicall"}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q08_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and Contrast Clinical Features of COPD (Pink Puffers and Blue Bloaters)","batch":1,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with COPD presents with worsening dyspnea. He has a persistent productive cough with copious sputum that began 15 years ago. Physical examination reveals central cyanosis, elevated jugular venous pressure, and bilateral ankle edema. Arterial blood gas shows pH 7.34, PaCO₂ 58 mm Hg, PaO₂ 52 mm Hg. Echocardiogram demonstrates right ventricular hypertrophy and pulmonary hypertension. Which pathophysiologic mechanism best explains this patient's cor pulmonale?","choices":{"A":"Loss of elastic recoil causing dynamic airway collapse and air trapping","B":"Chronic hypoxemia stimulating pulmonary vasoconstriction and increasing right ventricular afterload","C":"Left ventricular failure causing pulmonary venous congestion and secondary pulmonary hypertension","D":"Destruction of pulmonary capillaries reducing cross-sectional area of pulmonary vascular bed","E":"Increased cardiac output from hypermetabolic state overwhelming pulmonary circulation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient is a \"blue bloater\" (chronic bronchitis dominant) with cor pulmonale. Blue bloaters develop chronic hypoxemia (PaO₂ 52 mm Hg) and hypercapnia early in the disease due to ventilation-perfusion mismatch from mucous plugging, airway inflammation, and hypoventilation. Chronic alveolar hypoxia triggers hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction, increasing pulmonary vascular resistance and right ventricular afterload. Over time, this leads to right ventricular hypertrophy, dilation, and eventual right heart failure (cor pulmonale) with systemic venous congestion (elevated JVP, peripheral edema). The cyanosis (\"blue\" bloater) reflects chronic hypoxemia and increased deoxygenated hemoglobin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins' 'blue bloater' (bronchitis-dominant) — persistent productive cough with hypercapnia, hypoxemia and mild cyanosis; cor pulmonale is common, versus uncommon until end stage in emphysema. FA: chronic hypoxic vasoconstriction → ↑ PVR → pulmonary hypertension and RVH. COMLEX: FA defines cor pulmonale as isolated right heart failure from a pulmonary cause — JVD, peripheral edema and congestive hepatomegaly follow the raised venous pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — pulmonary hypertension, causes and mechanisms","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q09_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and Contrast Clinical Features of COPD (Pink Puffers and Blue Bloaters)","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist is teaching medical students to distinguish emphysema-predominant from chronic bronchitis-predominant COPD. Which statement best characterizes the fundamental difference in elastic recoil between these two phenotypes?","choices":{"A":"Pink puffers have normal elastic recoil with increased airway resistance, while blue bloaters have low elastic recoil","B":"Pink puffers have increased elastic recoil causing restrictive physiology, while blue bloaters have normal elastic recoil","C":"Pink puffers have low elastic recoil from alveolar wall destruction, while blue bloaters have normal elastic recoil","D":"Both phenotypes have equally reduced elastic recoil from shared pathophysiology","E":"Elastic recoil is increased in both phenotypes due to chronic inflammation and fibrosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This fundamental difference in elastic recoil distinguishes the dominant pathophysiology of these two COPD phenotypes. Pink puffers (emphysema-predominant) have low elastic recoil because emphysema destroys alveolar walls and elastic fibers, eliminating the \"spring\" that normally pulls airways open and drives expiration. This loss of elastic recoil causes lung hyperinflation and loss of radial traction, contributing to dynamic airway collapse. Blue bloaters (chronic bronchitis-predominant) have normal elastic recoil because alveolar walls are preserved; their obstruction results from increased airway resistance due to mucous gland hyperplasia, mucous plugging, inflammation, and bronchiolar fibrosis affecting airways, not alveoli.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins Table 15.4 — elastic recoil is low in emphysema and normal in chronic bronchitis; airway resistance is normal or slightly increased in emphysema and increased in bronchitis. FA: emphysema raises compliance and lowers recoil. COMLEX: Recoil loss explains the pink puffer's hyperinflation, while mucus and inflammation explain the blue bloater's airway resistance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — obstructive versus restrictive patterns on the maximum expiratory flow-volume curve","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Pink puffers (emphysema-predom"}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q10_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Etiology, Pathology, Morphology, and Clinical Features of Major Obstructive Pulmonary Diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 54-year-old man with a 40 pack-year smoking history has chronic bronchitis with thick, tenacious sputum. He does not have cystic fibrosis, and genetic testing shows no CFTR mutations. Robbins cites evidence for a smoking-related change in ion transport that makes his secretions worse. Which change is it?","choices":{"A":"Acquired CFTR dysfunction that dehydrates mucus","B":"Inherited loss of dynein arms in airway cilia","C":"Deficiency of α₁-antitrypsin in airway secretions","D":"Th2-driven IgE production against inhaled smoke","E":"Overproduction of surfactant by type II cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Robbins notes substantial evidence that smoking leads to acquired cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) dysfunction, which causes secretion of abnormal, dehydrated mucus that exacerbates chronic bronchitis; this adds to mucus hypersecretion from submucosal gland enlargement and goblet cell increase, chronic inflammation, and smoke-induced impairment of ciliary action. Loss of dynein arms is the inherited defect of primary ciliary dyskinesia, not a smoking effect. α₁-Antitrypsin deficiency is a genetic antiprotease defect that causes panacinar emphysema, not altered mucus hydration. Th2-driven IgE production is the mechanism of atopic asthma. Surfactant from type II cells lowers alveolar surface tension and does not thicken bronchial mucus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — substantial evidence that smoking causes acquired CFTR dysfunction, producing abnormal, dehydrated mucus that worsens chronic bronchitis, on top of mucus hypersecretion and gland hypertrophy. COMLEX: FA — chronic bronchitis shows hypertrophy and hyperplasia of mucus-secreting glands and metaplasia toward squamous epithelium.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — CFTR structure and normal function","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q11_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Etiology, Pathology, Morphology, and Clinical Features of Major Obstructive Pulmonary Diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist examining lung tissue from a patient with COPD notes that the distance from the basement membrane of the bronchial epithelium to the cartilage plate is 3 mm, and the submucosal mucous gland layer measures 1.8 mm in thickness. What is the Reid index for this specimen?","choices":{"A":"0.3, within normal limits for healthy lung tissue","B":"0.6, elevated and consistent with chronic bronchitis","C":"1.8, indicating severe mucous gland hyperplasia","D":"2.0, suggesting end-stage chronic obstructive disease","E":"3.0, representing the absolute thickness measurement"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Reid index is the thickness of the submucosal mucous gland layer divided by the thickness of the wall between the epithelium and the cartilage: 1.8 mm ÷ 3 mm = 0.6. Robbins (Ch 15) gives a normal Reid index of about 0.4, increasing in chronic bronchitis in proportion to severity and duration; First Aid 2025 gives > 50% in chronic bronchitis. A value of 0.6 is therefore elevated by either source and fits chronic bronchitis, which correlates with chronic productive cough. A ratio cannot exceed 1, so 1.8, 2.0 and 3.0 are raw thicknesses or miscalculations, and 0.3 would be normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reid index = gland layer ÷ wall thickness from epithelium to cartilage; Robbins' normal is about 0.4 and FA's cutoff for chronic bronchitis is > 50%, so 0.6 fits bronchitis. COMLEX: Robbins — the bronchitic pattern brings early productive cough, frequent infections, early periodic respiratory insufficiency and common cor pulmonale.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 9 — clinical differentiation of the obstructive lung diseases","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"A ratio cannot exceed 1, so 1.","D":"A ratio cannot exceed 1, so 1.","E":"A ratio cannot exceed 1, so 1."}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q12_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Etiology, Pathology, Morphology, and Clinical Features of Major Obstructive Pulmonary Diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with COPD is evaluated for worsening symptoms. His physician explains that cigarette smoke causes COPD through multiple mechanisms. Which mechanism is the primary pathophysiologic driver of emphysema development in smokers?","choices":{"A":"Smooth muscle hyperplasia leading to irreversible airway narrowing and obstruction","B":"Mucous gland hypertrophy causing excessive secretions and impaired clearance","C":"Squamous metaplasia of bronchial epithelium resulting in dysplastic changes","D":"Recurrent bacterial infections triggering chronic inflammatory airway remodeling","E":"Protease-antiprotease imbalance with toxic injury causing alveolar wall destruction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Emphysema develops through three key mechanisms: (1) toxic injury and inflammation from inhaled smoke damaging respiratory epithelium and releasing inflammatory mediators (leukotriene B4, IL-8, TNF); (2) protease-antiprotease imbalance where inflammatory cells release proteases (elastase) that break down connective tissue while protective antiproteases are relatively deficient or inactivated by oxidants; and (3) oxidative stress from tobacco smoke and inflammatory cells causing tissue damage. These processes lead to irreversible alveolar wall destruction and airspace enlargement that define emphysema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins' emphysema mechanisms — toxic injury and inflammation (LTB4, IL-8, TNF), protease–antiprotease imbalance with relative antiprotease deficiency, and oxidative stress from smoke and inflammatory cells. FA: imbalance with ↑ elastase → loss of elastic fibers → alveolar destruction. COMLEX: Smoking cessation is the one intervention that removes all three drivers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — alveolar changes in emphysema versus pneumonia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q13_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Etiology, Pathology, and Morphology of Four Main Types of Emphysema","batch":1,"stem":"A 70-year-old retired coal miner with a 50 pack-year smoking history undergoes lung biopsy showing emphysematous changes. Microscopic examination reveals that within individual acini, the respiratory bronchioles show marked dilation and destruction, while the distal alveolar ducts and alveolar sacs appear relatively preserved. In which lung regions would these lesions most likely be concentrated?","choices":{"A":"Lower lobes and lung bases with uniform distribution throughout zones","B":"Adjacent to pleural surfaces and areas of previous scarring or fibrosis","C":"Randomly distributed small foci associated with areas of irregular scarring","D":"Anterior lung margins with symmetric bilateral involvement of all lobes","E":"Upper lobes, especially the apical segments, sparing the lower lobes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This description matches centriacinar (centrilobular) emphysema, the most common form (>95% of cases) occurring in heavy smokers. The key feature is selective involvement of the central/proximal acinus (respiratory bronchioles) with relative sparing of distal structures (alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs). This creates a pattern where emphysematous and normal airspaces coexist within the same acinus. Centriacinar emphysema characteristically affects the upper lobes, especially apical segments, more severely than lower lobes. Lesions along the pleura and next to scars are the pattern of distal acinar (paraseptal) emphysema, and small random foci tied to scarring are irregular emphysema; neither matches a lesion centered on the respiratory bronchioles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Centriacinar emphysema involves the proximal acinus (respiratory bronchioles) while distal alveoli are spared, so emphysematous and normal spaces share the same acinus; lesions are worse in the upper lobes, especially the apical segments. COMLEX: FA — coal workers' pneumoconiosis also favors the upper lobes, with small rounded nodular opacities and risk of Caplan syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 4 — the major pneumoconioses, including coal workers' pneumoconiosis","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Lesions along the pleura and n"}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q14_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Etiology, Pathology, and Morphology of Four Main Types of Emphysema","batch":1,"stem":"At autopsy of a 70-year-old man, the pathologist finds small foci of emphysema in which the acini are involved in an irregular pattern, each focus lying immediately beside an old, healed scar. Which statement best describes this type of emphysema?","choices":{"A":"It accounts for more than 95% of significant cases","B":"It is caused by α₁-antitrypsin deficiency","C":"It most often causes pneumothorax in young adults","D":"It follows scarring and is usually insignificant","E":"It is most severe at the bases and anterior margins"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins describes irregular (paracicatricial) emphysema, named because the acinus is irregularly involved, as almost invariably associated with scarring; in most instances it occurs in small foci and is clinically insignificant. Of the four types, only centriacinar and panacinar emphysema cause clinically significant airflow obstruction. More than 95% of clinically significant cases are centriacinar emphysema of heavy smokers. α₁-Antitrypsin deficiency causes panacinar emphysema, which is most severe at the bases and the anterior margins. Distal acinar (paraseptal) emphysema underlies many spontaneous pneumothoraces in young adults.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Irregular (paracicatricial) emphysema involves the acinus irregularly and is almost invariably associated with scarring; it usually occurs in small foci and is clinically insignificant. Of the four types, only centriacinar and panacinar cause clinically significant airflow obstruction. COMLEX: Robbins — apical blebs or bullae in advanced disease can rupture into the pleura.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 4 — other forms of emphysema with overinflation or focal emphysematous change","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q15_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Etiology, Pathology, and Morphology of Four Main Types of Emphysema","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist examines microscopic sections from the lungs of a 66-year-old woman with advanced emphysema. Besides abnormally large alveoli separated by thin septa with focal centriacinar fibrosis, which microscopic finding does Robbins describe in this disease?","choices":{"A":"Large pores of Kohn, leaving septa floating with clubbed ends","B":"Hyaline membranes lining alveolar ducts and alveolar walls","C":"Hemosiderin-laden macrophages filling the alveolar spaces","D":"Polypoid plugs of loose connective tissue in the bronchioles","E":"Asbestos bodies within dense subpleural interstitial fibrosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Robbins describes the microscopic picture of emphysema as abnormally large alveoli separated by thin septa with focal centriacinar fibrosis, loss of attachments between alveoli and the outer wall of small airways, and pores of Kohn so large that septa appear to be floating or protrude blindly into alveolar spaces with a club-shaped end; as alveolar walls are destroyed, the capillary bed area decreases. Hyaline membranes mark the acute phase of diffuse alveolar damage. Hemosiderin-laden macrophages (heart failure cells) mark chronic passive congestion. Polypoid plugs of loose connective tissue in bronchioles and alveolar ducts characterize organizing pneumonia. Asbestos bodies with interstitial fibrosis indicate asbestosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins' emphysema histology — abnormally large alveoli separated by thin septa with focal centriacinar fibrosis, loss of attachments between alveoli and small-airway walls, and pores of Kohn so large that septa seem to float or end in club shapes. COMLEX: Loss of these alveolar attachments removes radial traction, the structural basis of expiratory airway collapse.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — alveolar wall structure, including the pores of Kohn","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q16_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and Contrast Clinical Features of COPD (Pink Puffers and Blue Bloaters)","batch":1,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with long-standing COPD presents for routine follow-up. She reports severe dyspnea that began in her early 60s, limiting her daily activities. Physical examination shows a barrel-shaped chest with hyperresonance to percussion. She sits leaning forward on her hands and breathes through pursed lips. Cough is minimal with scant sputum. Arterial blood gas shows pH 7.42, PaCO₂ 38 mm Hg, PaO₂ 88 mm Hg on room air. Which pulmonary function test abnormality would most likely be present?","choices":{"A":"Normal FEV₁/FVC ratio with isolated reduction in total lung capacity","B":"Normal elastic recoil with isolated increase in airway wall thickness","C":"Increased airway resistance with elevated total lung capacity from restriction","D":"Reduced residual volume indicating inability to fully exhale trapped air","E":"Markedly reduced diffusing capacity (DLCO) due to alveolar wall destruction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient is a classic \"pink puffer\" with emphysema-predominant COPD. The key features include severe early dyspnea, barrel chest (hyperinflation), pursed-lip breathing, forward-leaning posture, minimal cough, and relatively preserved blood gases at rest. Pink puffers characteristically have markedly reduced diffusing capacity because emphysema destroys alveolar walls and pulmonary capillaries, reducing the surface area available for gas exchange. Despite this impairment, they maintain relatively normal blood gases through increased ventilatory effort (hence \"pink\" from adequate oxygenation and \"puffer\" from labored breathing).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Emphysema destroys alveolar walls and capillary membrane, so DLCO falls (FA); Robbins' pink puffer has low diffusion capacity yet relatively normal resting blood gases. COMLEX: The pulmonary OMM lab notes hyperinflation in COPD shortens respiratory muscles and raises resting tone, and accessory muscle use (scalenes) signals primary muscle strain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 11 — the CO method for measuring diffusing capacity","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q17_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and Contrast Clinical Features of COPD (Pink Puffers and Blue Bloaters)","batch":1,"stem":"A 46-year-old man presents with a 4-year history of productive cough with thick sputum, worse in the mornings. He has had three episodes of acute bronchitis requiring antibiotics in the past year. Physical examination reveals central cyanosis and scattered wheezes. Spirometry shows FEV₁/FVC 0.58 with minimal bronchodilator response. Arterial blood gas shows pH 7.32, PaCO₂ 62 mm Hg, PaO₂ 56 mm Hg. What is the most likely age of symptom onset and expected timing of respiratory insufficiency in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Symptom onset age 50-75 years with end-stage respiratory insufficiency only","B":"Symptom onset age 30-40 years with respiratory insufficiency never developing","C":"Symptom onset age 40-45 years with early periodic respiratory insufficiency","D":"Symptom onset age 60-70 years with respiratory insufficiency unpredictable","E":"Symptom onset variable, with respiratory insufficiency based solely on smoking burden"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient is a \"blue bloater\" with chronic bronchitis-predominant COPD. The hallmarks include chronic productive cough (the cardinal symptom, often present for years before dyspnea), frequent respiratory infections, cyanosis, hypercapnia (PaCO₂ 62 mm Hg), and hypoxemia (PaO₂ 56 mm Hg). Blue bloaters typically develop symptoms in their 40s (younger than pink puffers who present at 50-75 years) and experience early, periodic episodes of respiratory insufficiency triggered by infections or other stressors. The hypercapnia and hypoxemia occur relatively early because mucous plugging and V/Q mismatch impair gas exchange before severe ventilatory capacity limitation develops.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins Table 15.4 — bronchitis-dominant disease: age 40–45, mild late dyspnea, early cough with copious sputum, common infections, early periodic respiratory insufficiency, common cor pulmonale; emphysema: age 50–75, severe early dyspnea, end-stage insufficiency. COMLEX: Robbins — death in COPD often comes from pulmonary hypertension and heart failure or acute respiratory failure with exacerbation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 14 — when hypoxia and hypercapnia coexist","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Blue bloaters typically develo"}},{"id":"sdl17_ex1_q18_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and Contrast Clinical Features of COPD (Pink Puffers and Blue Bloaters)","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student reviews chest radiographs from two patients with severe COPD, one with emphysema-predominant disease and one with chronic bronchitis-predominant disease. Which radiographic finding would most reliably distinguish the emphysema patient (pink puffer) from the chronic bronchitis patient (blue bloater)?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral pleural effusions indicating right heart failure and volume overload","B":"Prominent pulmonary vessels and cardiomegaly suggesting pulmonary hypertension","C":"Ground-glass opacities and interstitial markings suggesting active inflammation","D":"Flattened diaphragms and a larger retrosternal airspace from hyperinflation","E":"Kerley B lines and cephalization indicating pulmonary venous congestion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pink puffers (emphysema-predominant) show characteristic chest X-ray findings of hyperinflation: flattened diaphragms, increased anteroposterior diameter (barrel chest), increased retrosternal airspace on lateral view, and attenuated pulmonary vascular markings. The heart size appears normal or even small. These findings reflect air trapping and overdistention from loss of elastic recoil. In contrast, blue bloaters show prominent pulmonary vessels, increased bronchovascular markings from chronic inflammation, and cardiomegaly (right heart enlargement from cor pulmonale).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins Table 15.4 — the emphysema chest radiograph shows hyperinflation with a normal heart size; chronic bronchitis shows prominent vessels and a large heart. FA: emphysema CXR — barrel chest, ↑ AP diameter (best on lateral), flattened diaphragm, ↑ lucency. COMLEX: A flattened diaphragm has poor excursion; the pulmonary OMM lab targets diaphragm release and rib motion in COPD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — the diaphragm and the mechanics of inspiration and expiration","isHighYield":false}],"18":[{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q01_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Other Forms of Emphysema Associated with Lung Overinflation or Focal Emphysematous Changes","batch":1,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with severe COPD presents to the emergency department with sudden-onset right-sided chest pain and dyspnea. Chest X-ray reveals a large right-sided pneumothorax. CT imaging after chest tube placement shows a large subpleural air-filled space (>1 cm diameter) near the right lung apex. Which form of emphysema most likely caused this complication?","choices":{"A":"Bullous emphysema with rupture of large subpleural bleb causing pneumothorax","B":"Centriacinar emphysema with selective respiratory bronchiole involvement","C":"Panacinar emphysema with uniform acinar enlargement throughout lung","D":"Interstitial emphysema with air dissecting through connective tissue planes","E":"Compensatory emphysema with overdistention of remaining functional lung tissue"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bullous emphysema describes the presence of large subpleural blebs or bullae (airspaces >1 cm in diameter) that can occur in any form of emphysema, often located near the lung apex. These thin-walled structures are prone to rupture, particularly during activities that increase intrathoracic pressure or with minimal trauma, leading to pneumothorax. The CT finding of a large subpleural air-filled space near the apex is characteristic of a bulla. Rupture of bullae is a common cause of spontaneous pneumothorax in patients with underlying emphysema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bullous emphysema is a descriptive term for large subpleural blebs or bullae (> 1 cm when distended) that can occur in any form of emphysema, often near the apex; rupture may cause pneumothorax. FA: secondary spontaneous pneumothorax comes from diseased lung such as emphysematous bullae. COMLEX: FA — pneumothorax gives ↓ fremitus, hyperresonance and diminished breath sounds on the affected side.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 2 — spontaneous, secondary and tension pneumothorax","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q02_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Other Forms of Emphysema Associated with Lung Overinflation or Focal Emphysematous Changes","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with severe emphysema develops sudden neck swelling and crackling sensation in the chest wall after a prolonged coughing episode. Physical examination reveals subcutaneous crepitus extending from the neck to the upper chest. Chest X-ray shows air outlining the mediastinal structures and dissecting into subcutaneous tissues. Which pathophysiologic mechanism best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Rupture of subpleural bulla causing direct communication between alveoli and pleural space","B":"Alveolar tear lets air into the connective tissue stroma during a pressure surge","C":"Infection with gas-forming organisms producing subcutaneous emphysema and mediastinal air","D":"Bronchial perforation from foreign body aspiration allowing air escape into surrounding tissues","E":"Pulmonary vascular rupture with air embolism dissecting through tissue planes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient has interstitial emphysema (pneumomediastinum with subcutaneous emphysema). Interstitial emphysema occurs when air enters the connective tissue stroma of the lung, mediastinum, or subcutaneous tissue. In patients with pulmonary emphysema, alveolar tears can occur during transient increases in intraalveolar pressure, such as during vigorous coughing, straining, or positive pressure ventilation. Air escapes from ruptured alveoli into the interstitium and tracks along perivascular and peribronchial connective tissue planes to the mediastinum, then dissects into subcutaneous tissues of the neck and chest wall, producing characteristic crepitus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Interstitial emphysema — air enters the connective tissue stroma of the lung, mediastinum or subcutaneous tissue, usually through alveolar tears in emphysematous lungs during transient rises in intraalveolar pressure such as coughing; less often chest wounds or rib fractures provide the entry. COMLEX: Subcutaneous crepitus over the neck and chest after coughing or trauma is the bedside sign.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — the mechanics of expiration, including the alveolar pressure rises of coughing and straining","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q03_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Other Forms of Emphysema Associated with Lung Overinflation or Focal Emphysematous Changes","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man undergoes left lower lobectomy for lung cancer. Six months postoperatively, chest X-ray shows the remaining left upper lobe hyperlucent and expanded to fill the left hemithorax. Which mechanism best explains these radiographic findings?","choices":{"A":"Progressive development of centriacinar emphysema from undiagnosed smoking history","B":"Panacinar emphysema from occult α₁-antitrypsin deficiency accelerated by surgery","C":"Bullous emphysema with formation of multiple large subpleural blebs postoperatively","D":"Interstitial emphysema from barotrauma during mechanical ventilation in perioperative period","E":"Compensatory emphysema with overdistention of the remaining lobe to fill the hemithorax"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Compensatory hyperinflation (compensatory emphysema) is dilation of alveoli in response to loss of lung substance elsewhere, for example after surgical removal of a lung or lobe (Robbins Ch 15). After lobectomy the remaining lobe overexpands to fill the space; this is overdistention of structurally normal alveoli, not true emphysema with wall destruction. Centriacinar, panacinar and bullous emphysema are destructive processes that develop over years and are not caused by surgery, and interstitial emphysema from barotrauma puts air in the interstitium rather than producing a hyperexpanded lobe months later.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Compensatory hyperinflation is dilation of alveoli in response to loss of lung substance elsewhere, as after removal of a lung or lobe for cancer; Robbins lists it among conditions with lung overinflation that are not true destructive emphysema. COMLEX: Guyton — removing an entire lung halves membrane surface area, yet resting gas exchange is preserved until area falls to about one-third to one-fourth.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 9 — diffusing capacity and its dependence on membrane surface area","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Centriacinar, panacinar and bu"}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q04_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Etiology and Pathogenesis of Asthma, Differentiating Among Various Phenotypes","batch":1,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy presents with episodic wheezing and dyspnea, worse at night and triggered by exposure to cats. He has a history of eczema, and his father has allergic rhinitis. Skin prick testing shows positive wheal-and-flare reactions to cat dander, dust mites, and tree pollens. Serum testing reveals elevated total IgE and positive radioallergosorbent tests (RAST) for specific allergens. Which asthma phenotype best characterizes this patient's condition?","choices":{"A":"Nonatopic asthma triggered by respiratory viral infections without allergen sensitization","B":"Drug-induced asthma related to aspirin sensitivity with associated nasal polyps","C":"Atopic asthma representing IgE-mediated type I hypersensitivity to environmental allergens","D":"Occupational asthma caused by workplace exposure to organic dusts or chemical fumes","E":"Exercise-induced asthma without evidence of allergic sensitization or family history"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient has classic atopic asthma, an IgE-mediated (type I) hypersensitivity reaction. Key features include: (1) childhood onset, (2) environmental allergen triggers (cat dander, dust, pollen), (3) positive family history (father with allergic rhinitis), (4) personal history of other atopic conditions (eczema), (5) positive skin prick tests showing immediate wheal-and-flare reactions, and (6) laboratory evidence of allergen sensitization (elevated total IgE and positive RAST). Atopic asthma typically begins in childhood and represents the most common asthma phenotype.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Atopic asthma, the most common form, is a classic IgE-mediated type I reaction — childhood onset, environmental allergens (dusts, pollens, dander, foods), often with viral cofactors, a positive family history, and an immediate wheal-and-flare skin test; high total IgE or RAST also support it. COMLEX: FA — asthma is associated with atopy (eczema, allergic rhinitis) and triggered by allergens, viral URIs and stress.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 9 — the mast-cell immunologic process of an asthma attack","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q05_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Etiology and Pathogenesis of Asthma, Differentiating Among Various Phenotypes","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with a history of asthma and chronic rhinitis presents to the emergency department with acute bronchospasm and urticaria 30 minutes after taking ibuprofen for a headache. She reports similar reactions in the past after taking aspirin. Physical examination reveals nasal polyps on anterior rhinoscopy. Which pathophysiologic mechanism best explains this patient's asthma exacerbation?","choices":{"A":"IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation triggered by cross-reactive drug allergen epitopes","B":"Direct histamine release from basophils causing immediate bronchoconstriction and urticaria","C":"Viral respiratory infection exacerbating underlying chronic airway inflammation and hyperreactivity","D":"Cyclooxygenase inhibition decreasing prostaglandin E₂ and increasing leukotriene production","E":"Occupational allergen exposure to pharmaceutical compounds causing type I hypersensitivity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient has aspirin-sensitive (aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease) asthma, an uncommon phenotype associated with the classic triad: asthma, chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps, and sensitivity to aspirin/NSAIDs. The mechanism involves cyclooxygenase (COX) pathway inhibition by aspirin and other NSAIDs. COX inhibition causes rapid decrease in prostaglandin E₂ (PGE₂), which normally inhibits 5-lipoxygenase. Loss of PGE₂ inhibition leads to shunting of arachidonic acid metabolism toward the lipoxygenase pathway, causing overproduction of proinflammatory leukotrienes (B₄, C₄, D₄, E₄). These leukotrienes cause prolonged bronchoconstriction, increased vascular permeability, mucus secretion, and urticaria. Robbins classes this as drug-induced asthma caused by cyclooxygenase inhibition, not as an IgE-mediated allergy to the drug.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aspirin-sensitive asthma (uncommon) occurs with recurrent rhinitis and nasal polyps; aspirin and NSAIDs inhibit cyclooxygenase, rapidly lowering prostaglandin E2, which normally restrains leukotriene-generating enzymes — leukotrienes then drive the attack and urticaria. FA: Samter's triad — asthma, nasal polyps, COX-inhibitor sensitivity. COMLEX: FA — leukotriene receptor antagonists are especially good for aspirin-induced asthma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 1 — the drug classes, including leukotriene modifiers, for asthma and COPD","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q06_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Etiology and Pathogenesis of Asthma, Differentiating Among Various Phenotypes","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with asthma experiences frequent exacerbations during winter months, often following upper respiratory infections. He denies environmental allergies, has negative skin prick tests to common allergens, and has normal total serum IgE levels. His family history is negative for asthma or atopy. Which asthma phenotype best describes this patient's condition?","choices":{"A":"Atopic asthma with IgE-mediated hypersensitivity to seasonal allergens","B":"Nonatopic asthma with viral respiratory infections as predominant triggers","C":"Drug-induced asthma from aspirin or NSAID sensitivity","D":"Occupational asthma triggered by workplace chemical exposures","E":"Exercise-induced asthma exacerbated by cold air during winter activities"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient has nonatopic asthma, characterized by: (1) absence of allergen sensitization (negative skin tests, normal IgE), (2) negative family history of asthma/atopy, and (3) viral respiratory infections as primary triggers. Common viral triggers include rhinovirus, parainfluenza virus, and respiratory syncytial virus (RSV). Nonatopic asthma patients experience bronchospasm triggered by respiratory infections, air pollutants (tobacco smoke, sulfur dioxide, ozone, nitrogen dioxide), cold air, and exercise, without evidence of IgE-mediated allergic mechanisms. The winter clustering reflects increased viral respiratory infections during cold months.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nonatopic asthma — no allergen sensitization, usually negative skin tests, less often a positive family history; respiratory viruses (rhinovirus, parainfluenza, RSV) are common triggers, and pollutants, cold or exercise can also provoke attacks. COMLEX: Upper respiratory infections are a leading trigger for exacerbations; the pulmonary OMM lab's URI approach targets the upper thoracic region, ribs and thoracic inlet.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 9 — viral bronchiolitis and its sequelae","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q07_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Fundamental Abnormality of Asthma - Exaggerated Th2 Response to Environmental Antigens","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher studying atopic asthma pathogenesis investigates the role of T helper cells in the disease. Which cytokine profile secreted by Th2 cells is most responsible for the characteristic features of atopic asthma, including IgE production, eosinophil recruitment, and mucus hypersecretion?","choices":{"A":"IL-12 promoting Th1 differentiation and interferon-gamma production for cellular immunity","B":"IL-4 drives IgE, IL-5 activates eosinophils, and IL-13 promotes mucus secretion","C":"IL-17 recruiting neutrophils and IL-22 enhancing epithelial barrier function","D":"TNF-α inducing systemic inflammation and IL-1β activating endothelial adhesion molecules","E":"IL-10 suppressing inflammatory responses and TGF-β promoting regulatory T cell function"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Th2 cells are central to atopic asthma pathogenesis and secrete a characteristic cytokine profile: (1) IL-4 stimulates B cells to undergo class switching to produce IgE antibodies, which bind to mast cell Fc receptors; (2) IL-5 activates and recruits eosinophils, key effector cells in allergic inflammation; and (3) IL-13 stimulates mucus secretion from bronchial submucosal glands and also promotes IgE production by B cells. This cytokine profile creates the type 2 immunity characteristic of allergic diseases. Group 2 innate lymphoid cells (ILC2s) produce similar cytokines and contribute to asthma pathogenesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — Th2 cells secrete IL-4 (stimulates IgE), IL-5 (activates recruited eosinophils) and IL-13 (stimulates submucosal mucus secretion and promotes IgE); group 2 innate lymphoid cells make the same cytokines (type 2 immunity). COMLEX: FA's biologics map onto this list — omalizumab (anti-IgE), mepolizumab/reslizumab/benralizumab (IL-5 axis), dupilumab (IL-4Rα).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 7 — genetic susceptibility and environmental factors in asthma","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q08_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Fundamental Abnormality of Asthma - Exaggerated Th2 Response to Environmental Antigens","batch":1,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl with atopic asthma undergoes allergen inhalation challenge in a research study. Within 15 minutes, she develops acute bronchoconstriction, increased mucus production, and wheezing (early phase reaction). Four hours later, she experiences recurrent bronchospasm with eosinophil and T cell infiltration (late phase reaction). Which mechanism initiates the early phase reaction?","choices":{"A":"Th2 cell activation and cytokine secretion causing delayed eosinophil recruitment","B":"Neutrophil degranulation releasing myeloperoxidase and neutrophil elastase","C":"Mast cell degranulation triggered by IgE cross-linking releasing preformed mediators","D":"Macrophage activation producing tumor necrosis factor and interleukin-1","E":"B cell antibody production causing immune complex deposition in airways"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The early phase reaction (immediate hypersensitivity) in atopic asthma is initiated when inhaled allergen cross-links IgE antibodies bound to Fc receptors on mast cells, triggering mast cell degranulation. Mast cells release preformed mediators (histamine, tryptase, heparin) and rapidly synthesize new mediators (leukotrienes C₄, D₄, E₄; prostaglandin D₂; platelet-activating factor). These mediators cause immediate bronchoconstriction (within minutes), increased mucus production, vasodilation, and increased vascular permeability. The late phase reaction (setting in 2 to 24 hours later, Robbins Ch 6) involves recruitment of eosinophils, neutrophils, and additional T cells through chemokines and cytokines.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Repeat allergen exposure crosslinks IgE on submucosal mast cells, which release granule contents — the early phase of bronchoconstriction, mucus, vasodilation and permeability, partly via vagal reflexes; the late phase is leukocyte recruitment, notably eosinophils. FA: immediate phase in minutes (histamine, tryptase, leukotrienes), late phase in hours. COMLEX: Bronchoconstriction has an inflammatory and a parasympathetic arm — FA notes therapy targets both.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 9 — the mast-cell process that mediates an asthma attack","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q09_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Fundamental Abnormality of Asthma - Exaggerated Th2 Response to Environmental Antigens","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student reviewing asthma pathophysiology learns about various inflammatory mediators involved in bronchospasm. Which mediator is most responsible for the prolonged bronchoconstriction lasting several hours after allergen exposure, and whose role is supported by the clinical efficacy of specific antagonist drugs?","choices":{"A":"Histamine causing immediate but transient bronchoconstriction through H1 receptor activation","B":"Acetylcholine released from parasympathetic nerves stimulating muscarinic receptors","C":"Prostaglandin D₂ producing vasodilation and bronchial smooth muscle contraction","D":"Leukotrienes C₄, D₄, and E₄ causing bronchoconstriction that lasts hours","E":"Platelet-activating factor triggering platelet aggregation and serotonin release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Leukotrienes C₄, D₄, and E₄ (collectively called slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis, SRS-A) are the most important mediators of prolonged bronchoconstriction in asthma. They are synthesized by mast cells, eosinophils, and other inflammatory cells through the 5-lipoxygenase pathway. Leukotrienes cause: (1) prolonged, potent bronchoconstriction lasting several hours, (2) increased vascular permeability and edema, (3) mucus hypersecretion, and (4) inflammatory cell recruitment. The clinical efficacy of leukotriene receptor antagonists (montelukast) and 5-lipoxygenase inhibitors (zileuton) in treating asthma supports their central role in disease pathogenesis. Platelet-activating factor, which aggregates platelets and releases serotonin, is in the group Robbins calls minor contributors because potent antagonists have not proved effective.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leukotrienes C4, D4 and E4 (Guyton's slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis) sustain bronchoconstriction; Robbins notes their central role in aspirin-induced asthma. COMLEX: FA — montelukast and zafirlukast block CysLT1 receptors and zileuton blocks 5-lipoxygenase, the clinical proof of their role.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 1 — pharmacologic classes, including antileukotrienes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q10_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Other Forms of Emphysema Associated with Lung Overinflation or Focal Emphysematous Changes","batch":1,"stem":"A 71-year-old man with advanced COPD undergoes chest CT for evaluation of worsening dyspnea. The scan reveals multiple large air-filled spaces (2-4 cm diameter) in the upper lobes bilaterally, predominantly in subpleural regions. His pulmonologist discusses surgical options for these lesions. Which complication is this patient at highest risk for developing?","choices":{"A":"Pulmonary embolism from hypercoagulability associated with chronic inflammation","B":"Aspergilloma formation within pre-existing cavitary spaces and fungal colonization","C":"Spontaneous pneumothorax from rupture of thin-walled subpleural air spaces","D":"Pulmonary hypertension from compression of pulmonary vasculature by expanding lesions","E":"Bronchopulmonary fistula causing persistent air leak requiring surgical intervention"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The patient has bullous emphysema with large bullae (>1 cm airspaces) in subpleural locations. The primary complication is spontaneous pneumothorax from bulla rupture, which can occur with minimal trauma, coughing, or changes in intrathoracic pressure. Bullae have thin walls and are prone to rupture, particularly those located in subpleural regions near the apex. This is the most common acute complication requiring urgent intervention. Surgical options include bullectomy (removing large bullae that compress adjacent lung) or pleurodesis to prevent recurrent pneumothorax. Pulmonary emboli almost always come from deep leg vein thrombi, not from bullae, and aspergillomas grow in pre-existing cavities, classically old tuberculous ones; Robbins names rupture with pneumothorax as the complication of bullae.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bullae (> 1 cm subpleural spaces) can occur in any form of emphysema, often near the apex, and their rupture may give rise to pneumothorax; Robbins lists bullectomy among treatment options for COPD. COMLEX: FA — tension pneumothorax (air in, not out) deviates the trachea away, kinks venous return and causes obstructive shock with JVD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — alveolar changes in emphysema versus pneumonia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q11_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Other Forms of Emphysema Associated with Lung Overinflation or Focal Emphysematous Changes","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with severe emphysema is intubated for respiratory failure. Shortly after initiating positive pressure ventilation at high tidal volumes, she develops sudden hypotension, jugular venous distention, and subcutaneous crepitus palpable in the suprasternal notch and anterior chest wall. Chest X-ray shows air outlining the heart border and dissecting into soft tissues. Which mechanism caused this complication?","choices":{"A":"Tension pneumothorax from bulla rupture with one-way valve effect trapping air","B":"Pulmonary embolism with acute right heart strain and cardiovascular collapse","C":"Cardiac tamponade from pericardial effusion compressing cardiac chambers","D":"Alveolar overdistention and rupture with air tracking into the mediastinum","E":"Esophageal perforation during intubation allowing gastric air to enter mediastinum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient has developed interstitial emphysema (pneumomediastinum with subcutaneous emphysema) from barotrauma. Positive pressure ventilation with high tidal volumes in emphysematous lungs can cause alveolar overdistention and rupture. Air escapes from ruptured alveoli into the interstitium and tracks along perivascular and peribronchial connective tissue planes to the mediastinum (seen as air outlining the heart on X-ray). Air then dissects into neck and chest wall soft tissues, causing subcutaneous crepitus. The hypotension results from decreased venous return due to increased intrathoracic pressure. A tension pneumothorax could also cause hypotension and jugular distention, but its film would show a collapsed lung with mediastinal shift, not air outlining the heart and soft tissues; pulmonary embolism and tamponade do not put air in the mediastinum, and nothing here suggests esophageal injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Interstitial emphysema arises from alveolar tears when intraalveolar pressure surges, letting air dissect along the stroma into the mediastinum and subcutaneous tissue. FA: mechanical ventilation at high pressures causes barotrauma and can produce pneumothorax. COMLEX: FA — sudden hypotension, tachycardia and JVD in a ventilated patient suggest tension pneumothorax with obstructive shock.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — pulmonary barotrauma and its pathophysiology","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"A tension pneumothorax could a","E":"A tension pneumothorax could a"}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q12_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Other Forms of Emphysema Associated with Lung Overinflation or Focal Emphysematous Changes","batch":1,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has a slow-growing carcinoid tumor that partly obstructs his left lower lobe bronchus. Expiratory chest radiographs show the left lower lobe hyperlucent and enlarged rather than collapsed, and it is beginning to compress the adjacent left upper lobe. Which mechanism best explains the lobe's overexpansion?","choices":{"A":"Air trapped behind a ball-valve partial obstruction","B":"Loss of lung tissue elsewhere filled by expansion","C":"Air entering the interstitium through alveolar tears","D":"Complete obstruction with resorption of distal air","E":"Elastase destruction of alveolar walls around the tumor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Robbins describes obstructive overinflation, in which the lung expands because air is trapped within it, most commonly from subtotal obstruction of an airway by a tumor or foreign object. It occurs either because the obstruction acts as a ball valve, letting air in on inspiration but not out on expiration, or because collateral channels (pores of Kohn and canals of Lambert) bring air in behind the obstruction; it can be life-threatening if the affected portion distends enough to compress adjacent uninvolved lung. Expansion to fill space left by lost lung describes compensatory hyperinflation after lobectomy. Air in the connective tissue stroma through alveolar tears is interstitial emphysema. Complete obstruction with absorption of distal air causes resorption atelectasis, a collapsed lobe. Elastase destruction of alveolar walls is the mechanism of COPD-associated emphysema, not of a lobe distended behind a partial obstruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Obstructive overinflation — the lung expands because air is trapped within it, commonly from subtotal obstruction by a tumor or foreign body acting as a ball valve (air in on inspiration, not out on expiration) or from collateral filling; it can be life-threatening if it compresses adjacent lung. COMLEX: Contrast with complete obstruction, where trapped air is absorbed and the lobe collapses (obstructive atelectasis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 8 — carcinoid tumors, classic endobronchial obstructing masses","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q13_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Etiology and Pathogenesis of Asthma, Differentiating Among Various Phenotypes","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old factory worker presents with episodic wheezing and dyspnea that began 6 months after starting a new job in furniture manufacturing. Symptoms improve significantly on weekends and during vacations but recur within hours of returning to work. Peak flow monitoring shows marked variability correlating with work exposure. Which asthma phenotype best characterizes this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Atopic asthma with IgE-mediated sensitization to wood dust allergens","B":"Nonatopic asthma triggered by viral respiratory infections","C":"Aspirin-sensitive asthma with associated chronic rhinosinusitis","D":"Exercise-induced bronchospasm exacerbated by physical labor","E":"Occupational asthma triggered by wood dust and formaldehyde at work"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient has occupational asthma, defined by the characteristic pattern of symptoms correlating with workplace exposure and improving away from work. Furniture manufacturing involves exposure to multiple respiratory sensitizers including wood dusts (red cedar, oak), formaldehyde (used in adhesives and finishes), and organic chemicals. The 6-month latency period suggests sensitization development. Mechanisms vary: some agents cause type I IgE reactions, others cause direct bronchospasm, and some trigger hypersensitivity of unknown mechanism. The temporal relationship with work exposure is diagnostic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Occupational asthma can be triggered by fumes (epoxy resins, plastics), organic and chemical dusts (wood, cotton, platinum), gases (toluene) or chemicals (formaldehyde, penicillin products); minute quantities trigger attacks after repeated exposure, by type I reactions, direct bronchoconstrictor release or unknown hypersensitivity. COMLEX: Symptoms that improve on weekends and recur at work point to an occupational cause.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 5 — using exposure history to differentiate the pneumoconioses","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q14_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Etiology and Pathogenesis of Asthma, Differentiating Among Various Phenotypes","batch":1,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl has episodic wheezing triggered by dust and cat dander, and her mother has asthma. She cannot stop her daily antihistamine, so skin-prick testing is not possible. Which laboratory test does Robbins describe for demonstrating allergen sensitization in suspected atopic asthma?","choices":{"A":"Sweat chloride concentration test","B":"Serum α₁-antitrypsin concentration","C":"Anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies","D":"Serum RAST for allergen-specific IgE","E":"Sputum culture for Aspergillus species"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins notes that atopic asthma, a type I IgE-mediated hypersensitivity that usually begins in childhood with a positive family history, can be diagnosed by an immediate wheal-and-flare skin test reaction or, alternatively, from high total serum IgE or evidence of allergen sensitization on serum radioallergosorbent tests (RASTs), which detect IgE antibodies specific for individual allergens. Sweat chloride testing diagnoses cystic fibrosis. Serum α₁-antitrypsin is measured for suspected early panacinar emphysema. ANCA testing evaluates vasculitides such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis. Aspergillus in sputum is pursued in allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis, a complication of asthma or cystic fibrosis, not to show sensitization to dust and dander.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — atopic asthma is diagnosed by an immediate wheal-and-flare skin test, or by high total serum IgE or allergen-specific IgE on radioallergosorbent tests (RAST). FA: type I testing — skin test or blood test (ELISA) for allergen-specific IgE. COMLEX: A serum test for allergen-specific IgE is the alternative whenever skin testing cannot be done.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 9 — the IgE–mast cell process in asthma","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"ANCA testing evaluates vasculi"}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q15_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Etiology and Pathogenesis of Asthma, Differentiating Among Various Phenotypes","batch":1,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl with atopic asthma, eczema, and seasonal allergic rhinitis undergoes immunologic evaluation. Laboratory testing reveals markedly elevated serum IgE levels and strongly positive radioallergosorbent tests (RAST) for multiple environmental allergens. Her mother and maternal grandmother both have asthma. Which genetic susceptibility factor most strongly associated with her phenotype?","choices":{"A":"HLA-DR4 alleles linked to autoimmune-mediated bronchial inflammation","B":"Chromosome 7 mutations causing cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator dysfunction","C":"Chromosome 5q polymorphisms near IL-4, IL-5, IL-13 gene cluster and IL-4 receptor","D":"α₁-antitrypsin gene variants on chromosome 14 causing protease-antiprotease imbalance","E":"Chromosome 11 mutations in surfactant protein genes causing alveolar instability"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A susceptibility locus for atopic asthma is located on chromosome 5q near genes encoding IL-3, IL-4, IL-5, IL-9, IL-13, and IL-4 receptor. Among these, IL-13 gene polymorphisms have the strongest and most consistent associations with asthma and allergic disease, while IL-4 receptor variants associate with atopy, elevated IgE, and asthma. These cytokines are central to Th2-mediated allergic inflammation: IL-4 drives IgE production, IL-5 activates eosinophils, and IL-13 promotes mucus secretion and IgE. Other genetic associations include IL-33/ST2 genes and thymic stromal lymphopoietin (TSLP) gene variants.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — a susceptibility locus for asthma lies on chromosome 5q near the cluster encoding IL-3, IL-4, IL-5, IL-9 and IL-13 and the IL-4 receptor. COMLEX: Robbins — atopic asthma typically begins in childhood with a positive family history, consistent with this inherited susceptibility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 7 — genetic susceptibility and environmental factors in asthma","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q16_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Fundamental Abnormality of Asthma - Exaggerated Th2 Response to Environmental Antigens","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher studying asthma pathophysiology examines bronchial biopsies from patients with chronic atopic asthma. Structural changes observed include smooth muscle hypertrophy and hyperplasia, subepithelial fibrosis, increased airway vascularity, and mucous gland enlargement. Which term best describes these collective changes resulting from repeated allergen exposure?","choices":{"A":"Acute bronchospasm reflecting immediate hypersensitivity response to allergen","B":"Bronchiectasis representing permanent airway dilation from chronic infection","C":"Type I hypersensitivity demonstrating IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation","D":"Airway remodeling caused by chronic inflammation and growth factor secretion","E":"Emphysematous destruction showing alveolar wall breakdown and airspace enlargement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Airway remodeling describes structural changes in bronchial walls from repeated allergen exposure and chronic inflammation in asthma. These changes include: (1) smooth muscle hypertrophy and hyperplasia (increased muscle mass), (2) subepithelial fibrosis (sub-basement membrane collagen deposition), (3) increased airway vascularity (angiogenesis), (4) mucous gland enlargement (subepithelial gland hyperplasia), (5) epithelial injury and thickening, and (6) thickened basement membrane. Remodeling results from growth factors (TGF-β, VEGF, PDGF) secreted by inflammatory cells and epithelium. It contributes to progressive, partially irreversible airflow obstruction. Type I hypersensitivity is the immediate IgE and mast cell reaction that triggers each attack; the lasting structural changes it leads to over time are what Robbins calls remodeling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Repeated allergen exposure and immune reactions produce airway remodeling — bronchial smooth-muscle hypertrophy and hyperplasia, epithelial injury, increased airway vascularity, subepithelial mucous gland enlargement and subepithelial fibrosis. COMLEX: FA — inhaled glucocorticoids, which inhibit synthesis of virtually all cytokines, are first-line for chronic asthma control.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 6 — integrating pathophysiology to choose asthma and COPD regimens","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q17_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Fundamental Abnormality of Asthma - Exaggerated Th2 Response to Environmental Antigens","batch":1,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy with atopic asthma undergoes allergen challenge testing. Investigators measure inflammatory cells in bronchoalveolar lavage fluid 8 hours after allergen exposure. Which cell type would be most predominant in the late-phase reaction?","choices":{"A":"Eosinophils recruited by IL-5 and chemokines released during Th2 response","B":"Mast cells undergoing degranulation and releasing preformed histamine granules","C":"Plasma cells producing IgE antibodies specific for environmental allergens","D":"CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes causing direct epithelial cell destruction","E":"Alveolar macrophages phagocytosing inhaled particulate matter and pathogens"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The late-phase reaction (setting in 2 to 24 hours after allergen exposure, Robbins Ch 6) is characterized by recruitment of inflammatory cells, predominantly eosinophils, along with neutrophils and additional T cells. Eosinophils are recruited by: (1) IL-5 secreted by Th2 cells and ILC2s, which activates and attracts eosinophils; (2) eotaxin and other chemokines produced by epithelium and other cells; and (3) leukotriene B₄. Eosinophils release toxic granule proteins (major basic protein, eosinophil cationic protein, eosinophil peroxidase) and galectin-10 (forms Charcot-Leyden crystals), amplifying inflammation and airway damage. Eosinophil-predominant infiltration is a hallmark of atopic asthma. Alveolar macrophages are resident cells that are always present; they are not the leukocytes recruited in the late phase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — the late-phase reaction is dominated by leukocyte recruitment, notably eosinophils (activated by IL-5), with neutrophils and more T cells; eosinophil products such as major basic protein and eosinophil cationic protein damage the epithelium. COMLEX: FA — anti-IL-5 biologics (mepolizumab, reslizumab, benralizumab) target this eosinophilic arm.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — the therapeutic rationale for key asthma drug classes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Alveolar macrophages are resid"}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q18_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Fundamental Abnormality of Asthma - Exaggerated Th2 Response to Environmental Antigens","batch":1,"stem":"A pharmaceutical company develops a new asthma medication that blocks the synthesis of leukotrienes C₄, D₄, and E₄ by inhibiting 5-lipoxygenase. Clinical trials demonstrate efficacy in reducing bronchoconstriction. Which characteristic of these leukotrienes makes them critical therapeutic targets?","choices":{"A":"Rapid metabolism within seconds producing only transient bronchospasm effects","B":"Prolonged bronchoconstriction lasting hours, unlike histamine's brief effect","C":"Selective action on large central airways without affecting small peripheral bronchioles","D":"Production exclusively by mast cells during immediate early-phase reaction only","E":"Primary role in mucus hypersecretion without significant bronchoconstriction effects"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Leukotrienes C₄, D₄ and E₄ (slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis) cause prolonged, potent bronchoconstriction lasting hours, unlike the rapid but transient effect of histamine, and they also increase vascular permeability and mucus secretion. The clinical benefit of leukotriene receptor antagonists (montelukast) and 5-lipoxygenase inhibitors (zileuton) supports their central role. They are made by mast cells, eosinophils and macrophages and act on the late phase as well, they act throughout the airways rather than only on central bronchi, and they are not cleared within seconds.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leukotrienes C4, D4 and E4 cause prolonged bronchoconstriction (Guyton's slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis), and prostaglandin E2 normally restrains their synthesis (Robbins). FA: zileuton inhibits 5-lipoxygenase, lowering conversion of arachidonic acid to leukotrienes. COMLEX: FA flags zileuton as hepatotoxic, unlike the CysLT1 blockers montelukast and zafirlukast.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — adverse effects and safety of asthma and COPD drug classes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"They are made by mast cells, e","C":"They are made by mast cells, e"}}],"19":[{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q01_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Genetic susceptibility and environmental factors in asthma; morphology in status asthmaticus","batch":1,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with severe asthma dies in the emergency department despite aggressive treatment. Autopsy reveals overinflated lungs with complete occlusion of bronchi and bronchioles. Microscopic examination of the airway contents shows shed epithelial cells, numerous eosinophils, and hexagonal bipyramidal crystals. Which protein comprises these characteristic crystals?","choices":{"A":"Major basic protein released from eosinophil primary granules causing epithelial damage","B":"Surfactant protein D forming aggregates in response to inflammatory airway injury","C":"Crystallized mucin glycoproteins from hyperplastic goblet cells and submucosal glands","D":"Galectin-10 (eosinophil-derived protein) forming Charcot-Leyden crystals","E":"Immunoglobulin E antibodies precipitated by high local concentrations in airways"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Charcot-Leyden crystals are a characteristic finding in the sputum and airway contents of patients with atopic asthma, particularly in fatal status asthmaticus. These hexagonal bipyramidal crystals are composed of galectin-10 (also called Charcot-Leyden crystal protein), a protein released from eosinophil granules. Recent research shows these crystals are not merely byproducts but are strong inducers of inflammation and mucus production, contributing to disease pathogenesis. They are commonly seen alongside numerous eosinophils in bronchoalveolar lavage fluid and autopsy specimens from patients dying of acute severe asthma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Status asthmaticus at autopsy — overinflated lungs with small atelectatic areas and bronchi occluded by thick, tenacious mucus plugs containing shed epithelium; sputum or lavage shows Curschmann spirals, many eosinophils and Charcot-Leyden crystals made of the eosinophil protein galectin-10. FA: Charcot-Leyden crystals are eosinophilic, hexagonal, double-pointed crystals from eosinophil breakdown. COMLEX: Eosinophil-rich sputum marks type 2 (Th2) inflammation, the target of anti-IL-5 biologics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 6 — the exaggerated Th2 response at the core of asthma","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q02_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Genetic susceptibility and environmental factors in asthma; morphology in status asthmaticus","batch":1,"stem":"An epidemiologist studying rising asthma prevalence in industrialized nations investigates the \"hygiene hypothesis.\" Which mechanism best explains how reduced early childhood microbial exposure may increase later asthma risk?","choices":{"A":"Nutritional deficiencies from processed foods impairing normal immune system development","B":"Urban pollution exposure overwhelms respiratory defenses causing chronic airway inflammation","C":"Increased allergen production by dust mites thriving in modern climate-controlled buildings","D":"Genetic mutations accumulating in populations with reduced selective pressure from infections","E":"Limited early microbial exposure impairs immune maturation and protective responses"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The hygiene hypothesis proposes that reduced microbial exposure during early childhood prevents proper immune system maturation and shifts immune responses toward allergic (Th2) rather than protective (Th1) patterns. Microbial antigens help \"train\" the developing immune system; without adequate exposure, children may be more susceptible to developing allergic diseases including asthma and atopy. While the exact mechanisms remain unclear, this hypothesis explains why asthma prevalence is higher in industrialized societies and why early-life microbial exposure (farm environments, daycare, older siblings) appears protective. This has prompted trials of probiotics and intentional early allergen exposure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hygiene hypothesis (Robbins) — city life limits early childhood exposure to microbial antigens, which may protect against asthma and atopy; mechanisms are unclear, but it has prompted trials of probiotics and early allergen exposure. Infections are cofactors: sensitized young children with rhinovirus C or RSV lower respiratory infection have a 10- to 30-fold higher risk of persistent or severe asthma. COMLEX: Ask about early viral wheezing illnesses when taking a childhood asthma history.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — the etiology and phenotypes of asthma","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q03_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Genetic susceptibility and environmental factors in asthma; morphology in status asthmaticus","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist examines lung tissue from a patient who died from status asthmaticus. In addition to mucus plugging and eosinophilic inflammation, characteristic spiral-shaped structures are observed in the airway lumens. Which process most likely produces these Curschmann spirals?","choices":{"A":"Helical arrangement of inflammatory cells adhering to damaged bronchial epithelium","B":"Twisted arrangement of shed epithelial cells sloughed during acute airway inflammation","C":"Spiral contraction patterns of hypertrophied bronchial smooth muscle during bronchospasm","D":"Mucus plugs extruded from submucosal gland ducts or bronchioles form spiral casts","E":"Coiled collagen fibers deposited during sub-basement membrane fibrosis and remodeling"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Curschmann spirals are characteristic spiral-shaped mucus casts found in sputum and bronchoalveolar lavage specimens from patients with atopic asthma, particularly during acute exacerbations and status asthmaticus. They are thought to result from extrusion of mucus plugs from subepithelial mucous gland ducts or bronchioles, forming twisted cylindrical structures with a central core. Along with numerous eosinophils and Charcot-Leyden crystals, Curschmann spirals are a diagnostic feature helping identify asthma. Their presence reflects the severe mucus hypersecretion and airway obstruction characteristic of acute severe asthma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Curschmann spirals, seen in sputum or lavage of atopic asthma, may form from extrusion of mucus plugs from subepithelial mucous gland ducts or bronchioles; FA describes them as shed epithelium forming whorled mucus plugs. They accompany eosinophils and Charcot-Leyden crystals. COMLEX: FA — mucus plugging is part of asthma's mechanism alongside hyperresponsive bronchi and reversible bronchoconstriction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 9 — the mast-cell immunologic process of an asthma attack","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q04_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Bronchiectasis — pathogenesis, morphology, clinical features","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old man presents with chronic productive cough, recurrent sinusitis, and infertility. Chest CT shows bilateral bronchial dilation extending to the pleural surfaces. He reports that his symptoms began in childhood. Genetic testing reveals mutations affecting ciliary motor proteins. Which syndrome best explains this constellation of findings?","choices":{"A":"Cystic fibrosis with CFTR gene mutations causing thick viscous secretions","B":"Primary ciliary dyskinesia with possible Kartagener syndrome including situs abnormalities","C":"Immunodeficiency syndrome with recurrent bacterial pneumonias causing airway destruction","D":"Idiopathic bronchiectasis from dysfunctional host immunity to infectious agents","E":"Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis with Th2-mediated hypersensitivity to Aspergillus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient has primary ciliary dyskinesia, an autosomal recessive disease (1 in 10,000-20,000 births) caused by mutations affecting ciliary motor proteins (e.g., dynein). Ciliary dysfunction prevents mucociliary clearance, leading to recurrent infections and bronchiectasis. The triad of chronic sinusitis, bronchiectasis, and male infertility (from sperm dysmotility) is characteristic. Approximately 50% of patients have Kartagener syndrome, which includes situs inversus or partial lateralizing abnormalities because ciliary function is required during embryogenesis for proper organ rotation. The childhood onset, chronic productive cough, sinusitis, infertility, and genetic findings confirm primary ciliary dyskinesia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary ciliary dyskinesia — autosomal recessive, 1 in 10,000–20,000 births, mutations in ciliary motor proteins such as dynein; poor mucociliary clearance leads to bronchiectasis, and about half have Kartagener syndrome (situs inversus with bronchiectasis and sinusitis); males are often infertile from sperm dysmotility. COMLEX: FA — a low nasal nitric oxide is the screening test; women have ↑ ectopic pregnancy risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — tracheal structure and cell types, including ciliated cells","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q05_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Bronchiectasis — pathogenesis, morphology, clinical features","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with chronic bronchiectasis undergoes chest CT. The radiologist notes that the bronchi can be clearly visualized extending almost to the pleural surfaces. In a normal lung, bronchioles typically become invisible beyond what distance from the pleural surface?","choices":{"A":"2-3 cm from the pleural surface on imaging studies","B":"1 cm from the pleural surface except near hilum","C":"5-7 cm from the pleural surface in all lung zones","D":"8-10 cm from the pleural surface in upper lobes","E":"Bronchioles are normally visible to the pleural surface"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A characteristic morphologic feature of bronchiectasis is that the dilated bronchi and bronchioles can be followed almost to the pleural surfaces on imaging or grossly on cut lung sections. In contrast, in normal lungs the bronchioles cannot be visualized beyond a point 2-3 cm from the pleural surface because they become too small to see. This finding, along with airways dilated up to four times normal size and cystic appearance filled with mucopurulent secretions on cut surfaces, is diagnostic of bronchiectasis. The ability to trace airways to the periphery reflects the pathologic dilation from chronic infection and inflammation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In bronchiectasis the dilated bronchi and bronchioles can be followed almost to the pleura, whereas normal bronchioles cannot be seen beyond 2–3 cm from the pleural surface; airways may dilate up to four times normal. COMLEX: FA — bronchiectasis is confirmed by imaging showing airway dilation and bronchial thickening, with an obstructive PFT pattern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — branching morphogenesis and the bronchopulmonary segments","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"In contrast, in normal lungs t"}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q06_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Bronchiectasis — pathogenesis, morphology, clinical features","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old man with post-infectious bronchiectasis presents with worsening symptoms. Sputum culture grows organisms typical of the bronchiectatic microenvironment. Which two bacteria are most commonly isolated from bronchiectasis patients?","choices":{"A":"Streptococcus pneumoniae (80%) and Staphylococcus aureus (40%)","B":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis (60%) and Klebsiella pneumoniae (30%)","C":"Haemophilus influenzae (50%) and Pseudomonas aeruginosa (12-30%)","D":"Escherichia coli (70%) and Enterococcus species (35%)","E":"Legionella pneumophila (45%) and Moraxella catarrhalis (25%)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Haemophilus influenzae is found in approximately 50% and Pseudomonas aeruginosa in 12-30% of sputum cultures from bronchiectasis patients, with four other bacterial types (including nontuberculous mycobacteria) making up most remaining cases. This narrow microbial spectrum suggests that the inflamed, mucoid, sometimes anaerobic bronchiectatic microenvironment supports colonization by relatively few bacterial species. These organisms are well-adapted to persist in damaged airways with impaired clearance. In cystic fibrosis-associated bronchiectasis, Pseudomonas is particularly common and associated with worse outcomes. The figures given for Streptococcus pneumoniae and Staphylococcus aureus, or for Legionella and Moraxella, have no support in Robbins, which names Haemophilus influenzae and Pseudomonas aeruginosa as the two leading isolates.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — H. influenzae is found in about 50% and P. aeruginosa in 12–30% of bronchiectasis sputum cultures, with four other bacterial types (including nontuberculous mycobacteria) making up most of the rest; the inflamed, mucoid, sometimes anaerobic environment supports few species. COMLEX: FA lists Mycobacterium avium among the infectious causes of bronchiectasis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 3 — the key community-acquired organisms, including H. influenzae and P. aeruginosa","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"The figures given for Streptoc"}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q07_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Clinical differentiation of obstructive lung diseases (presentation, diagnostics, pathology)","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old man presents with severe persistent cough and foul-smelling sputum production that is particularly worse in the mornings when he first gets out of bed. He reports occasional hemoptysis. Chest CT shows dilated airways extending to the lung periphery. Which pathophysiologic mechanism best explains why symptoms worsen upon rising in the morning?","choices":{"A":"Circadian variation in bronchial smooth muscle tone causing increased airway resistance after sleep","B":"Change of position drains pus and secretions pooled in dilated airways into the bronchi","C":"Nocturnal accumulation of histamine and leukotrienes triggering early morning bronchoconstriction","D":"Morning cortisol surge paradoxically increasing airway inflammation before medications take effect","E":"Overnight mucus plug formation from goblet cell hypersecretion during supine positioning"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient has bronchiectasis, characterized by permanently dilated airways that accumulate purulent secretions and pus. Paroxysms of cough are particularly frequent when the patient rises in the morning because the change from supine to upright position causes collections of pus and secretions to drain from the dilated peripheral airways into the central bronchi, triggering intense coughing. This positional effect on symptoms is characteristic of bronchiectasis and distinguishes it from other causes of chronic productive cough. The foul-smelling sputum reflects chronic bacterial colonization (H. influenzae, P. aeruginosa) in the dilated, poorly-cleared airways.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bronchiectasis causes severe persistent cough with foul-smelling, sometimes bloody sputum, and occasionally massive hemoptysis; coughing paroxysms are worst on rising in the morning, when the change in position drains pooled pus and secretions into the bronchi. COMLEX: Robbins credits antibiotics combined with physical therapy for nearly doubling life expectancy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 6 — complications of bacterial pneumonia such as abscess and empyema","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q08_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Clinical differentiation of obstructive lung diseases (presentation, diagnostics, pathology)","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student is learning to differentiate obstructive lung diseases. Which clinical finding most reliably distinguishes bronchiectasis from COPD and asthma?","choices":{"A":"Chronic cough with foul-smelling purulent sputum and hemoptysis","B":"Presence of wheezing and dyspnea indicating airflow obstruction","C":"FEV₁/FVC ratio less than 0.7 on spirometry testing","D":"Evidence of chronic inflammation and airway remodeling on biopsy","E":"Progressive worsening of symptoms over months to years"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bronchiectasis is distinguished from other obstructive diseases by severe persistent cough with foul-smelling, purulent sputum (often bloody) reflecting chronic bacterial colonization in dilated airways. The foul odor results from anaerobic bacterial metabolism in poorly-cleared secretions. Hemoptysis (sometimes massive) occurs from inflammation and erosion of bronchial vessels in damaged airway walls. COPD typically produces clear or white sputum unless infected; asthma produces minimal sputum or clear/white mucoid secretions. The combination of chronic purulent, foul-smelling sputum with hemoptysis is highly characteristic of bronchiectasis. An FEV1/FVC below 0.7 marks airflow obstruction shared by COPD and asthma, and slow worsening over months to years is also typical of COPD, so neither separates bronchiectasis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bronchiectasis stands apart from COPD and asthma by daily purulent, foul-smelling sputum, recurrent infections and hemoptysis (Robbins; FA: chronic cough with daily purulent sputum). COMLEX: FA — confirm with imaging showing airway dilation and bronchial thickening, and look for causes such as cystic fibrosis, Kartagener syndrome or ABPA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — the clinical features of the major obstructive lung diseases","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q09_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Clinical differentiation of obstructive lung diseases (presentation, diagnostics, pathology)","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man undergoes pulmonary function testing to evaluate chronic dyspnea. Results show FEV₁ 1.9 L (55% predicted), FVC 2.8 L (68% predicted), FEV₁/FVC 0.68, and TLC 7.2 L (125% predicted). Which obstructive disease pattern do these findings most strongly suggest?","choices":{"A":"Asthma with reversible bronchospasm and normal lung volumes between episodes","B":"Restrictive lung disease with reduced volumes and preserved FEV₁/FVC ratio","C":"Bronchiectasis with mixed obstruction and localized restrictive components","D":"Emphysema with air trapping, hyperinflation, and increased total lung capacity","E":"Chronic bronchitis with increased airway resistance but normal elastic recoil"},"correct":"D","explanation":"These findings show obstruction (FEV₁/FVC 0.68, which is <0.7) with marked hyperinflation (TLC 125% predicted). The increased TLC results from air trapping due to loss of elastic recoil and dynamic airway collapse during expiration in emphysema. Despite reduced absolute lung volumes (low FEV₁ and FVC from obstruction), the TLC is increased because residual volume and functional residual capacity are elevated from inability to fully exhale. This pattern is characteristic of emphysema, particularly in \"pink puffers.\" Chronic bronchitis also causes obstruction but typically shows less hyperinflation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FEV1/FVC < 0.7 with a raised TLC is obstruction with hyperinflation — the emphysema pattern (FA: obstructive disease ↑ RV, FRC and TLC). Robbins: emphysema-dominant patients have low elastic recoil and severe overdistention. COMLEX: Robbins' 'pink puffer' — barrel chest, prolonged expiration, pursed lips, low diffusion capacity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — obstructive versus restrictive patterns on the flow-volume curve","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q10_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Genetic susceptibility and environmental factors in asthma; morphology in status asthmaticus","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with a family history of asthma and atopic dermatitis presents with worsening dyspnea and wheezing. Genetic analysis reveals polymorphisms on chromosome 5q affecting cytokine production. Which cytokine gene cluster is most likely affected by these genetic variants?","choices":{"A":"IL-8, IL-10, and IL-12","B":"IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-α","C":"IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13","D":"IL-17, IL-22, and IL-23","E":"IL-2, IL-7, and IL-15"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Chromosome 5q contains a critical gene cluster encoding IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13, which are central to allergic inflammation in asthma. Polymorphisms in this region increase susceptibility to asthma by promoting Th2 immune responses, IgE production, and eosinophilic inflammation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — an asthma susceptibility locus on chromosome 5q lies near the genes for IL-3, IL-4, IL-5, IL-9 and IL-13 and the IL-4 receptor; IL-4 drives IgE, IL-5 activates eosinophils, IL-13 drives mucus secretion and IgE. COMLEX: FA's IL-4Rα blocker dupilumab and anti-IL-5 agents act on products of this same cluster.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 6 — the exaggerated Th2 response in asthma","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q11_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Genetic susceptibility and environmental factors in asthma; morphology in status asthmaticus","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old construction worker develops progressive dyspnea and wheezing after 5 years of exposure to isocyanates in spray foam insulation. Pulmonary function testing shows reversible airflow obstruction. What mechanism best explains the development of occupational asthma in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Isocyanates act as haptens binding to airway proteins","B":"Particulate matter directly destroys bronchial epithelium","C":"Chronic hypoxemia triggers smooth muscle hypertrophy","D":"Silica deposits activate alveolar macrophage responses","E":"Nitrogen dioxide causes irreversible fibrotic changes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Occupational asthma from isocyanates occurs through haptenization, where the chemical acts as a hapten by binding to airway proteins, creating neoantigens that trigger IgE-mediated allergic responses and airway inflammation. Robbins attributes occupational asthma to type I reactions, direct release of bronchoconstrictors, or other hypersensitivity responses, not to particles destroying the epithelium, and nitrogen dioxide-driven fibrosis would not give the reversible obstruction seen here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — occupational asthma follows repeated exposure to fumes, dusts, gases or chemicals, and minute quantities then trigger attacks; mechanisms include type I reactions, direct release of bronchoconstrictors and hypersensitivity of unknown origin. COMLEX: An exposure history that links symptoms to the workplace is the key to diagnosis and removal from exposure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — asthma phenotypes, including occupational asthma","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q12_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Genetic susceptibility and environmental factors in asthma; morphology in status asthmaticus","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman dies from status asthmaticus despite maximal therapy. Autopsy examination of the lungs reveals severely overinflated parenchyma with airways completely occluded by thick mucus plugs. What underlying mechanism caused the hyperinflation observed at autopsy?","choices":{"A":"Destruction of alveolar walls from chronic inflammation","B":"Air trapping behind mucus plugs and bronchospasm","C":"Pulmonary vascular congestion with edema formation","D":"Fibrotic changes reducing lung tissue compliance","E":"Loss of elastic recoil from proteolytic enzyme activity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The hyperinflation in status asthmaticus results from air trapping due to complete airway obstruction by mucus plugs combined with severe bronchospasm. This creates a ball-valve effect where air enters but cannot exit, progressively overinflating the lungs. Alveolar wall destruction and protease-driven loss of elastic recoil describe emphysema, fibrosis stiffens the lung and shrinks it rather than overinflating it, and vascular congestion with edema is not what Robbins describes at autopsy in acute severe asthma (overinflated lungs with mucus plugs and small areas of atelectasis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: In status asthmaticus the lungs are overinflated, with small areas of atelectasis, because thick mucus plugs and bronchospasm occlude bronchi and bronchioles and trap air. COMLEX: The pulmonary OMM lab lists severe asthma among causes of respiratory failure — persistent accessory muscle use signals muscle fatigue and impending failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 4 — overinflation from airway obstruction, including the ball-valve mechanism","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Alveolar wall destruction and "}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q13_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Bronchiectasis — pathogenesis, morphology, clinical features","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with recurrent pneumonias develops chronic productive cough with copious purulent sputum. CT chest shows permanently dilated bronchi with destruction of smooth muscle and elastic tissue in airway walls. What pathogenic mechanism primarily causes this structural damage?","choices":{"A":"Autoimmune destruction by antineutrophil antibodies","B":"Genetic defects causing defective collagen synthesis","C":"Necrotizing inflammation from persistent infection","D":"Chronic hypoxemia leading to tissue ischemia","E":"Accumulated cigarette smoke toxins damaging fibers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The permanent dilation in bronchiectasis results from necrotizing inflammation caused by persistent or severe infections that destroy the smooth muscle and elastic tissue framework of bronchial walls. This structural damage prevents the airways from maintaining normal caliber. Robbins names obstruction and infection as the major conditions behind bronchiectasis; antineutrophil autoimmunity and defective collagen synthesis are not among its listed causes, and cigarette smoke is the cause of chronic bronchitis and emphysema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bronchiectasis is destruction of smooth muscle and elastic tissue by inflammation from persistent or severe infection, giving permanent dilation of bronchi and bronchioles. FA: an initial infection plus obstruction or impaired clearance → dysregulated host response → permanently dilated airways. COMLEX: FA — most commonly associated with cystic fibrosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — the pathology and pathogenesis of cystic fibrosis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q14_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Bronchiectasis — pathogenesis, morphology, clinical features","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with bronchiectasis undergoes surgical resection of the affected lung segment. Gross pathological examination reveals airways that are dilated and easily visible extending to the pleural surface. What is the normal maximum distance from the pleura at which bronchioles should remain visible?","choices":{"A":"Bronchioles normally invisible beyond 2-3 cm from pleura","B":"Bronchioles normally invisible beyond 7-8 cm from pleura","C":"Bronchioles normally visible at all depths in tissue","D":"Bronchioles normally invisible beyond 1-2 cm from pleura","E":"Bronchioles normally invisible beyond 5-6 cm from pleura"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In bronchiectasis, dilated airways become visible all the way to the pleural surface, which is pathologic. Normally, bronchioles should not be visible beyond 2-3 cm from the pleural surface because they taper to microscopic size in the peripheral lung.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — in bronchiectasis the airways can be followed to the pleura, but normal bronchioles are not visible beyond 2–3 cm from the pleural surface; on cut section the dilated bronchi look cystic and are filled with mucopurulent secretions. COMLEX: Distribution matters — bilateral lower-lobe disease suggests a diffuse cause; localized disease suggests obstruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — bronchioles and their normal size and structure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q15_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Bronchiectasis — pathogenesis, morphology, clinical features","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with cystic fibrosis develops worsening bronchiectasis with frequent exacerbations. Sputum cultures repeatedly grow the same organism known to form biofilms and cause progressive lung damage. Which organism is most likely responsible for this clinical pattern?","choices":{"A":"Klebsiella pneumoniae","B":"Streptococcus pneumoniae","C":"Staphylococcus epidermidis","D":"Moraxella catarrhalis","E":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa accounts for 12-30% of bronchiectasis infections and is particularly common in cystic fibrosis patients. It forms biofilms, causes progressive lung damage, and leads to frequent exacerbations due to its virulence and antimicrobial resistance. Klebsiella, Streptococcus pneumoniae, Staphylococcus epidermidis, and Moraxella are not among the organisms Robbins ties to cystic fibrosis (Staphylococcus aureus, Haemophilus influenzae, and Pseudomonas aeruginosa), and about 80% of patients are colonized by Pseudomonas by age 20.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — the inflamed, mucoid, sometimes anaerobic bronchiectatic airway supports colonization by relatively few microbial species, and P. aeruginosa is one of the main ones. FA: cystic fibrosis is the most common association of bronchiectasis. COMLEX: Recurrent Pseudomonas in CF airways drives repeated exacerbations and progressive lung damage.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — the clinical features of cystic fibrosis","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Klebsiella, Streptococcus pneu","B":"Klebsiella, Streptococcus pneu","C":"Klebsiella, Streptococcus pneu","D":"Klebsiella, Streptococcus pneu"}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q16_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Clinical differentiation of obstructive lung diseases (presentation, diagnostics, pathology)","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman presents with chronic productive cough and reports that her symptoms are significantly worse in the morning, requiring her to hang her head over the bed edge to clear secretions. She produces copious amounts of foul-smelling sputum daily. What finding best explains her postural symptom pattern?","choices":{"A":"Nocturnal bronchospasm from circadian rhythm changes","B":"Gravity-dependent drainage of pooled airway secretions","C":"Supine position worsening ventilation-perfusion mismatch","D":"Morning cortisol surge triggering inflammatory mediators","E":"Overnight accumulation of inspired environmental irritants"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The morning postural drainage symptom is classic for bronchiectasis. Overnight, secretions pool in the dilated, damaged airways due to impaired mucociliary clearance. In the morning, changing position allows gravity to drain these pooled secretions, causing productive cough.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — coughing paroxysms are particularly frequent on rising in the morning, because the change in position drains collections of pus and secretions into the bronchi. COMLEX: The pulmonary OMM lab notes rib raising helps loosen tenacious mucus and improve rib motion in pulmonary disease, alongside physical therapy and antibiotics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 1 — host defenses such as mucociliary clearance that bronchiectasis loses","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q17_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Clinical differentiation of obstructive lung diseases (presentation, diagnostics, pathology)","batch":1,"stem":"Two patients have bronchiectasis on CT. In the first, dilated bronchi involve the lower lobes of both lungs, mainly the vertical air passages. In the second, the dilation is confined to a single segment of the right middle lobe and the rest of both lungs is normal. Which cause is most likely in the second patient?","choices":{"A":"Cystic fibrosis with thick, viscous secretions","B":"Primary ciliary dyskinesia from dynein defects","C":"Severe necrotizing pneumonia of both lungs","D":"An idiopathic defect of host immunity to microbes","E":"Bronchial obstruction by tumor or foreign body"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Robbins explains that bronchiectasis usually affects the lower lobes bilaterally, particularly vertical air passages, but when a tumor, aspiration of a foreign body, or mucus impaction obstructs a bronchus, the bronchiectasis is localized to the obstructed lung segment, because secretions pool and become infected distal to the obstruction. Cystic fibrosis and primary ciliary dyskinesia impair mucociliary clearance throughout the airways and cause diffuse disease. Severe necrotizing pneumonia of both lungs would not confine the damage to one segment. Idiopathic cases, up to 50% of the total, reflect dysfunctional host immunity and are not characteristically limited to one obstructed segment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — bronchiectasis usually affects the lower lobes bilaterally, particularly vertical air passages, and is most severe distally; when a tumor or aspirated foreign body causes it, involvement is localized. COMLEX: Robbins notes aspirated material enters the more vertical right mainstem bronchus more often, so localized right-sided disease raises suspicion of a foreign body.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — atelectasis types, including obstructive atelectasis from a blocked bronchus","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q18_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Clinical differentiation of obstructive lung diseases (presentation, diagnostics, pathology)","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with dyspnea undergoes pulmonary function testing showing total lung capacity of 8.2 L (125% predicted) with FEV₁/FVC ratio of 0.68. High-resolution CT reveals widespread destruction of alveolar walls with enlarged airspaces. What mechanism explains the increased lung capacity?","choices":{"A":"Loss of elastic recoil from alveolar wall destruction","B":"Mucus hypersecretion causing airway distension","C":"Vascular remodeling increasing blood volume","D":"Smooth muscle hypertrophy increasing airway rigidity","E":"Pulmonary fibrosis reducing tissue compliance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The increased TLC (125% predicted) with obstructive pattern (FEV₁/FVC <0.70) and alveolar wall destruction indicates emphysema. Loss of elastic recoil from destroyed alveolar walls allows the lungs to hyperinflate, increasing total lung capacity and causing air trapping. Pulmonary fibrosis is restrictive: it reduces lung volume and compliance, the opposite of the raised TLC here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Emphysema destroys alveolar walls and elastic tissue, so elastic recoil falls (Robbins Table 15.4), compliance rises (FA), and the lungs hyperinflate — TLC and RV rise with FEV1/FVC < 0.7. COMLEX: The pulmonary OMM lab ties COPD hyperinflation to a barrel chest with shortened, high-tone respiratory muscles.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — the four types of emphysema and their morphology","isHighYield":false}],"22":[{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q01_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List and categorize commonly prescribed medications within each pharmacological class used for the treatment of asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with newly diagnosed COPD is started on maintenance therapy with two inhaled medications: one is a long-acting β2-agonist taken twice daily, and the other is a long-acting muscarinic antagonist with once-daily dosing. Which combination is most likely prescribed for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Terbutaline and aclidinium","B":"Albuterol and ipratropium","C":"Salmeterol and montelukast","D":"Theophylline and budesonide","E":"Formoterol and tiotropium"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The combination of formoterol (a long-acting β2-agonist with 12-hour duration requiring twice-daily dosing) and tiotropium (a long-acting antimuscarinic agent with 24-hour duration allowing once-daily dosing) is a standard maintenance regimen for COPD. This combination provides sustained bronchodilation through complementary mechanisms. Terbutaline is a short-acting agonist, and aclidinium, though a long-acting antimuscarinic, lasts about 12 hours and is taken twice daily; albuterol and ipratropium are both short-acting; montelukast is a leukotriene receptor antagonist, not an antimuscarinic; theophylline is a methylxanthine and budesonide an inhaled corticosteroid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LABAs salmeterol and formoterol last about 12 hours (twice daily); tiotropium and umeclidinium last 24 hours (once daily), while aclidinium lasts 12 hours. Katzung: daily tiotropium improves functional capacity and reduces COPD exacerbations. COMLEX: FA — muscarinic antagonists block bronchoconstriction and are used in COPD; tiotropium is the long-acting agent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — current asthma and COPD treatment guidelines","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q02_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List and categorize commonly prescribed medications within each pharmacological class used for the treatment of asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with persistent asthma takes an oral medication daily that blocks leukotriene receptors. She reports this medication also helps control her allergic rhinitis symptoms. Which drug class does this medication belong to?","choices":{"A":"Leukotriene pathway modifiers","B":"Methylxanthine phosphodiesterase inhibitors","C":"Mast cell stabilizers","D":"Long-acting beta-2 adrenergic agonists","E":"Inhaled corticosteroids"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Montelukast is a leukotriene receptor antagonist (LTD4-receptor antagonist) taken orally once daily. It belongs to the leukotriene pathway modifiers class and is particularly useful for patients with concurrent asthma and allergic rhinitis, as it effectively treats both conditions. Methylxanthines such as theophylline inhibit phosphodiesterase, mast cell stabilizers (cromolyn, nedocromil) prevent degranulation, long-acting β2 agonists are inhaled bronchodilators, and inhaled corticosteroids suppress inflammation; none of them blocks the leukotriene receptor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leukotriene pathway modifiers — zafirlukast and montelukast block the LTD4 (CysLT1) receptor, and zileuton inhibits 5-lipoxygenase; all three improve asthma control and reduce exacerbations, though less effectively than even low-dose ICS (Katzung). FA: antileukotrienes are especially good for aspirin-induced and exercise-induced asthma. COMLEX: Katzung — many patients prefer them because they work when taken orally.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — asthma phenotypes, including aspirin-sensitive asthma","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q03_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List and categorize commonly prescribed medications within each pharmacological class used for the treatment of asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with severe asthma uncontrolled by high-dose inhaled corticosteroids and long-acting β2-agonists has elevated peripheral eosinophil counts. His physician prescribes a subcutaneous injection targeting interleukin-5. Which pharmacological class does this medication represent?","choices":{"A":"Short-acting muscarinic antagonists","B":"Phosphodiesterase-4 inhibitors","C":"Leukotriene synthesis inhibitors","D":"Targeted monoclonal antibody therapy","E":"Methylxanthine bronchodilators"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Anti-IL-5 monoclonal antibodies (mepolizumab, reslizumab, benralizumab) represent targeted biologic therapy for severe asthma with eosinophilia that remains uncontrolled despite ICS/LABA treatment. These biologics reduce eosinophil numbers and activity in the airway mucosa. Short-acting muscarinic antagonists such as ipratropium are inhaled bronchodilators, roflumilast is an oral PDE4 inhibitor, leukotriene synthesis inhibitors such as zileuton block 5-lipoxygenase, and theophylline is an oral methylxanthine; none is an injected antibody against IL-5.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Monoclonal antibodies for asthma (Katzung Table 20–1) — omalizumab (IgE), mepolizumab and reslizumab (IL-5), benralizumab (IL-5 receptor), dupilumab (IL-4 receptor), tezepelumab (TSLP); anti-IL-5 agents treat severe eosinophilic asthma. FA: IL-5 blockade prevents eosinophil differentiation, maturation, activation and survival. COMLEX: Peripheral eosinophilia in uncontrolled asthma on ICS/LABA identifies the patient likely to respond.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 7 — environmental factors and the eosinophil-rich morphology of status asthmaticus","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Short-acting muscarinic antago"}},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q04_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacological strategies for asthma and COPD management, focusing on the key therapeutic goals","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with moderate persistent asthma experiences daily symptoms and nocturnal awakenings more than once a week. Her physician recommends daily controller therapy to prevent bronchial inflammation and hyperreactivity. What is the primary therapeutic goal of initiating inhaled corticosteroid therapy in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Provide immediate bronchodilation during acute symptoms","B":"Block acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors","C":"Antagonize leukotriene receptors in smooth muscle","D":"Inhibit phosphodiesterase to increase cyclic AMP","E":"Reduce airway inflammation and prevent exacerbations"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The primary therapeutic goal of inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) therapy is to reduce chronic airway inflammation by inhibiting inflammatory cytokine production. This anti-inflammatory action prevents exacerbations, reduces bronchial hyperreactivity, and maintains long-term asthma control. ICS therapy is the most effective controller medication for asthma. Blocking acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors is the action of antimuscarinics such as ipratropium and tiotropium, and leukotriene receptor antagonism is the action of montelukast and zafirlukast, not of corticosteroids.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Corticosteroids do not relax airway smooth muscle directly; their most important action is inhibiting infiltration of the airways by lymphocytes, eosinophils and mast cells, and regular use reduces bronchial reactivity and exacerbations (Katzung). FA: ICS inhibit synthesis of virtually all cytokines by inactivating NF-κB and are first-line for chronic asthma. COMLEX: Katzung calls ICS the core 'long-term controllers' of asthma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 6 — the Th2 inflammation that controller therapy suppresses","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q05_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacological strategies for asthma and COPD management, focusing on the key therapeutic goals","batch":1,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with COPD experiences progressive dyspnea and demonstrates irreversible airflow obstruction on spirometry. His physician prescribes tiotropium to improve his functional capacity. What is the primary mechanism by which long-acting antimuscarinic agents achieve therapeutic benefit in COPD?","choices":{"A":"Stimulating adenylyl cyclase to increase cAMP production","B":"Inhibiting inflammatory cytokine gene transcription","C":"Blocking leukotriene-mediated bronchoconstriction","D":"Reducing parasympathetic-mediated airway tone","E":"Preventing mast cell degranulation and mediator release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Long-acting antimuscarinic agents like tiotropium competitively block acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors, thereby reducing parasympathetic (vagal) tone in the airways. This results in sustained bronchodilation and reduced mucus secretion. In COPD, anticholinergics are first-line maintenance therapy and have been shown to reduce exacerbation frequency. Raising cAMP through adenylyl cyclase is the β-agonist mechanism, blocking cytokine gene transcription is the corticosteroid mechanism, and preventing mast cell degranulation is the action of cromolyn and nedocromil.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Muscarinic antagonists block acetylcholine from vagal motor nerves, preventing airway smooth-muscle contraction and the mucus secretion vagal activity drives (Katzung). Guyton: vagal fibers cause mild to moderate bronchiolar constriction, worsened when disease has already narrowed the airways. COMLEX: FA — tiotropium (long-acting) and ipratropium are used in COPD; Katzung notes ipratropium is about as effective as albuterol in COPD with partly reversible obstruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 13 — sympathetic versus parasympathetic effects on bronchiole diameter","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q06_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacological strategies for asthma and COPD management, focusing on the key therapeutic goals","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman with asthma uses albuterol as needed for symptom relief but requires it more than twice weekly. Her physician adds a daily inhaled corticosteroid to achieve better disease control. What fundamental distinction exists between the therapeutic strategies of short-term relievers and long-term controllers in asthma management?","choices":{"A":"Relievers inhibit phosphodiesterase; controllers stimulate adenylyl cyclase","B":"Relievers block leukotriene receptors; controllers inhibit mast cells","C":"Relievers prevent exacerbations; controllers treat acute symptoms","D":"Relievers provide bronchodilation; controllers reduce inflammation","E":"Relievers reduce eosinophils; controllers antagonize muscarinic receptors"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Short-term relievers (SABAs like albuterol) provide rapid bronchodilation for acute symptom relief by stimulating β2-adrenergic receptors. Long-term controllers (ICS) reduce chronic airway inflammation, prevent exacerbations, and decrease bronchial hyperreactivity. This fundamental distinction guides the stepwise approach to asthma management.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Katzung divides asthma therapy into 'short-term relievers' for acute bronchoconstriction (inhaled β2 agonists such as albuterol) and 'long-term controllers' that reduce symptoms and prevent attacks (especially inhaled corticosteroids). FA: albuterol is for acute symptoms; ICS are first-line for chronic asthma. COMLEX: Needing a reliever more often is the cue to add or step up a controller.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — current pharmacologic treatment guidelines for asthma and COPD","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q07_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the key adverse effects and safety considerations associated with the major pharmacological classes used to treat asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman using high-dose inhaled corticosteroids for asthma control reports persistent hoarseness and white patches in her mouth. Physical examination reveals oropharyngeal candidiasis. What adverse effect profile is most characteristic of inhaled corticosteroid therapy?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle tremor and tachycardia","B":"Hepatotoxicity and elevated liver enzymes","C":"Nausea, vomiting, and cardiac arrhythmias","D":"Oropharyngeal candidiasis and dysphonia","E":"Urinary retention and cognitive impairment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The most common local adverse effects of inhaled corticosteroids are oropharyngeal candidiasis (thrush) and dysphonia (hoarseness). These effects result from local drug deposition in the oropharynx. Risk can be reduced by using a spacer device and gargling/expectorating after each use. The candidiasis is easily treated with topical clotrimazole. Tremor and tachycardia belong to β2 agonists, liver enzyme elevation to zileuton, nausea and arrhythmias to theophylline, and urinary retention with cognitive concerns to antimuscarinics.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A special problem of inhaled corticosteroids is oropharyngeal candidiasis and dysphonia; gargling water after use and using a spacer reduce the risk, and candidiasis responds to topical clotrimazole (Katzung). FA: use a spacer or rinse the mouth to prevent thrush. COMLEX: Katzung — chronic ICS use may also raise osteoporosis and cataract risk and slows children's growth by about 1 cm in the first year.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — the therapeutic rationale for each pharmacologic class","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q08_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the key adverse effects and safety considerations associated with the major pharmacological classes used to treat asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with COPD is prescribed theophylline for additional bronchodilation. His physician orders serum drug level monitoring due to the medication's narrow therapeutic index. Which adverse effect profile is most concerning with theophylline therapy?","choices":{"A":"Oropharyngeal candidiasis and adrenal suppression","B":"Dry mouth and urinary retention","C":"Neuropsychiatric effects and hepatotoxicity","D":"Skeletal muscle tremor and hypokalemia","E":"Nausea, tremor, arrhythmias, and seizures"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Theophylline has a narrow therapeutic index and requires serum level monitoring. Its dose-dependent adverse effects include nausea, vomiting, tremor, cardiac arrhythmias, and at toxic levels, seizures. These central nervous system and cardiac toxicities, combined with numerous drug-drug interactions affecting metabolism, have made theophylline largely obsolete in asthma treatment. Oropharyngeal candidiasis and adrenal suppression are inhaled corticosteroid effects, and dry mouth with urinary retention are antimuscarinic effects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Theophylline is a phosphodiesterase inhibitor that raises cAMP (and blocks adenosine); its use has declined because of toxicities — nausea, vomiting, tremulousness, arrhythmias — and the need for serum level monitoring given a narrow therapeutic index and variable metabolism (Katzung). FA: narrow therapeutic index with cardiotoxicity and neurotoxicity; metabolized by cytochrome P-450. COMLEX: Watch drug interactions — Katzung notes frequent drug–drug interactions make monitoring more necessary.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — the therapeutic rationale and place of each drug class","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q09_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the key adverse effects and safety considerations associated with the major pharmacological classes used to treat asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman with asthma is prescribed a long-acting β2-agonist in combination with an inhaled corticosteroid. Her physician emphasizes that the LABA should never be used as monotherapy for asthma. What is the primary safety concern that necessitates concurrent inhaled corticosteroid therapy with LABA use?","choices":{"A":"LABAs cause adrenal suppression requiring steroid coverage","B":"LABAs provide bronchodilation but lack anti-inflammatory action","C":"LABAs reduce bronchial hyperreactivity only with steroids","D":"LABAs increase risk of cardiac arrhythmias without protection","E":"LABAs cause oropharyngeal candidiasis prevented by steroids"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Long-acting β2-agonists (LABAs) provide sustained bronchodilation but have no anti-inflammatory action. When used as monotherapy, they can mask ongoing airway inflammation while patients experience prolonged symptom relief, potentially leading to severe exacerbations. LABAs should always be combined with inhaled corticosteroids for asthma treatment to address both bronchoconstriction and inflammation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LABAs have no anti-inflammatory action, so they should not be used as monotherapy for asthma; their prolonged bronchodilation masks symptoms of bronchial inflammation, so they are given only with an ICS in asthma — though they may be used alone in COPD (Katzung). COMLEX: Katzung notes LABAs also appear to interact with inhaled corticosteroids to improve asthma control.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — guideline differences between asthma and COPD","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q10_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List and categorize commonly prescribed medications within each pharmacological class used for the treatment of asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old man presents to the emergency department with an acute COPD exacerbation. He receives nebulized treatments combining a short-acting β2-agonist with a short-acting muscarinic antagonist for rapid bronchodilation. Which medication combination is most likely administered?","choices":{"A":"Formoterol and aclidinium","B":"Salmeterol and tiotropium","C":"Albuterol and ipratropium","D":"Terbutaline and montelukast","E":"Theophylline and budesonide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The combination of albuterol (a short-acting β2-agonist) and ipratropium (a short-acting antimuscarinic agent) is the standard nebulized treatment for acute COPD exacerbations. This combination provides rapid bronchodilation through complementary mechanisms: β2-receptor stimulation and muscarinic receptor blockade. Formoterol, salmeterol, aclidinium, and tiotropium are long-acting maintenance drugs, montelukast is a leukotriene antagonist used for prophylaxis, and theophylline and budesonide are a methylxanthine and an inhaled corticosteroid, so none of those pairs is a short-acting β2 agonist plus a short-acting antimuscarinic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Albuterol is a short-acting β2 agonist (relaxes airway smooth muscle via cAMP); ipratropium is an inhaled quaternary atropine analog, poorly absorbed and so relatively free of systemic atropine-like effects (Katzung). FA: bronchoconstriction has an inflammatory and a parasympathetic arm, so the two bronchodilators act on separate pathways. COMLEX: Guyton — airway irritation from dust, smoke or infection triggers vagal constrictor reflexes, which antimuscarinics block.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 13 — sympathetic versus parasympathetic control of bronchioles","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q11_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List and categorize commonly prescribed medications within each pharmacological class used for the treatment of asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman with persistent asthma is prescribed a daily inhaled medication containing both fluticasone and salmeterol in a single device. Which two pharmacological classes are combined in this formulation?","choices":{"A":"Short-acting β2-agonist and leukotriene modifier","B":"Antimuscarinic agent and methylxanthine","C":"Inhaled corticosteroid and long-acting β2-agonist","D":"Mast cell stabilizer and phosphodiesterase inhibitor","E":"Leukotriene modifier and monoclonal antibody"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fluticasone/salmeterol combines an inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) with a long-acting β2-agonist (LABA) in a single inhaler. These combination products (also include budesonide/formoterol, mometasone/formoterol) are widely used for asthma maintenance therapy, providing both anti-inflammatory control and sustained bronchodilation. Fluticasone is not an antimuscarinic, mast cell stabilizer, or leukotriene modifier, and salmeterol is not a methylxanthine, phosphodiesterase inhibitor, or monoclonal antibody.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fluticasone/salmeterol pairs an ICS (anti-inflammatory controller) with a 12-hour LABA (bronchodilator); because LABAs lack anti-inflammatory action, asthma guidance uses them only with an ICS (Katzung). FA: ICS inhibit cytokine synthesis via NF-κB; salmeterol and formoterol relax bronchial smooth muscle. COMLEX: Rinse the mouth or use a spacer after the ICS component to prevent thrush (FA).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 6 — using pathophysiology to choose and justify regimens","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q12_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List and categorize commonly prescribed medications within each pharmacological class used for the treatment of asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with severe COPD inadequately controlled on bronchodilators is prescribed an oral medication that selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase-4 to reduce inflammation and exacerbation frequency. Which drug class does this medication represent?","choices":{"A":"Methylxanthine phosphodiesterase inhibitors","B":"Selective PDE4 inhibitors","C":"Leukotriene synthesis inhibitors","D":"Long-acting muscarinic antagonists","E":"Targeted monoclonal antibodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Roflumilast is a selective phosphodiesterase-4 (PDE4) inhibitor approved for severe COPD with chronic bronchitis and history of exacerbations. Unlike theophylline (a non-selective PDE inhibitor), selective PDE4 inhibition reduces inflammatory cell activity and exacerbation frequency with fewer cardiac and CNS effects. Long-acting muscarinic antagonists are inhaled bronchodilators that block acetylcholine, and monoclonal antibodies are injected agents aimed at IgE, IL-5, or related targets; neither inhibits PDE4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Roflumilast, a selective PDE4 inhibitor, improves pulmonary function and reduces exacerbation frequency and is FDA-approved for COPD; other PDE4 inhibitors were abandoned for nausea, headache and diarrhea (Katzung). FA: roflumilast raises cAMP for bronchodilation and less airway inflammation — used in COPD only. COMLEX: It suits the bronchitis-dominant, exacerbation-prone patient (Robbins' 'blue bloater' pattern).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 3 — the pink puffer and blue bloater phenotypes of COPD","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Unlike theophylline (a non-sel"}},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q13_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacological strategies for asthma and COPD management, focusing on the key therapeutic goals","batch":1,"stem":"A 25-year-old man with asthma uses albuterol only before exercise to prevent exercise-induced bronchoconstriction. He experiences asthma symptoms less than twice weekly and has no nocturnal awakenings. What is the primary therapeutic goal of this as-needed β2-agonist strategy?","choices":{"A":"Provide rapid bronchodilation for symptom relief","B":"Inhibit leukotriene-mediated inflammatory responses","C":"Suppress eosinophil recruitment to airway mucosa","D":"Reduce chronic airway inflammation and prevent remodeling","E":"Block vagally-mediated bronchoconstriction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"For mild intermittent asthma with infrequent symptoms, as-needed short-acting β2-agonists (SABAs) provide rapid bronchodilation for acute symptom relief or pre-exercise prophylaxis. This patient does not require daily controller therapy. The therapeutic goal is immediate relief of bronchoconstriction, not chronic inflammation control. Suppressing eosinophil recruitment is an anti-inflammatory goal served by corticosteroids or anti-IL-5 antibodies; β2 agonists have no anti-inflammatory action.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A short-acting β2 agonist is a 'short-term reliever' — rapid bronchodilation for acute symptoms (FA: albuterol is for acute symptoms); Katzung notes leukotriene modifiers also block airway responses to exercise. COMLEX: Robbins — exercise and cold can trigger attacks, especially in nonatopic asthma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — asthma phenotypes and triggers such as exercise","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Suppressing eosinophil recruit","D":"The therapeutic goal is immedi"}},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q14_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacological strategies for asthma and COPD management, focusing on the key therapeutic goals","batch":1,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with moderate persistent asthma is started on combination fluticasone/salmeterol therapy. Her physician explains that combining these medications allows better asthma control than using either agent alone at higher doses. What pharmacological interaction explains this synergistic effect?","choices":{"A":"LABAs enhance histone deacetylase activity of corticosteroids","B":"LABAs directly inhibit eosinophil infiltration of the airways","C":"Corticosteroids potentiate β2 agonists and suppress inflammation","D":"Corticosteroids directly relax airway smooth muscle within minutes","E":"LABAs inhibit inflammatory cytokine transcription"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Corticosteroids do not relax airway smooth muscle directly; they reduce airway obstruction mainly by inhibiting the infiltration of the airways by lymphocytes, eosinophils and mast cells, and in part by potentiating the effects of β-receptor agonists (Katzung Ch 20). The LABA supplies sustained bronchodilation, which the corticosteroid lacks, and salmeterol is described as potentiating corticosteroid effects; this complementary action is why adding a LABA is more effective than doubling the ICS dose. LABAs do not block eosinophil infiltration or cytokine transcription — those are corticosteroid actions — and enhancement of histone deacetylase is not the explanation offered for the combination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Katzung — corticosteroids reduce airway obstruction mainly by inhibiting infiltration by lymphocytes, eosinophils and mast cells, and in part by contracting engorged mucosal vessels and potentiating β-agonist effects; salmeterol in turn potentiates corticosteroid effects. COMLEX: The combination lets a lower ICS dose achieve control, limiting thrush, dysphonia and the bone and eye risks of high doses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — the rationale behind each pharmacologic class","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"LABAs do not block eosinophil "}},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q15_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacological strategies for asthma and COPD management, focusing on the key therapeutic goals","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with asthma demonstrates poor adherence to inhaled corticosteroid therapy, resulting in frequent exacerbations requiring emergency department visits and oral prednisone courses. What fundamental principle of asthma management is violated by discontinuing controller therapy during symptom-free periods?","choices":{"A":"Oral steroids are more effective than inhaled steroids","B":"Relievers provide superior long-term disease control","C":"Controller therapy must be taken continuously to work","D":"Bronchodilators alone prevent airway remodeling","E":"Exacerbations only occur during viral infections"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A fundamental principle of asthma management is that controller medications (especially ICS) must be used continuously, even during symptom-free periods, to maintain anti-inflammatory effects and prevent exacerbations. Discontinuing controller therapy allows airway inflammation to worsen, increasing exacerbation risk. This is distinct from as-needed reliever use.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inhaled corticosteroids reduce bronchial reactivity and the frequency of exacerbations only if taken regularly (Katzung); they are FA's first-line therapy for chronic asthma. COMLEX: Katzung notes many of the roughly 3500 annual US asthma deaths are considered preventable — adherence to controller therapy is central.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 7 — environmental factors and the morphology of fatal status asthmaticus","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q16_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the key adverse effects and safety considerations associated with the major pharmacological classes used to treat asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman with acute asthma exacerbation receives multiple nebulized albuterol treatments in the emergency department. She develops fine tremor in her hands and complains of palpitations. Her ECG shows sinus tachycardia at 110 bpm. Which adverse effect profile is characteristic of high-dose β2-agonist therapy?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle tremor and tachycardia","B":"Oropharyngeal candidiasis and dysphonia","C":"Nausea, vomiting, and cardiac arrhythmias","D":"Dry mouth and urinary retention","E":"Neuropsychiatric effects and hepatotoxicity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"High-dose or frequent use of β2-agonists causes dose-dependent adverse effects including skeletal muscle tremor (most common), tachycardia, palpitations, and hypokalemia. These effects result from β2-receptor stimulation in non-airway tissues. While β2-selective agents minimize cardiac effects compared to non-selective agents, they still occur at higher doses. Oropharyngeal candidiasis and dysphonia come from inhaled corticosteroids, and dry mouth with urinary retention comes from antimuscarinics.","boardPrep":"Step 1: β-agonist adverse effects — tachycardia, skeletal muscle tremor and decreased serum potassium (β2 stimulation of Na+/K+-ATPase drives K+ into cells); overdose can cause arrhythmias (Katzung). FA: inhaled β2 agonists can cause tremor and arrhythmia. COMLEX: Check potassium when a patient receives repeated nebulized albuterol.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 13 — sympathetic β-adrenergic dilation of the bronchioles","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q17_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the key adverse effects and safety considerations associated with the major pharmacological classes used to treat asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with severe asthma requires chronic oral prednisone therapy to maintain disease control despite maximal inhaled therapy. After 6 months, he develops hyperglycemia, weight gain, and decreased bone mineral density. Which adverse effect profile is associated with chronic systemic corticosteroid therapy?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle tremor and hypokalemia","B":"Oropharyngeal candidiasis and dysphonia","C":"Dry mouth, urinary retention, and dementia risk","D":"Nausea, tremor, and cardiac arrhythmias","E":"Hyperglycemia, osteoporosis, and immunosuppression"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Chronic systemic corticosteroid therapy causes multiple serious adverse effects including hyperglycemia/diabetes, osteoporosis, weight gain, immunosuppression, adrenal suppression, hypertension, cataracts, and cushingoid features. These toxicities underscore the importance of minimizing systemic steroid use and maximizing inhaled therapy to achieve adequate asthma control. Tremor and hypokalemia are β2-agonist effects, candidiasis and dysphonia are local effects of inhaled corticosteroids, dry mouth, urinary retention, and dementia risk are antimuscarinic effects, and nausea, tremor, and arrhythmias are theophylline toxicities.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Systemic glucocorticoids (FA) cause Cushing syndrome, osteoporosis, hyperglycemia and immunosuppression; Katzung uses prednisone 30–60 mg/day for severe exacerbations and tapers it, and when switching chronic oral therapy to an ICS, tapers slowly to avoid adrenal insufficiency. COMLEX: Higher-dose ICS often allows oral prednisone to be tapered and stopped, because ICS systemic risks are negligible by comparison (Katzung).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — guideline steps, including when oral corticosteroids are used","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q18_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the key adverse effects and safety considerations associated with the major pharmacological classes used to treat asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 65-year-old man with COPD is prescribed ipratropium and tiotropium for maintenance therapy. His physician advises against using both short-acting and long-acting antimuscarinic agents concurrently due to dose-dependent adverse effects. Which adverse effect profile necessitates this precaution?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle tremor and tachycardia","B":"Oropharyngeal candidiasis and dysphonia","C":"Hyperglycemia and adrenal suppression","D":"Dry mouth, urinary retention, and confusion","E":"Nausea, vomiting, and ventricular arrhythmias"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Antimuscarinic agents cause dose-dependent anticholinergic adverse effects including dry mouth (most common), urinary retention in susceptible individuals, and potential cognitive impairment/dementia risk with advancing age. Concurrent use of short-acting and long-acting antimuscarinics increases these risks and is not recommended. Spacer use and dose reduction can minimize symptoms. Tremor and tachycardia are β2-agonist effects, candidiasis and dysphonia are inhaled corticosteroid effects, hyperglycemia and adrenal suppression are corticosteroid effects, and nausea, vomiting, and arrhythmias are theophylline toxicities.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inhaled antimuscarinics can cause dry mouth (managed with a spacer or lower dose), rarely urinary retention in susceptible people, and raise concern for dementia with advancing age; because these effects are dose dependent, combining short- and long-acting inhaled antimuscarinics is not recommended (Katzung). COMLEX: Ask older men with COPD about urinary hesitancy before adding antimuscarinic therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — therapeutic rationale and safety of each class","isHighYield":false}],"23":[{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q01_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic rationale for key pharmacologic classes used in asthma and COPD management","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman with persistent asthma inadequately controlled on low-dose inhaled corticosteroids is started on combination fluticasone/salmeterol therapy instead of doubling her ICS dose. What therapeutic rationale supports adding a LABA rather than increasing the corticosteroid dose?","choices":{"A":"Combination therapy reduces medication administration frequency","B":"LABAs prevent corticosteroid-induced adrenal suppression","C":"Higher ICS doses cause unacceptable systemic toxicity","D":"LABAs provide anti-inflammatory effects equivalent to ICS","E":"ICS plus LABA works better than doubling the ICS dose"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Multiple studies demonstrate that adding a long-acting β2-agonist (LABA) to a standard ICS dose provides better asthma control than doubling the ICS dose. The combination achieves synergistic effects: ICS increases β2-receptor expression and prevents receptor downregulation, while LABAs may enhance corticosteroid anti-inflammatory effects. Systemic toxicity is not the reason: Katzung notes that even high inhaled doses cause at most mild adrenal suppression, with risks negligible compared with oral corticosteroids.","boardPrep":"Step 1: When a standard ICS dose fails, Katzung's options are to double the ICS or add a drug; theophylline or a leukotriene antagonist adds modest benefit, but adding a LABA (salmeterol, formoterol) gives the most impressive gains and has repeatedly beaten doubling the ICS dose. COMLEX: Fixed-dose ICS–LABA inhalers (fluticasone/salmeterol, budesonide/formoterol) keep the LABA always paired with its ICS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — the pharmacologic strategies and goals in asthma and COPD","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q02_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic rationale for key pharmacologic classes used in asthma and COPD management","batch":1,"stem":"A 65-year-old man with severe COPD and frequent exacerbations despite LABA/LAMA therapy undergoes blood testing showing peripheral eosinophilia. His physician considers adding inhaled corticosteroids based on this biomarker. What therapeutic rationale supports using eosinophil counts to guide ICS therapy in COPD?","choices":{"A":"Eosinophils indicate bacterial infection requiring antibiotics","B":"Low eosinophil counts contraindicate bronchodilator use","C":"Elevated eosinophils predict ICS treatment responsiveness","D":"Eosinophilia indicates need for systemic corticosteroids","E":"Eosinophil levels correlate with theophylline efficacy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Recent evidence-based guidelines recommend using blood eosinophil levels to predict ICS benefit in COPD. Elevated blood eosinophils identify patients with T2-high inflammation more likely to respond to ICS therapy, while low eosinophils suggest lower likelihood of benefit and potentially increased pneumonia risk from ICS use.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Katzung — ICS is less central in COPD (lower efficacy; associated bacterial pneumonia risk) and is recommended for severe obstruction, prior exacerbations, or a clear asthma history; guidelines now use low versus high blood eosinophils to predict low versus reasonable ICS benefit, mirroring T2-low and T2-high asthma. COMLEX: Katzung — COPD inflammation is commonly neutrophilic rather than eosinophilic, which is why most COPD responds poorly to ICS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 3 — the clinical phenotypes of COPD","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q03_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic rationale for key pharmacologic classes used in asthma and COPD management","batch":1,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with severe eosinophilic asthma uncontrolled on high-dose ICS/LABA therapy is started on mepolizumab, an anti-IL-5 monoclonal antibody. What therapeutic rationale explains targeting IL-5 in this patient's treatment regimen?","choices":{"A":"IL-5 drives eosinophil maturation and survival","B":"IL-5 activates mast cell degranulation","C":"IL-5 stimulates mucus hypersecretion in airways","D":"IL-5 enhances β2-receptor downregulation","E":"IL-5 increases IgE production by B lymphocytes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Interleukin-5 (IL-5) is the primary cytokine responsible for eosinophil recruitment, activation, and survival in the airways. Anti-IL-5 biologics (mepolizumab, reslizumab, benralizumab) reduce eosinophil numbers and activity, providing benefit in severe eosinophilic asthma phenotypes that remain uncontrolled despite maximal inhaled therapy. β2-receptor downregulation (tachyphylaxis) follows repeated β-agonist use, not IL-5, and B-cell IgE production is driven mainly by IL-4 (with IL-13), not IL-5.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Some severe asthma has airway and peripheral eosinophilia driven by IL-5-secreting T2 lymphocytes; mepolizumab and reslizumab (anti-IL-5) and benralizumab (anti-IL-5 receptor) treat severe eosinophilic asthma (Katzung). FA: IL-5 blockade prevents eosinophil differentiation, maturation, activation and survival. COMLEX: Robbins — IL-5 activates recruited eosinophils, whose major basic protein and cationic protein damage the epithelium.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 6 — the Th2 response at the heart of asthma","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q04_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the current pharmacological treatment guidelines for the management of asthma and COPD, highlighting both commonalities and differences","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with asthma experiences symptoms four times weekly requiring albuterol use. He wakes at night with symptoms twice a month, and his FEV₁ is 85% of predicted. According to current asthma management guidelines, what is the appropriate initial pharmacological approach for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Add low-dose ICS to as-needed SABA","B":"Add leukotriene modifier to as-needed SABA","C":"Start combination ICS/LABA therapy","D":"Continue as-needed SABA monotherapy only","E":"Initiate daily LABA with as-needed SABA"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This patient has mild persistent asthma: symptoms on more than 2 days a week but not daily, infrequent nocturnal symptoms and an FEV₁ above 80% of predicted. Guidelines recommend starting controller therapy with low-dose inhaled corticosteroid, the most effective controller medication, while keeping a SABA for relief; a leukotriene modifier is an alternative if ICS is not tolerated. SABA alone is no longer adequate once symptoms are persistent, ICS/LABA combination belongs to the next step up, and a LABA must never be used without an ICS.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Katzung — when rescue therapy is needed more than twice a week, nocturnal symptoms occur more than twice a month, or FEV1 is below 80% of predicted, add treatment; the first recommendation is low-dose ICS, with a leukotriene receptor antagonist as an alternative. COMLEX: Katzung warns that even mild asthma carries a low but nonzero risk of a severe, life-threatening attack.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — therapeutic goals and strategies in asthma management","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"SABA alone is no longer adequa","E":"SABA alone is no longer adequa"}},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q05_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the current pharmacological treatment guidelines for the management of asthma and COPD, highlighting both commonalities and differences","batch":1,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with COPD experiences persistent dyspnea limiting his daily activities despite using as-needed albuterol. Spirometry shows FEV₁ 55% predicted with minimal reversibility. According to GOLD guidelines, what is the most appropriate initial maintenance therapy?","choices":{"A":"High-dose inhaled corticosteroid monotherapy","B":"Theophylline with as-needed bronchodilators","C":"Long-acting bronchodilator (LABA or LAMA)","D":"Combination of short-acting bronchodilators only","E":"Oral prednisone for continuous inflammation control"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For COPD patients with persistent symptoms and activity limitation, GOLD guidelines recommend initiating long-acting bronchodilator therapy (LABA or LAMA, or both). LAMAs are particularly effective first-line agents for COPD. Unlike asthma, ICS is not first-line therapy for COPD and is reserved for patients with severe obstruction, frequent exacerbations, or elevated eosinophils. Inhaled corticosteroid monotherapy is not first-line in COPD, which responds poorly even to high-dose ICS, and oral prednisone is a short course for exacerbations, not continuous maintenance, because of its systemic toxicity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Katzung (following GOLD) — for COPD with persistent exertional dyspnea and activity limitation, use a long-acting bronchodilator regularly: a LABA, a long-acting anticholinergic, or both; short-acting β agonist and/or ipratropium relieve acute symptoms. LABAs may be used alone in COPD, unlike asthma. COMLEX: Katzung — daily tiotropium improves functional capacity and reduces exacerbations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 1 — the drug classes used in asthma and COPD","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q06_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the current pharmacological treatment guidelines for the management of asthma and COPD, highlighting both commonalities and differences","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with acute respiratory distress. Her history reveals she has both asthma and COPD. She is treated with oxygen, nebulized bronchodilators, and systemic corticosteroids. What represents a key difference in the management of acute exacerbations between asthma and COPD?","choices":{"A":"Asthma exacerbations require oxygen; COPD exacerbations do not","B":"Asthma exacerbations use bronchodilators; COPD uses only steroids","C":"COPD exacerbations require hospitalization; asthma can be outpatient","D":"Asthma exacerbations use systemic steroids; COPD uses only inhaled","E":"COPD flares routinely get antibiotics; asthma flares usually do not"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A key difference in managing acute exacerbations is that COPD exacerbations routinely receive antibiotic therapy because they frequently involve bacterial infection of lower airways (commonly H. influenzae), while asthma exacerbations are typically triggered by viral infections and do not routinely require antibiotics. Both conditions use oxygen, nebulized bronchodilators, and systemic corticosteroids for acute management.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Katzung — management of COPD and asthma exacerbations is similar except for routine antibiotics in COPD, because COPD exacerbations frequently involve bacterial infection of the lower airways; β-lactams, doxycycline or azithromycin cover the prevalent pathogen Haemophilus influenzae. COMLEX: Katzung — COPD exacerbations carry higher mortality than asthma exacerbations, partly from cardiovascular comorbidity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 3 — the key community-acquired organisms, including H. influenzae","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Both conditions use oxygen, ne"}},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q07_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Integrate knowledge of the pathophysiology (pathology) to select and justify appropriate pharmacological treatment regimens for patients with asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with asthma has positive skin tests to perennial aeroallergens and elevated serum IgE levels (450 IU/mL). Despite high-dose ICS/LABA therapy, she continues having frequent exacerbations. Based on her pathophysiology, which targeted therapy is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Mepolizumab targeting IL-5","B":"Roflumilast inhibiting PDE4","C":"Omalizumab targeting IgE","D":"Tiotropium blocking muscarinic receptors","E":"Zileuton inhibiting 5-lipoxygenase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient demonstrates classic IgE-mediated allergic asthma pathophysiology with documented allergen sensitivity and elevated IgE. Omalizumab (anti-IgE monoclonal antibody) is specifically indicated for patients with moderate-to-severe allergic asthma, positive allergen testing, and an IgE level within the range that injections can reduce sufficiently (Katzung Ch 20), who remain uncontrolled on ICS/LABA therapy. Mepolizumab targets IL-5 for the eosinophilic phenotype, roflumilast is approved for COPD but not asthma, tiotropium is a bronchodilator, and zileuton blocks leukotriene synthesis; none lowers IgE, the driver in this allergic, high-IgE patient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Omalizumab binds the portion of IgE that attaches to its receptors, so it blocks IgE binding without activating IgE already bound (no mast-cell degranulation); it is restricted to moderate-to-severe asthma with perennial allergic sensitization, dosed by total IgE and body weight (Katzung). FA: used in allergic asthma with ↑ IgE resistant to ICS and LABA. COMLEX: Katzung — its main clinical effect is fewer and less severe exacerbations with lower corticosteroid requirement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 9 — the IgE–mast cell process behind an asthma attack","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q08_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Integrate knowledge of the pathophysiology (pathology) to select and justify appropriate pharmacological treatment regimens for patients with asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with COPD continues to have exacerbations despite optimal bronchodilator therapy. Pulmonary function tests show FEV₁ 45% predicted, and complete blood count reveals low blood eosinophil levels. Based on his pathophysiology, what treatment consideration is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Add high-dose inhaled corticosteroids","B":"Initiate anti-IL-5 biologic therapy","C":"Start omalizumab for allergic component","D":"Optimize bronchodilators without adding ICS","E":"Add leukotriene receptor antagonist therapy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient has severe COPD with low eosinophil counts, indicating neutrophilic rather than eosinophilic inflammation (T2-low phenotype). Low blood eosinophil levels predict poor ICS response and potentially increased pneumonia risk. Current guidelines recommend against routine ICS use in COPD patients with low eosinophils, favoring optimized bronchodilator therapy. Omalizumab is restricted to moderate to severe asthma with perennial allergic sensitization, which this patient does not have.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Katzung — COPD is commonly neutrophilic rather than eosinophilic and responds poorly even to high-dose ICS; ICS is linked to bacterial pneumonia, and low blood eosinophils predict a low likelihood of ICS benefit, so bronchodilators are optimized instead. COMLEX: Katzung — roflumilast reduces exacerbation frequency and is an option when exacerbations continue on bronchodilators.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — adverse effects and safety of the major drug classes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Current guidelines recommend a"}},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q09_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Integrate knowledge of the pathophysiology (pathology) to select and justify appropriate pharmacological treatment regimens for patients with asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 40-year-old man with moderate persistent asthma demonstrates eosinophilic airway inflammation on induced sputum analysis. His symptoms occur daily with nocturnal awakenings three times weekly despite regular SABA use. What pharmacological approach best addresses the underlying inflammatory pathophysiology?","choices":{"A":"Increase SABA frequency to prevent symptoms","B":"Add LABA for sustained bronchodilation","C":"Add oral theophylline for bronchodilator effect","D":"Start inhaled corticosteroid controller therapy","E":"Start antimuscarinic agent to reduce mucus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient has moderate persistent asthma with documented eosinophilic inflammation. The pathophysiology of eosinophilic asthma involves T2 lymphocyte-mediated inflammation with IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 production. Inhaled corticosteroids directly address this pathophysiology by inhibiting inflammatory cytokine production and are the most effective controller therapy for eosinophilic asthma. SABAs alone do not address underlying inflammation. A LABA has no anti-inflammatory action and should not be used without an ICS in asthma, and antimuscarinics have only a limited, mainly bronchodilator role in asthma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Katzung — 'T2-high' asthma (more sputum and blood eosinophilia, positive skin tests, higher IgE) improved FEV1 with 8 weeks of ICS, whereas 'T2-low' asthma did not; ICS act mainly by inhibiting airway infiltration by lymphocytes, eosinophils and mast cells. COMLEX: FA — ICS inactivate NF-κB and are first-line controller therapy for chronic asthma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 7 — environmental factors and eosinophil-rich asthma morphology","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"SABAs alone do not address und","E":"A LABA has no anti-inflammator"}},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q10_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic rationale for key pharmacologic classes used in asthma and COPD management","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with moderate persistent asthma is prescribed budesonide/formoterol combination therapy to be used both twice daily for maintenance and as needed for symptom relief (SMART therapy). What therapeutic rationale supports this flexible dosing strategy compared to traditional fixed-dose controller plus separate reliever approach?","choices":{"A":"Formoterol has a slower onset than albuterol for symptom relief","B":"One inhaler gives anti-inflammatory and bronchodilator action","C":"Budesonide prevents formoterol-induced tachyphylaxis","D":"Combination therapy eliminates need for rescue medication","E":"SMART therapy reduces total medication costs significantly"},"correct":"B","explanation":"SMART (single maintenance and reliever therapy) uses budesonide/formoterol for both scheduled maintenance and as-needed relief. The rationale is that each symptom-requiring dose provides both rapid bronchodilation (formoterol has rapid onset like albuterol) and additional anti-inflammatory effect (budesonide), potentially providing better inflammation control than fixed-dose ICS with separate SABA reliever.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SMART (single maintenance and reliever therapy) — formoterol's rapid onset lets low-dose budesonide/formoterol serve as both controller and reliever; it is part of US guidelines since 2020 for scheduled and as-needed use (Katzung). COMLEX: Each rescue puff also delivers corticosteroid, so the anti-inflammatory dose rises when symptoms do.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 1 — the pharmacologic classes, including ICS–LABA combinations","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q11_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic rationale for key pharmacologic classes used in asthma and COPD management","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with severe COPD continues having frequent exacerbations despite triple therapy with LABA/LAMA/ICS. His physician considers adding roflumilast. What therapeutic rationale supports adding a selective PDE4 inhibitor in this clinical context?","choices":{"A":"PDE4 inhibitors provide additional bronchodilation beyond triple therapy","B":"Roflumilast replaces the need for inhaled corticosteroids","C":"PDE4 inhibition reduces exacerbations through anti-inflammatory effects","D":"PDE4 inhibitors enhance mucociliary clearance of bacteria","E":"Selective PDE4 inhibition prevents LABA-induced tachycardia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Roflumilast is a selective phosphodiesterase-4 (PDE4) inhibitor that reduces inflammatory cell activity, particularly neutrophils, and decreases exacerbation frequency in severe COPD with chronic bronchitis. Unlike non-selective theophylline, selective PDE4 inhibition provides anti-inflammatory benefits with fewer cardiac and CNS effects. It's added when exacerbations persist despite optimal bronchodilator and ICS therapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Roflumilast is a selective PDE4 inhibitor (raises cAMP, lowers airway inflammation) that reduces the frequency of COPD exacerbations; it is approved for COPD but not for asthma, and Katzung credits it with a better therapeutic ratio than theophylline. COMLEX: Its place is the patient who keeps exacerbating on maximal inhaled therapy, and Katzung notes that PDE4 inhibitors were limited by nausea, headache, and diarrhea.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — the narrow therapeutic index of theophylline, the nonselective PDE inhibitor that roflumilast improves on","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q12_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic rationale for key pharmacologic classes used in asthma and COPD management","batch":1,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with asthma and aspirin sensitivity experiences severe bronchospasm after taking ibuprofen for headache. Her physician explains that her leukotriene levels are elevated. What therapeutic rationale supports preferential use of leukotriene modifiers in aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease?","choices":{"A":"Leukotriene modifiers prevent NSAID absorption","B":"Aspirin sensitivity indicates IgE-mediated allergy","C":"NSAID hypersensitivity contraindicates corticosteroids","D":"Leukotriene modifiers enhance aspirin metabolism","E":"Aspirin triggers leukotriene overproduction pathway"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (AERD) involves cyclooxygenase-1 inhibition by NSAIDs, shunting arachidonic acid metabolism toward the leukotriene pathway, causing cysteinyl leukotriene overproduction. This leads to severe bronchospasm and rhinosinusitis. Leukotriene modifiers (montelukast, zafirlukast, zileuton) are particularly effective in AERD by blocking this pathophysiologic mechanism. ICS remains important, but leukotriene modifiers provide additional targeted benefit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease combines asthma, nasal polyps, and reactions to COX-1–inhibiting NSAIDs, with leukotriene overproduction driving the bronchoconstriction; montelukast and zafirlukast block the CysLT1 receptor and zileuton blocks 5-lipoxygenase. COMLEX: FA singles out antileukotrienes as especially good for aspirin-induced asthma, so ask any asthmatic with nasal polyps about NSAID reactions before suggesting an NSAID.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — the asthma phenotypes, including the aspirin-sensitive form","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q13_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the current pharmacological treatment guidelines for the management of asthma and COPD, highlighting both commonalities and differences","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman with asthma experiences daily symptoms requiring albuterol use and has nocturnal awakenings four times weekly. Her FEV₁ is 65% predicted. According to stepwise asthma management guidelines, what is the most appropriate treatment approach?","choices":{"A":"Low-dose ICS daily with as-needed SABA only","B":"Daily leukotriene modifier as monotherapy","C":"High-dose ICS monotherapy with as-needed albuterol","D":"Biologic therapy with omalizumab injections","E":"Low-dose ICS–formoterol maintenance and reliever"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient has moderate persistent asthma (daily symptoms, frequent nocturnal awakenings and reduced FEV₁). At this step, guidelines prefer low-dose ICS–formoterol taken both as maintenance and as reliever (the SMART approach, now part of US guidelines); Katzung notes that low-dose budesonide–formoterol used this way is as effective as a four-times-higher dose of budesonide, and that adding a LABA is more effective than doubling the ICS dose (Ch 20). Low-dose ICS with SABA alone is the step for mild persistent asthma, a leukotriene modifier alone is a weaker alternative, high-dose ICS monotherapy is inferior to ICS plus LABA, and omalizumab is reserved for severe allergic asthma uncontrolled on high-dose therapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rescue SABA use more than twice a week, night symptoms more than twice a month, or FEV1 below 80% predicted means as-needed albuterol alone is no longer enough; Katzung notes low-dose budesonide–formoterol taken as both controller and reliever (SMART) works as well as a four-times-higher budesonide dose. COMLEX: Formoterol's rapid onset is what lets one combination inhaler serve as the reliever as well as the controller.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 4 — FEV1, FVC, and the FEV1/FVC ratio used to grade airway obstruction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q14_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the current pharmacological treatment guidelines for the management of asthma and COPD, highlighting both commonalities and differences","batch":1,"stem":"A 68-year-old former smoker with COPD reports dyspnea with minimal exertion and has had three exacerbations requiring antibiotics and prednisone in the past year. His FEV₁ is 42% predicted with blood eosinophils of 350 cells/μL. According to GOLD guidelines, which treatment regimen is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"LABA/LAMA/ICS triple therapy","B":"LAMA monotherapy","C":"LABA plus LAMA combination","D":"SABA as needed only","E":"LABA monotherapy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This patient has severe airflow limitation (FEV₁ 42% predicted), frequent exacerbations (three in the past year), and raised blood eosinophils, which suggest ICS responsiveness (Katzung Ch 20). GOLD guidelines recommend LABA/LAMA/ICS triple therapy for patients with severe airflow limitation and frequent exacerbations, especially with elevated eosinophils predicting ICS benefit. As-needed SABA alone is for acute relief and is far too little for persistent symptoms and repeated exacerbations, which call for regular long-acting bronchodilators.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In COPD, blood eosinophil counts predict which patients will benefit from an inhaled corticosteroid; severe airflow obstruction plus frequent exacerbations plus high eosinophils is the combination that justifies LABA/LAMA/ICS. COMLEX: Weigh that benefit against the higher risk of bacterial pneumonia that Katzung links to ICS use in COPD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — the etiology, pathology, and clinical course of COPD, the exacerbation-prone disease treated here","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q15_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the current pharmacological treatment guidelines for the management of asthma and COPD, highlighting both commonalities and differences","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with COPD asks why his physician prescribes him both a long-acting bronchodilator and encourages smoking cessation but doesn't emphasize inhaled steroids as strongly as his friend with asthma. What principle distinguishes COPD management priorities from asthma management?","choices":{"A":"ICS is contraindicated in all COPD patients due to infection risk","B":"COPD requires antibiotics as primary therapy; asthma requires steroids","C":"Bronchodilators work in COPD; only biologics work in asthma","D":"COPD centers on smoking cessation and bronchodilators, not ICS","E":"COPD management focuses on systemic steroids; asthma uses inhaled"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A key difference in management priorities is that COPD treatment emphasizes smoking cessation (slows disease progression) and bronchodilators (LABA/LAMA) as first-line therapy. Unlike asthma where ICS is the cornerstone controller therapy, ICS plays a less central role in COPD, reserved for patients with frequent exacerbations, severe obstruction, or elevated eosinophils, partially due to lower efficacy and pneumonia risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Katzung contrasts the two diseases — COPD inflammation is usually neutrophilic rather than eosinophilic, responds poorly even to high-dose ICS, and its obstruction is not fully reversible, whereas ICS is the cornerstone controller in asthma. COMLEX: Long-acting antimuscarinics (tiotropium, umeclidinium, aclidinium) are approved for COPD maintenance and reduce exacerbations, which is why bronchodilators anchor COPD care.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 9 — clinically differentiating the obstructive lung diseases, the distinction that drives the two treatment plans","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q16_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Integrate knowledge of the pathophysiology (pathology) to select and justify appropriate pharmacological treatment regimens for patients with asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old man with asthma reports using his albuterol inhaler 4-5 times daily for the past month. Despite this frequent SABA use, he continues having symptoms and his peak flow measurements show progressive decline. What pathophysiologic principle explains why increasing SABA frequency without adding controller therapy is inadequate?","choices":{"A":"β2-receptors undergo downregulation with frequent agonist exposure","B":"SABAs provide bronchodilation but do not address airway inflammation","C":"Albuterol loses efficacy after one month of regular use","D":"Frequent SABA use causes paradoxical bronchoconstriction","E":"SABAs induce corticosteroid resistance in airway cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The pathophysiologic principle is that SABAs provide symptomatic bronchodilation but have no anti-inflammatory effect on the underlying eosinophilic inflammation driving asthma. Frequent SABA use (>2 times/week) indicates inadequate disease control requiring controller therapy (ICS) to address inflammation. Relying on bronchodilators alone allows inflammation to progress, increasing exacerbation risk despite temporary symptom relief. Albuterol does not simply stop working after a month; regular use causes some tachyphylaxis at low doses, but maximal bronchodilation is still reached within usual doses.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rescue SABA use more than twice a week signals the need for a controller — a low-dose ICS first, with a leukotriene antagonist as the alternative — because bronchodilators relax airway smooth muscle but leave the airway inflammation untreated. COMLEX: FA lists inhaled glucocorticoids as first-line therapy for chronic asthma; rising rescue use with falling peak flows calls for stepping up, not more albuterol.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 9 — the mast cell–driven immunologic process of an asthma attack","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q17_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Integrate knowledge of the pathophysiology (pathology) to select and justify appropriate pharmacological treatment regimens for patients with asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with 40 pack-year smoking history and COPD continues smoking despite counseling. His physician explains that smoking cessation is the only intervention proven to slow FEV₁ decline in COPD. What pathophysiologic mechanism explains why pharmacologic therapy cannot substitute for smoking cessation in preventing disease progression?","choices":{"A":"Bronchodilators enhance cigarette smoke absorption","B":"ICS therapy accelerates emphysema progression in smokers","C":"Nicotine directly antagonizes LABA and LAMA mechanisms","D":"Smoke keeps driving inflammation and alveolar destruction","E":"Inhaled medications are completely inactivated by smoke"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pathophysiologic principle is that ongoing cigarette smoke exposure causes continuous oxidative stress, neutrophilic inflammation, protease activation, and progressive alveolar destruction (emphysema). While medications improve symptoms and reduce exacerbations, they cannot prevent the structural lung damage from ongoing smoke exposure. Smoking cessation is the only intervention that slows the accelerated FEV₁ decline characteristic of COPD, addressing the root cause rather than just managing symptoms. Katzung describes no antagonism of LABA or LAMA action by nicotine; the limit is that bronchodilators relieve airflow without stopping the smoke-driven inflammation, which keeps progressing while exposure continues.","boardPrep":"Step 1: COPD airflow limitation reflects an abnormal inflammatory response to cigarette smoke that progresses as long as exposure continues; FA ties the alveolar destruction to oxidative stress, CD8+ T cells, neutrophils, macrophages, and a protease–antiprotease imbalance that destroys elastic fibers. COMLEX: Drugs relieve symptoms and cut exacerbations, but Katzung reports mixed data on whether even LAMAs slow lung-function decline — removing the smoke is the disease-modifying step.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — centriacinar emphysema, the smoking-driven alveolar destruction that continues with ongoing exposure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q18_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Integrate knowledge of the pathophysiology (pathology) to select and justify appropriate pharmacological treatment regimens for patients with asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with asthma presents with an acute exacerbation triggered by a viral upper respiratory infection. Her symptoms are not improving with frequent albuterol use. Based on understanding viral-triggered asthma pathophysiology, what treatment addition is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Systemic corticosteroids to reduce inflammation","B":"LABA to provide longer bronchodilation","C":"Antihistamines to reduce viral inflammation","D":"Antibiotics to treat the viral infection","E":"Leukotriene modifiers to prevent viral replication"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Viral respiratory infections trigger asthma exacerbations by inducing airway inflammation, increasing bronchial hyperreactivity, and causing epithelial damage that enhances allergen exposure. The pathophysiology involves amplified inflammatory response with increased eosinophils, cytokines, and mucus production. Systemic corticosteroids (prednisone or methylprednisolone) directly address this inflammatory cascade, reduce airway obstruction, and prevent hospitalization. Antibiotics are ineffective for viral infections, and bronchodilators alone cannot resolve the inflammatory component.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Asthma exacerbations are most often associated with viral respiratory infections and combine intense smooth-muscle contraction with mucus plugging and mucosal edema; an exacerbation not responding to bronchodilators needs systemic corticosteroids (Katzung: oral prednisone 30–60 mg/day or IV methylprednisolone 0.5–1 mg/kg every 6–12 hours). COMLEX: Katzung notes systemic steroids can usually be stopped in 5–10 days once the obstruction improves.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 7 — the environmental triggers of asthma and the morphology of status asthmaticus","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Antibiotics are ineffective fo"}}],"25":[{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q01_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major restrictive lung diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old male former smoker presents with progressive dyspnea and nonproductive cough over 18 months. High-resolution CT shows reticular opacities with honeycombing predominantly in the lower lobes and subpleural regions. Lung biopsy reveals patchy interstitial fibrosis with areas of dense fibrosis adjacent to relatively normal lung. What pathologic feature is the hallmark of this patient's disease?","choices":{"A":"Temporal homogeneity of fibrotic lesions","B":"Noncaseating granulomas along lymphatics","C":"Temporal heterogeneity with fibroblastic foci","D":"Masson bodies in alveolar spaces","E":"Accumulation of pigmented macrophages in airspaces"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient has idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF) with the usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP) pattern. The hallmark features are spatial heterogeneity (normal lung adjacent to fibrotic areas) and temporal heterogeneity (coexistence of early fibroblastic foci and late dense fibrosis). This contrasts with NSIP, which shows temporal homogeneity. Noncaseating granulomas along lymphatics point to sarcoidosis, Masson bodies in airspaces to organizing pneumonia, and airspaces packed with pigmented macrophages to desquamative interstitial pneumonia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UIP, the histologic pattern of IPF, is patchy fibrosis with spatial heterogeneity (normal lung beside scar) and temporal heterogeneity (early fibroblastic foci beside dense old collagen), worst in the lower lobes, subpleural regions, and interlobular septa, ending in honeycomb lung. COMLEX: An older patient with insidious exertional dyspnea, dry cough, and velcro-like crackles fits IPF; Robbins gives a median survival of about 3.8 years.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — the restrictive pattern on the maximum expiratory flow–volume curve","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q02_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major restrictive lung diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with IPF undergoes genetic testing revealing mutations in TERT and TERC genes. What pathogenic mechanism do these mutations affect in the development of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis?","choices":{"A":"Telomere maintenance, causing cell senescence","B":"Surfactant protein folding in type II pneumocytes","C":"Mucin secretion altering mucociliary clearance","D":"Collagen synthesis by activated fibroblasts","E":"TGF-β production by injured epithelial cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"TERT and TERC genes encode components essential for telomere maintenance. Mutations in these genes (along with PARN and RTEL1) are associated with increased risk of IPF (Robbins Ch 15). Defective telomere maintenance leads to cellular senescence, making alveolar epithelial cells more susceptible to environmental injury. Robbins does not tie TERT and TERC to surfactant folding, mucin secretion, or collagen synthesis; mucin secretion is the effect of the separate MUC5B promoter variant, and collagen deposition is a downstream step of fibrosis driven by cytokines such as TGF-β.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TERT, TERC, PARN, and RTEL1 mutations impair telomere maintenance and push alveolar epithelium into senescence; up to 15% of familial IPF carries inherited telomere-gene defects and up to 25% of sporadic IPF shows abnormal telomere shortening. COMLEX: IPF rarely appears before age 50, and hypoxemia, cyanosis, and clubbing arrive late in its insidious course.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — the alveolar epithelial cells whose recurrent injury and senescence start IPF","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q03_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major restrictive lung diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old African American woman presents with bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy on chest X-ray during routine evaluation. Mediastinal lymph node biopsy reveals well-formed noncaseating granulomas composed of epithelioid macrophages. Bronchoalveolar lavage fluid shows a CD4:CD8 ratio of 10:1. What cytokine is released in particularly large amounts and serves as a marker of disease activity?","choices":{"A":"Interleukin-5","B":"Interleukin-13","C":"Interferon-gamma","D":"Tumor necrosis factor","E":"Interleukin-4"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient has sarcoidosis. Activated alveolar macrophages release tumor necrosis factor (TNF) in particularly large amounts, and TNF concentration in bronchoalveolar fluid serves as a marker of sarcoidosis disease activity. The elevated CD4:CD8 ratio (5:1 to 15:1) reflects oligoclonal T-cell expansion in response to an unidentified antigen. IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 are Th2 cytokines of allergic asthma, and IFN-γ, though raised in sarcoidosis as a Th1 cytokine that activates macrophages, is not the macrophage product Robbins names as the activity marker.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sarcoidosis is a CD4+ Th1 disease — BAL CD4:CD8 of 5:1 to 15:1 with IL-2 and IFN-γ — and activated alveolar macrophages release TNF in particularly large amounts, so BAL TNF tracks disease activity. COMLEX: Systemic clues include anergy to PPD and Candida skin tests and polyclonal hypergammaglobulinemia (Robbins); FA adds raised serum ACE and hypercalcemia from macrophage 1α-hydroxylase.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 49 → Obj 4 — TB granuloma biology and the TST, the infectious mimic that must be excluded before diagnosing sarcoidosis","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 are Th2 ","C":"IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 are Th2 ","E":"IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 are Th2 "}},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q04_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major restrictive lung diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old dairy farmer presents with episodic dyspnea, fever, and cough occurring 4-6 hours after working in the barn with moldy hay. Lung biopsy shows interstitial pneumonitis with lymphocytes and plasma cells, along with poorly formed nonnecrotizing granulomas. What is the primary immunologic mechanism underlying this patient's condition?","choices":{"A":"IgE-mediated type I hypersensitivity","B":"Type III and IV hypersensitivity to inhaled organic antigens","C":"Autoantibody formation against alveolar basement membrane","D":"Deficiency of alpha-1 antitrypsin","E":"Interleukin-5 mediated eosinophilic inflammation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient has hypersensitivity pneumonitis (farmer's lung) caused by thermophilic actinomycetes in moldy hay. The pathogenesis involves both type III hypersensitivity (immune complex deposition, evidenced by serum antibodies and complement in vessel walls) and type IV cell-mediated hypersensitivity (granuloma formation, T-cell infiltration). The episodic timing (4-6 hours post-exposure) is characteristic. α1-Antitrypsin deficiency causes panacinar emphysema, not a granulomatous pneumonitis after organic dust exposure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypersensitivity pneumonitis is a mixed type III (serum antibodies; complement and immunoglobulin in vessel walls) and type IV (nonnecrotizing granulomas in about two-thirds) reaction to inhaled organic antigens such as thermophilic actinomycetes in moldy hay; unlike asthma, it targets the alveolar walls. COMLEX: Attacks of fever, dyspnea, and cough begin 4–6 hours after exposure and recur with re-exposure, so the occupational and hobby history is the key diagnostic step.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 6 — the exaggerated Th2, type I reaction of asthma, the contrasting hypersensitivity pattern","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q05_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major restrictive lung diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old pigeon breeder develops progressive dyspnea over several years despite reducing bird contact. Lung biopsy shows interstitial fibrosis with fibroblastic foci, honeycombing, and obliterative bronchiolitis. What phase of hypersensitivity pneumonitis does this morphology represent?","choices":{"A":"Acute phase with alveolar damage","B":"Subacute phase with granulomas","C":"Resolution phase with normal architecture","D":"Organizing phase with Masson bodies","E":"Chronic phase with irreversible fibrosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This represents chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis with progression to irreversible interstitial fibrosis. The chronic phase shows fibroblastic foci, honeycombing, and obliterative bronchiolitis. Some chronic cases progress to a UIP-like pattern that lacks characteristic features and may be irreversible despite antigen removal. This contrasts with acute/subacute phases, which may be reversible with antigen avoidance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Continuous, protracted antigen exposure turns episodic hypersensitivity pneumonitis into a chronic form with interstitial fibrosis, fibroblastic foci, honeycombing, and obliterative bronchiolitis alongside granulomas. COMLEX: FA notes acute HP is often self-limiting once the stimulus is removed but chronic HP leaves irreversible fibrosis, so early antigen removal is the intervention that matters.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 7 — how fibrosis and other disease states impair gas diffusion across the respiratory membrane","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q06_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major restrictive lung diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with progressive dyspnea undergoes lung biopsy showing diffuse interstitial fibrosis with lesions of roughly the same age and appearance. No fibroblastic foci, honeycomb changes, or granulomas are identified. What key morphologic feature distinguishes this pattern from usual interstitial pneumonia?","choices":{"A":"Presence of Masson bodies in alveolar ducts","B":"Accumulation of smokers' macrophages","C":"Noncaseating granulomas along lymphatics","D":"Temporal homogeneity of fibrotic lesions","E":"Eosinophilic infiltration of alveolar walls"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient has nonspecific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP) with the fibrosing pattern. The key distinguishing feature from UIP is temporal homogeneity—all fibrotic lesions appear roughly the same age. NSIP lacks the fibroblastic foci and honeycomb changes characteristic of UIP. NSIP has a better prognosis than UIP and may respond to immunosuppressive therapy. Masson bodies define organizing pneumonia, smokers' macrophages define desquamative interstitial pneumonia, granulomas along lymphatics define sarcoidosis, and eosinophil-rich infiltrates define the pulmonary eosinophilias; none of these is what separates NSIP from UIP.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The fibrosing pattern of NSIP shows lesions of roughly the same age (temporal homogeneity) with no honeycombing, hyaline membranes, or granulomas; NSIP carries a much better prognosis than UIP. COMLEX: Typical patients are female nonsmokers in their sixth decade with bilateral, symmetric, lower-lobe reticular opacities, and unlike UIP the disease may respond to immunosuppressive drugs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 4 — FEV1, FVC, and the preserved FEV1/FVC ratio of restrictive disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q07_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the key distinguishing characteristics and pathologic findings","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man develops cough and dyspnea following viral pneumonia. Chest CT shows patchy areas of airspace consolidation. Lung biopsy reveals polypoid plugs of loose organizing connective tissue within alveolar ducts and alveoli, all of the same age. The underlying lung architecture is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia","B":"Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","C":"Desquamative interstitial pneumonia","D":"Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","E":"Sarcoidosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This patient has cryptogenic organizing pneumonia (COP), characterized by Masson bodies (polypoid plugs of organizing connective tissue) of uniform age in alveolar ducts, alveoli, and bronchioles. The underlying architecture is normal with no interstitial fibrosis or honeycomb change. COP can follow infections or be idiopathic. Most patients require oral steroids for 6+ months but have good long-term prognosis. Sarcoidosis forms noncaseating granulomas along lymphatics, not airspace plugs of organizing connective tissue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Masson bodies — polypoid plugs of loose organizing connective tissue in alveolar ducts, alveoli, and often bronchioles — define organizing pneumonia, which is called cryptogenic only when no cause (infection, inhaled toxins, drugs, connective tissue disease, GVHD) is found. COMLEX: Patients have cough, dyspnea, and patchy subpleural or peribronchial consolidation; some recover spontaneously, but most need oral steroids for 6 months or longer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 7 — the injury pattern of viral pneumonia, one of the infections that organizing pneumonia can follow","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The underlying architecture is"}},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q08_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the key distinguishing characteristics and pathologic findings","batch":1,"stem":"A 48-year-old male current smoker presents with insidious dyspnea and dry cough with clubbing. Lung biopsy shows abundant macrophages with dusty brown pigment filling airspaces, with only mild thickening of alveolar septa and minimal interstitial fibrosis. What distinguishes this entity from other interstitial lung diseases?","choices":{"A":"Presence of noncaseating granulomas","B":"Macrophages filling airspaces, with little fibrosis","C":"Fibroblastic foci with temporal heterogeneity","D":"Organizing connective tissue plugs in alveoli","E":"Eosinophilic infiltration with Charcot-Leyden crystals"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient has desquamative interstitial pneumonia (DIP), characterized by large collections of pigmented macrophages (smokers' macrophages) filling the airspaces with minimal interstitial fibrosis. Despite its name suggesting \"desquamated pneumocytes,\" the cells are actually macrophages. DIP occurs in smokers, has excellent response to steroids and smoking cessation, and has much better prognosis than UIP. Noncaseating granulomas point to sarcoidosis or hypersensitivity pneumonitis, fibroblastic foci with temporal heterogeneity to UIP, organizing plugs to organizing pneumonia, and eosinophils with Charcot-Leyden crystals to asthma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DIP fills the airspaces with macrophages holding dusty brown pigment (smokers' macrophages), once mistaken for desquamated pneumocytes — hence the misnomer — with only mild septal thickening and fibrosis; virtually all patients smoke. COMLEX: It presents in the fourth or fifth decade with insidious dyspnea, dry cough, and clubbing, and typically responds well to steroids and smoking cessation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 6 — pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis, another smoking-related interstitial disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q09_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the key distinguishing characteristics and pathologic findings","batch":1,"stem":"Two women with connective tissue disease have biopsy-proven nonspecific interstitial pneumonia. The first biopsy shows mild to moderate interstitial inflammation of lymphocytes and a few plasma cells; the second shows diffuse interstitial fibrosis of uniform age. According to Robbins, how does the first patient's pattern compare with the second's?","choices":{"A":"It carries the worse prognosis of the two patterns","B":"It shows fibroblastic foci and honeycombing","C":"It is the pattern of older patients with UIP","D":"It does not respond to immunosuppressive drugs","E":"Its patients are younger, with a better prognosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Robbins divides nonspecific interstitial pneumonia into a cellular pattern, mild to moderate chronic interstitial inflammation of lymphocytes and a few plasma cells, and a fibrosing pattern of diffuse or patchy fibrosis of roughly the same age (temporal homogeneity). Patients with the cellular pattern are somewhat younger than those with the fibrosing pattern and have a better prognosis, and NSIP as a whole has a much better prognosis than UIP. A worse prognosis belongs to the fibrosing pattern, and worse still to UIP. Fibroblastic foci and honeycombing are absent in both NSIP patterns and mark UIP. Unlike UIP, NSIP may benefit from immunosuppressive drugs, suggesting an immune basis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins splits NSIP into a cellular pattern (lymphocytes and a few plasma cells) and a fibrosing pattern (uniform-age fibrosis); cellular-pattern patients are somewhat younger and have the better prognosis, and NSIP as a whole does far better than UIP. COMLEX: Immunosuppressive drugs may help NSIP, which suggests an immune basis, so the biopsy pattern shapes both prognosis and treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 9 — diffusing capacity, the PFT measure that falls as interstitial disease thickens the alveolar walls","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q10_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major restrictive lung diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 34-year-old African American woman presents with bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy on routine chest X-ray. Lymph node biopsy reveals noncaseating granulomas containing laminated calcium concretions and star-shaped inclusions within multinucleated giant cells. What are these pathologic inclusions called?","choices":{"A":"Charcot-Leyden crystals and Curschmann spirals","B":"Schaumann bodies and asteroid bodies","C":"Ferruginous bodies and asbestos fibers","D":"Mallory bodies and Councilman bodies","E":"Russell bodies and Dutcher bodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Schaumann bodies (laminated calcifications) and asteroid bodies (star-shaped inclusions within giant cells) are characteristic but not diagnostic findings in sarcoidosis granulomas. These inclusions are found within multinucleated giant cells in noncaseating granulomas. While not pathognomonic, they support the diagnosis of sarcoidosis in the appropriate clinical context. Charcot-Leyden crystals and Curschmann spirals are found in the sputum of patients with asthma, not in sarcoid granulomas.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sarcoid granulomas are well formed and nonnecrotizing; Schaumann bodies (laminated calcium–protein concretions) and asteroid bodies (stellate inclusions) sit within giant cells in about 60% of granulomas but also occur in TB, so they are characteristic, not pathognomonic. COMLEX: Hilar node or lung involvement occurs in 90% of cases, and granulomas in the bronchial submucosa give bronchoscopic biopsy its high diagnostic yield.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — GPA's necrotizing granulomatous vasculitis, a contrast to sarcoid's nonnecrotizing granulomas","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q11_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major restrictive lung diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with IPF has a family history of pulmonary fibrosis affecting his father and sister. Genetic testing reveals a MUC5B promoter polymorphism. What role does this genetic variant play in the pathogenesis of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis?","choices":{"A":"Defective telomere maintenance leading to cellular senescence","B":"Impaired surfactant protein folding in type II pneumocytes","C":"Enhanced collagen synthesis by activated fibroblasts","D":"Increased TGF-β signaling in epithelial cells","E":"Altered mucin production affecting mucociliary clearance"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The MUC5B promoter polymorphism (rs35705950) is the strongest genetic risk factor for IPF, associated with roughly one-third of IPF cases (Robbins Ch 15). This variant increases MUC5B mucin production, and the pathogenic mechanism appears to involve altered mucociliary clearance and impaired host defense, leading to recurrent epithelial injury. However, the exact mechanism by which increased mucin leads to fibrosis remains under investigation. Collagen production by fibroblasts is the downstream fibrotic step driven by cytokines such as TGF-β, not what the MUC5B variant changes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A MUC5B promoter polymorphism, present in roughly one-third of IPF cases, greatly increases MUC5B mucin secretion and may alter mucociliary clearance; exactly how this raises IPF risk is still uncertain. COMLEX: Inherited risk still interacts with exposures — cigarette smoking raises IPF risk several-fold, and air pollution, microaspiration, metal fumes, and wood dust are also implicated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the ciliated and goblet cells of the mucociliary apparatus that MUC5B mucin feeds","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q12_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major restrictive lung diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old man presents with bilateral hilar adenopathy, erythema nodosum, and arthralgias. Chest imaging shows symmetric hilar and right paratracheal lymphadenopathy without parenchymal infiltrates. What is the most likely stage of sarcoidosis and prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Stage 1, excellent prognosis with high spontaneous remission","B":"Stage 2 with moderate prognosis requiring corticosteroid therapy","C":"Stage 3 with poor prognosis and progressive fibrosis","D":"Stage 4 with end-stage fibrosis and honeycomb changes","E":"Stage 0 with normal radiographic findings"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This patient presents with Löfgren syndrome (bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, erythema nodosum, arthralgias), an acute presentation of sarcoidosis. Stage 1 sarcoidosis shows hilar/mediastinal lymphadenopathy without parenchymal disease and has the best prognosis, with frequent spontaneous remission; overall, 65–70% of patients with sarcoidosis recover with minimal or no residual disease (Robbins Ch 15). Löfgren syndrome specifically has excellent prognosis and often resolves without treatment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Löfgren syndrome is fever with bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, erythema nodosum, and migratory polyarthritis (Bootcamp), an acute presentation of sarcoidosis; erythema nodosum is a septal panniculitis producing painful red nodules on the shins (Robbins). COMLEX: Robbins notes sarcoidosis may remit spontaneously or with steroids, and FA reserves glucocorticoids for symptomatic disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 9 — lymphomas among the miscellaneous lung tumors, a differential for bilateral hilar adenopathy","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q13_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the key distinguishing characteristics and pathologic findings","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old male smoker with insidious dyspnea undergoes lung biopsy showing accumulation of pigmented macrophages in airspaces with minimal interstitial fibrosis. High-resolution CT demonstrates ground-glass opacities in lower lobes. What feature best distinguishes this entity from respiratory bronchiolitis-associated interstitial lung disease (RB-ILD)?","choices":{"A":"RB-ILD shows Masson bodies; DIP shows noncaseating granulomas","B":"RB-ILD centers on bronchioles; DIP fills alveoli diffusely","C":"RB-ILD responds to steroids; DIP requires smoking cessation alone","D":"RB-ILD has better prognosis; DIP progresses to honeycomb fibrosis","E":"RB-ILD shows temporal heterogeneity; DIP shows fibroblastic foci"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is desquamative interstitial pneumonia (DIP): pigmented macrophages filling the airspaces with minimal fibrosis. DIP and RB-ILD are both smoking-related and share pigmented macrophages, but they differ in distribution: RB-ILD is patchy and bronchiolocentric, with macrophages in respiratory bronchioles, while DIP involves the alveolar spaces diffusely (Robbins Ch 15). Prognosis does not separate them — DIP typically has an excellent response to steroid therapy and smoking cessation and only occasionally progresses to fibrosis. Masson bodies belong to organizing pneumonia and granulomas to sarcoidosis or hypersensitivity pneumonitis; temporal heterogeneity and fibroblastic foci define UIP; and both entities improve with smoking cessation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RB-ILD is patchy and bronchiolocentric — pigmented macrophages within first- and second-order respiratory bronchioles, with mild peribronchiolar fibrosis — whereas DIP fills the alveolar spaces diffusely; both occur in smokers and can coexist in different parts of the same lung. COMLEX: RB-ILD usually affects current smokers with more than 30 pack-years in the fourth or fifth decade, and stopping smoking usually brings improvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — centriacinar emphysema, the smoking lesion that also centers on the respiratory bronchiole","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q14_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the key distinguishing characteristics and pathologic findings","batch":1,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with scleroderma develops progressive dyspnea. Lung biopsy shows interstitial fibrosis with temporal uniformity, lacking fibroblastic foci or honeycomb changes. High-resolution CT reveals bilateral ground-glass opacities and reticular changes. What pattern of interstitial pneumonia is most commonly associated with systemic sclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP)","B":"Organizing pneumonia (OP)","C":"Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP)","D":"Desquamative interstitial pneumonia (DIP)","E":"Lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia (LIP)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"NSIP is the most common pattern of interstitial lung disease in systemic sclerosis (scleroderma), particularly the fibrosing subtype. NSIP shows temporal homogeneity (uniform age of lesions), lacks the fibroblastic foci characteristic of UIP, and has better prognosis. While UIP can also occur in connective tissue diseases, NSIP is more prevalent in scleroderma and has better response to immunosuppressive therapy. Organizing pneumonia fills airspaces with Masson bodies over normal lung architecture, without the interstitial fibrosis described here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Systemic sclerosis causes diffuse interstitial fibrosis in which the NSIP pattern is more common than UIP; across autoimmune diseases, NSIP, UIP, organizing pneumonia, and bronchiolitis are the usual lung patterns. COMLEX: Systemic sclerosis can also involve the pulmonary vasculature and cause group 1 pulmonary hypertension, so a scleroderma patient with dyspnea needs evaluation for both.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — pulmonary hypertension, where systemic sclerosis is the leading autoimmune cause in WHO group 1","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"NSIP shows temporal homogeneit"}},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q15_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the key distinguishing characteristics and pathologic findings","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man develops dyspnea following viral pneumonia. Lung biopsy reveals polypoid plugs of organizing connective tissue (Masson bodies) filling alveolar ducts and alveoli without architectural distortion. What key feature distinguishes cryptogenic organizing pneumonia from organizing diffuse alveolar damage?","choices":{"A":"COP: same-age plugs, intact lung; DAD: prior hyaline membranes","B":"COP destroys lung architecture; organizing DAD leaves it intact","C":"COP is irreversible; organizing DAD resolves without any treatment","D":"COP affects distal airways; organizing DAD affects proximal bronchi","E":"COP shows eosinophilic infiltration; organizing DAD shows neutrophils"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In cryptogenic organizing pneumonia the polypoid plugs of loose connective tissue (Masson bodies) are all the same age and the underlying lung architecture is normal, with no interstitial fibrosis or honeycombing (Robbins Ch 15). Organizing diffuse alveolar damage is the organizing phase of an acute lung injury whose exudative stage lined the alveoli with hyaline membranes. COP preserves rather than destroys the architecture, it usually responds well to steroids rather than being irreversible, it involves the distal airspaces, and it is not defined by eosinophils.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Organizing pneumonia's plugs are all of one age over preserved architecture; diffuse alveolar damage passes through an exudative stage with hyaline membranes before its organizing stage, in which type II pneumocytes proliferate and granulation tissue forms in alveolar walls and spaces. COMLEX: Some patients with an organizing-pneumonia pattern present with a picture resembling acute ARDS, so the biopsy pattern, not the clinical picture alone, separates the two.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — the hyaline membranes of the acute exudative stage of ARDS","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"COP preserves rather than dest","E":"COP preserves rather than dest"}},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q16_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze clinical scenarios involving restrictive lung disease and apply knowledge of pathology to predict patient presentation and potential outcomes","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with longstanding sarcoidosis develops progressive dyspnea despite corticosteroid therapy. Imaging shows upper lobe predominant fibrosis with traction bronchiectasis and honeycombing. Pulmonary function tests reveal severe restriction with reduced DLCO. What complication has this patient developed?","choices":{"A":"Acute exacerbation with superimposed infection","B":"Transformation to lymphoma","C":"Cor pulmonale from pulmonary hypertension","D":"Stage 4 sarcoidosis with end-stage fibrosis","E":"Hypersensitivity pneumonitis from medication"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient has progressed to Stage 4 sarcoidosis with end-stage pulmonary fibrosis. Stage 4 shows pulmonary fibrosis with architectural distortion, honeycombing, and typically upper lobe predominance (unlike IPF which is lower lobe predominant). It is a minority outcome with a poor prognosis: Robbins notes that 65–70% of patients recover with little or no residual disease, while most of the 10–15% who die succumb to progressive pulmonary fibrosis and cor pulmonale. These patients may develop complications including pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale, and respiratory failure, and may require lung transplantation. The imaging shows chronic upper-lobe fibrosis and honeycombing that has built up over time, which an acute infection or a drug pneumonitis would not produce, and lymphoma is not among the sarcoidosis outcomes Robbins lists.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins' outcome figures for sarcoidosis: 65–70% recover with minimal or no residual disease, 20% keep some permanent loss of lung function or vision, and of the remaining 10–15% most die of progressive pulmonary fibrosis and cor pulmonale. COMLEX: FA lists glucocorticoids for symptomatic sarcoidosis, but established honeycomb fibrosis does not reverse, and cor pulmonale is the cardiopulmonary endpoint to watch for.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — pulmonary hypertension from interstitial lung disease (WHO group 3), the route to cor pulmonale","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"The imaging shows chronic uppe"}},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q17_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze clinical scenarios involving restrictive lung disease and apply knowledge of pathology to predict patient presentation and potential outcomes","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old bird breeder with chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis undergoes lung biopsy showing bridging fibrosis, fibroblastic foci, and honeycomb changes indistinguishable from UIP. Despite complete avoidance of antigen exposure for 6 months, his dyspnea continues to worsen. What explains the progression despite antigen avoidance?","choices":{"A":"Ongoing occult antigen exposure from contaminated home environment","B":"Chronic phase has progressed to irreversible UIP-like fibrosis","C":"Development of superimposed bacterial pneumonia","D":"Malignant transformation to pulmonary lymphoma","E":"Acute exacerbation triggered by viral infection"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis can progress to a UIP-like pattern with irreversible fibrosis that continues despite antigen avoidance. In this chronic fibrotic phase, the original diagnostic features (granulomas, bronchiolocentric inflammation) may be absent, making it difficult to distinguish from IPF. This represents \"burnt-out\" HP where fibroproliferative mechanisms become self-sustaining and antigen-independent, resulting in progressive respiratory decline despite removing the inciting agent. Lymphoma is not among the outcomes Robbins lists for hypersensitivity pneumonitis, and a viral acute exacerbation would cause a sudden worsening, not steady progression with UIP-like scarring.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Some chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis progresses to a UIP-type fibrosis that lacks the characteristic HP features, and Robbins lists chronic HP with connective tissue disease and asbestosis as UIP look-alikes that must be separated from IPF. COMLEX: Robbins warns that pet birds and moldy basements are easily missed unless asked about specifically — early antigen removal is what prevents progression to fibrosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 4 — asbestosis, another exposure-driven fibrosis whose pattern resembles UIP","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Lymphoma is not among the outc"}},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q18_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze clinical scenarios involving restrictive lung disease and apply knowledge of pathology to predict patient presentation and potential outcomes","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with IPF and baseline FVC of 65% predicted develops acute worsening of dyspnea over 1 week without evidence of infection, pulmonary embolism, or heart failure. High-resolution CT shows new bilateral ground-glass opacities superimposed on reticular changes. What is the most likely diagnosis and expected outcome?","choices":{"A":"Superimposed bacterial pneumonia requiring antibiotics","B":"Pulmonary embolism requiring anticoagulation","C":"Acute exacerbation of IPF with high mortality risk","D":"Heart failure exacerbation requiring diuresis","E":"Drug-induced pneumonitis requiring medication cessation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Acute exacerbation of IPF is characterized by acute worsening of dyspnea, new bilateral ground-glass opacities on imaging, and absence of alternative causes (infection, aspiration, PE, heart failure). Pathologically, acute/organizing diffuse alveolar damage is superimposed on chronic UIP changes. Treatment is supportive with high-dose corticosteroids, though efficacy is limited. Pulmonary embolism was excluded in the stem, and no new drug exposure is described to suggest a drug pneumonitis; Robbins notes that IPF can have acute exacerbations with diffuse alveolar damage on top of UIP.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In an acute exacerbation of IPF, diffuse alveolar damage is superimposed on the chronic UIP changes, which matches new bilateral ground-glass opacities and a rapid downhill course. COMLEX: Lung transplantation is the only definitive therapy for IPF; Robbins notes a tyrosine kinase inhibitor and a TGF-β antagonist only slow progression, modestly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — ARDS pathogenesis, the diffuse alveolar damage that is superimposed in an acute exacerbation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Pulmonary embolism was exclude","E":"Pulmonary embolism was exclude"}}],"26":[{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q01_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major pneumoconioses","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old coal miner with 30 years of underground exposure develops progressive dyspnea. High-resolution CT shows multiple intensely blackened scars >1 cm in the upper lobes with central necrosis. Microscopic examination reveals dense collagen and pigment. What pathologic entity has developed?","choices":{"A":"Simple coal workers' pneumoconiosis with coal macules","B":"Silicosis with eggshell calcification of hilar nodes","C":"Asbestosis with pleural plaques","D":"Complicated CWP (progressive massive fibrosis)","E":"Caplan syndrome with rheumatoid nodules"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Progressive massive fibrosis (complicated CWP) is characterized by intensely blackened scars ≥1 cm in greatest diameter, typically in the upper lobes. Microscopically, the lesions consist of dense collagen and pigment, with central necrosis due to local ischemia. This develops on a background of simple CWP and requires many years to develop. It leads to significant pulmonary dysfunction, pulmonary hypertension, and cor pulmonale. Eggshell calcification of hilar nodes marks silicosis, pleural plaques mark asbestos exposure, and Caplan syndrome needs rheumatoid arthritis together with the pneumoconiosis, none of which is described here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Complicated CWP (progressive massive fibrosis) develops on a background of simple CWP over many years: multiple intensely blackened scars of 1 cm or more (up to 10 cm) made of dense collagen and pigment, often necrotic centrally from local ischemia. COMLEX: It occurs in fewer than 10% of coal workers, brings pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale, and can keep worsening even after dust exposure stops.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — obstructive lung disease, since coal workers also develop emphysema and chronic bronchitis independent of smoking","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q02_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major pneumoconioses","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old sandblaster presents with progressive dyspnea after 15 years of occupational exposure. Lung biopsy reveals nodules with whorled collagen fibers centrally and dust-laden macrophages peripherally. Polarized microscopy shows weakly birefringent particles. What pathogenic mechanism primarily drives fibrosis in this disease?","choices":{"A":"Coating of particles with iron-containing proteinaceous material","B":"Adsorption of tobacco carcinogens onto mineral fibers","C":"Inflammasome activation by crystals, releasing IL-1 and IL-18","D":"Free radical generation by iron molecules within mineral fibers","E":"Type III hypersensitivity reaction to inhaled organic antigens"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient has silicosis from sandblasting exposure. Phagocytosis of inhaled crystalline silica by macrophages activates the inflammasome and stimulates the release of IL-1 and IL-18, leading to recruitment of inflammatory cells and activation of fibroblasts with collagen deposition. The whorled collagen pattern with peripheral dust-laden macrophages is characteristic of silicotic nodules. Crystalline forms (quartz, cristobalite, tridymite) are most fibrogenic. An iron-containing protein coat is what forms asbestos bodies, and adsorbed tobacco carcinogens and iron-catalyzed free radicals are Robbins' explanations for the cancer risk of asbestos fibers, not for silica-driven fibrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Macrophages that phagocytose crystalline silica (quartz most often) activate the inflammasome and release IL-1 and IL-18, which recruit inflammatory cells and activate interstitial fibroblasts to lay down collagen; crystalline forms are far more fibrogenic than amorphous silica. COMLEX: Silicosis is the most prevalent chronic occupational disease worldwide, so ask about sandblasting, stone cutting, foundry work, and concrete demolition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 7 — how pulmonary fibrosis reduces the efficiency of gas diffusion","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"An iron-containing protein coa"}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q03_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major pneumoconioses","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old former shipyard worker develops progressive dyspnea. Lung biopsy shows diffuse interstitial fibrosis beginning in the lower lobes and subpleural regions, with honeycombing and golden-brown fusiform rods with translucent centers. What is the pathognomonic finding that distinguishes this entity from other causes of diffuse interstitial fibrosis?","choices":{"A":"Asbestos bodies (iron-coated asbestos fibers)","B":"Eggshell calcification of hilar lymph nodes","C":"Coal macules adjacent to respiratory bronchioles","D":"Noncaseating granulomas with Schaumann bodies","E":"Intraalveolar Masson bodies of organizing tissue"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Asbestosis is distinguished from other causes of diffuse interstitial fibrosis by the presence of asbestos bodies—golden-brown, fusiform or beaded rods with translucent centers consisting of asbestos fibers coated with iron-containing proteinaceous material. Asbestosis begins in lower lobes and subpleurally (unlike CWP and silicosis which affect upper lobes), progressing to honeycombing. The fibrosis pattern resembles UIP with fibroblastic foci. Eggshell calcification of hilar nodes belongs to silicosis, and coal macules beside respiratory bronchioles belong to coal workers' pneumoconiosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Asbestosis is diffuse interstitial fibrosis distinguished from other causes only by asbestos bodies — golden-brown, fusiform or beaded rods with translucent centers, asbestos fibers coated with iron-containing protein, the iron probably from macrophage ferritin; other particles coated this way are called ferruginous bodies. COMLEX: Symptoms rarely appear before 10 years and are most common 20–30 years after first exposure, so take an occupational history reaching back decades.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — IPF and its UIP pattern, the diffuse fibrosis that asbestosis resembles","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q04_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate among the major pneumoconioses based on patient history, radiographic findings, and gross/microscopic morphology","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old stone cutter presents with chronic cough and weight loss. Chest imaging reveals upper lobe nodular opacities, and hilar lymph nodes show thin sheets of peripheral calcification surrounding non-calcified centers. Sputum is positive for acid-fast bacilli. What radiographic finding is characteristic of this occupational pneumoconiosis?","choices":{"A":"Pleural plaques on diaphragmatic surfaces","B":"Progressive massive fibrosis with blackened scars","C":"Bilateral ground-glass opacities in lower lobes","D":"Honeycombing with lower lobe predominance","E":"Eggshell calcification of hilar lymph nodes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient has silicosis with superimposed tuberculosis. Eggshell calcification (calcium surrounding a zone lacking calcification) in hilar lymph nodes is characteristic of silicosis. Silicosis significantly increases susceptibility to tuberculosis due to impaired macrophage function. The upper lobe predominance and nodular pattern are typical of silicosis, unlike asbestosis which affects lower lobes. Pleural plaques mark asbestos exposure, and intensely blackened scars are the coal-pigmented lesions of complicated coal workers' pneumoconiosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Silicosis can lay thin sheets of calcium in hilar nodes that appear radiographically as eggshell calcification (calcium surrounding an uncalcified zone), with nodules concentrated in the upper lung zones. COMLEX: Crystalline silica inhibits macrophage killing of phagocytosed mycobacteria, so silicosis raises tuberculosis risk, and it also doubles lung-cancer risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 49 → Obj 3 — the host risk factors that predispose to active TB","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q05_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate among the major pneumoconioses based on patient history, radiographic findings, and gross/microscopic morphology","batch":1,"stem":"A 60-year-old insulation worker presents with progressive dyspnea. CT shows bilateral reticular opacities with lower lobe predominance and well-circumscribed calcified plaques on the anterior parietal pleura and diaphragmatic domes. What feature best distinguishes this pneumoconiosis from coal workers' pneumoconiosis?","choices":{"A":"Upper lobe predominance vs lower lobe involvement","B":"Progressive massive fibrosis vs pleural plaques","C":"Presence of coal macules vs asbestos bodies","D":"Lower lobe/subpleural vs upper lobe predominance","E":"Increased tuberculosis risk vs no TB predisposition"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Asbestosis characteristically begins in the lower lobes and subpleurally, while coal workers' pneumoconiosis predominantly affects the upper lobes and upper zones of lower lobes. Pleural plaques (well-circumscribed dense collagen, often calcified) are the most common manifestation of asbestos exposure, developing on anterior and posterolateral parietal pleura and diaphragmatic domes. Asbestosis shows honeycombing in advanced cases, while CWP develops progressive massive fibrosis. Tuberculosis risk does not separate the two this way: the added TB susceptibility belongs to silicosis, and Robbins finds no convincing evidence that coal workers' pneumoconiosis raises it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Asbestos 'is from the roof but affects the base' (FA): asbestosis begins in the lower lobes and subpleurally, whereas coal workers' pneumoconiosis and silicosis favor the upper lobes; pleural plaques of dense, often calcified collagen are the commonest manifestation of asbestos exposure. COMLEX: Plaques mark exposure rather than disease severity — their size and number do not correlate with exposure level or time since exposure, and they are usually asymptomatic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 1 — pleural effusions, including the recurrent effusions that asbestos exposure causes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q06_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate among the major pneumoconioses based on patient history, radiographic findings, and gross/microscopic morphology","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old coal miner presents with dyspnea. Imaging shows tiny discrete nodules in upper lobes. Biopsy reveals 1-2 mm coal macules consisting of carbon-laden macrophages with delicate collagen networks, located adjacent to respiratory bronchioles with associated centrilobular emphysema. Which statement best characterizes this stage of disease?","choices":{"A":"This represents simple CWP with minimal pulmonary dysfunction","B":"This indicates progressive massive fibrosis requiring lung transplantation","C":"This shows acute silicosis with alveolar proteinosis pattern","D":"This demonstrates asbestosis with fibroblastic foci","E":"This suggests Caplan syndrome with rheumatoid nodules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Simple coal workers' pneumoconiosis is characterized by coal macules (1-2 mm) and coal nodules located adjacent to respiratory bronchioles in the upper lobes. Coal macules contain carbon-laden macrophages; nodules also have delicate collagen networks. Dilation of adjacent alveoli may cause centrilobular emphysema. Simple CWP causes little pulmonary dysfunction and is usually benign. Progressive massive fibrosis (complicated CWP) requires lesions ≥1 cm. Asbestosis is a lower-lobe interstitial fibrosis with asbestos bodies, not upper-lobe coal macules.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Simple CWP consists of coal macules (1–2 mm, carbon-laden macrophages) and slightly larger nodules with a delicate collagen network, placed next to respiratory bronchioles and heaviest in the upper lobes and upper zones of the lower lobes, sometimes with centrilobular emphysema; it causes little or no pulmonary dysfunction. COMLEX: Anthracosis — the same carbon pigment without disease — is common in urban dwellers and smokers, so pigment alone is not pneumoconiosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — centrilobular (centriacinar) emphysema, the lesion that can form around coal macules","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Asbestosis is a lower-lobe int"}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q07_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphology of Langerhans cell histiocytosis and pulmonary alveolar proteinosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old male smoker presents with dyspnea and pneumothorax. CT shows characteristic cystic and nodular abnormalities with upper lobe predominance. Lung biopsy reveals focal collections of cells with grooved, coffee bean-shaped nuclei accompanied by eosinophils, with irregular cystic spaces from airway destruction. Molecular testing shows BRAF V600E mutation. What does this mutation indicate about the nature of this disease?","choices":{"A":"It represents a reactive inflammatory process responsive to smoking cessation","B":"It indicates impaired GM-CSF signaling requiring replacement therapy","C":"It marks the proliferation as a neoplasm rather than reactive process","D":"It suggests autoimmune pathogenesis with anti-GM-CSF antibodies","E":"It indicates hereditary surfactant dysfunction disorder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis shows focal collections of Langerhans cells (grooved, coffee bean-shaped nuclei) with eosinophils. While >90% of patients are young adult smokers and some improve with smoking cessation (suggesting reactive process), the presence of activating BRAF mutations marks these proliferations as neoplasms. This is a feature shared with Langerhans cell histiocytosis in other tissues. Despite the neoplastic nature, prognosis is usually excellent. Impaired GM-CSF signaling causes pulmonary alveolar proteinosis, and hereditary surfactant dysfunction comes from genes such as ABCA3; neither involves BRAF in Langerhans cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis forms focal collections of Langerhans cells, often with eosinophils, that scar into irregular cysts; activating BRAF mutations, shared with Langerhans cell histiocytosis in other tissues, mark these proliferations as neoplasms. COMLEX: More than 90% of patients are relatively young adult smokers or ex-smokers with cystic and nodular CT changes; the prognosis is usually excellent, though some patients progress to need lung transplantation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 9 — lymphangioleiomyomatosis (LAM), the other cystic lung disease among the miscellaneous lung tumors","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"While >90% of patients are you","D":"Impaired GM-CSF signaling caus"}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q08_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphology of Langerhans cell histiocytosis and pulmonary alveolar proteinosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old man presents with progressive dyspnea and cough productive of gelatinous material. High-resolution CT shows bilateral patchy asymmetric opacifications. Lung biopsy reveals intraalveolar pink homogeneous PAS-positive precipitates containing cholesterol clefts and surfactant proteins, with normal alveolar walls. Serum testing reveals anti-GM-CSF autoantibodies. What is the underlying pathophysiologic defect?","choices":{"A":"Hereditary mutations in ABCA3 lipid transporter genes","B":"Secondary impairment from hematologic malignancy","C":"Congenital GM-CSF or GM-CSF receptor mutations","D":"Autoantibody-mediated neutralization of GM-CSF function","E":"BRAF-mutated Langerhans cell proliferation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient has autoimmune (formerly \"acquired\") pulmonary alveolar proteinosis, which represents 90% of all PAP cases. It is caused by autoantibodies that bind and neutralize GM-CSF function. Loss of GM-CSF signaling blocks terminal differentiation of alveolar macrophages, impairing their ability to catabolize surfactant. This leads to intraalveolar accumulation of surfactant. PAP shows PAS-positive material with cholesterol clefts, distinguishing it from other causes of consolidation. ABCA3 mutations cause surfactant dysfunction disorders, mutations in GM-CSF or its receptor cause the very rare hereditary PAP of neonates, and BRAF-mutated Langerhans cells define pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis; the anti-GM-CSF antibodies here point to autoimmune PAP.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Autoimmune PAP (90% of PAP, mainly adults, no familial tendency) is caused by autoantibodies that neutralize GM-CSF; the alveoli fill with pink, homogeneous, PAS-positive precipitate containing cholesterol clefts and surfactant proteins while the alveolar walls stay normal. COMLEX: Adults cough up gelatinous chunks and get secondary infections; whole-lung lavage is the standard of care, and GM-CSF therapy helps more than half of autoimmune cases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — surfactant's composition and function, the material that accumulates when clearance fails","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"ABCA3 mutations cause surfacta"}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q09_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphology of Langerhans cell histiocytosis and pulmonary alveolar proteinosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman with pulmonary alveolar proteinosis undergoes whole-lung lavage with clinical improvement. What distinguishes the ultrastructural findings in autoimmune PAP from surfactant dysfunction disorders on electron microscopy?","choices":{"A":"Absence of cholesterol clefts in alveolar spaces","B":"Normal surfactant lamellae in type II pneumocytes","C":"Presence of lamellar bodies with electron-dense cores","D":"Accumulation of Langerhans cells with Birbeck granules","E":"Presence of asbestos bodies with iron coating"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In autoimmune PAP, ultrastructurally the surfactant lamellae in type II pneumocytes are NORMAL, as the defect is in macrophage clearance of surfactant (due to impaired GM-CSF signaling), not in surfactant production. In contrast, surfactant dysfunction disorders (e.g., ABCA3 mutations) show abnormal type II pneumocytes with small surfactant lamellae with electron-dense cores. Both show intraalveolar PAS-positive material, but the pneumocyte ultrastructure differs. Cholesterol clefts are present in the alveolar precipitate of autoimmune PAP, Birbeck granules mark Langerhans cells, and iron-coated asbestos bodies mark asbestosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ultrastructure separates the two: in PAP the surfactant lamellae inside type II pneumocytes are normal because the defect lies in clearance, whereas surfactant dysfunction disorders show abnormal lamellar bodies. COMLEX: Whole-lung lavage benefits PAP regardless of the underlying defect, consistent with surfactant that has accumulated rather than surfactant that is abnormal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — type II pneumocytes, whose lamellar bodies store and secrete surfactant","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q10_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major pneumoconioses","batch":1,"stem":"A 50-year-old demolition worker with 20 years of exposure to concrete dust presents with dyspnea. Chest CT reveals upper lobe nodular opacities. What particle size range is most dangerous for development of pneumoconiosis because these particles can reach terminal airways and deposit in their linings?","choices":{"A":"1 to 5 micrometers in diameter","B":"10 to 20 micrometers in diameter","C":"Less than 0.5 micrometers in diameter","D":"Greater than 25 micrometers in diameter","E":"5 to 10 micrometers in diameter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Particles from 1 to 5 µm in diameter are most dangerous because they can reach terminal small airways and air sacs and deposit in their linings. Larger particles are filtered in upper airways, while smaller particles may be exhaled. This size range allows deep penetration into the lung parenchyma. The particle size, along with dust concentration, duration of exposure, and clearance effectiveness, determines dust retention and disease development.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Particles 1–5 µm across are the most dangerous because they reach the terminal small airways and air sacs and deposit in their linings; dust retention also depends on dust concentration, exposure duration, and how well clearance works. COMLEX: Anything that impairs mucociliary clearance — notably cigarette smoking — increases dust accumulation, and smoking worsens the injury from all inhaled mineral dusts.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — the bronchioles, where conducting airways end and small inhaled particles deposit","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Larger particles are filtered ","C":"Larger particles are filtered ","D":"Larger particles are filtered ","E":"Larger particles are filtered "}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q11_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major pneumoconioses","batch":1,"stem":"A 56-year-old former asbestos insulation installer develops lung adenocarcinoma. He has a 40 pack-year smoking history. What magnitude of increased lung cancer risk would be expected from the combination of asbestos exposure and smoking compared to asbestos exposure alone?","choices":{"A":"Two-fold increase with combined exposure","B":"Same risk as asbestos alone","C":"Ten-fold increase with combined exposure","D":"55-fold increase vs 5-fold with asbestos alone","E":"No synergistic effect between asbestos and smoking"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Asbestos and tobacco smoking show remarkable synergy in lung carcinoma development. Studies show asbestos exposure alone increases lung cancer risk 5-fold, while the combination of asbestos and smoking increases risk 55-fold. This synergy occurs because tobacco carcinogens are adsorbed onto asbestos fibers, and smoking impairs mucociliary clearance of fibers. Asbestos acts as both tumor initiator and promoter through free radical generation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Asbestos and smoking are synergistic: one study of asbestos workers found a 5-fold rise in lung carcinoma with asbestos alone and a 55-fold rise with both, because tobacco carcinogens adsorb onto the fibers and smoking slows fiber clearance; asbestos acts as both tumor initiator and promoter. COMLEX: FA notes bronchogenic carcinoma is more common than mesothelioma after asbestos exposure, and that smoking is not a risk factor for mesothelioma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 2 — tobacco, industrial hazards, and air pollution as lung cancer risk factors","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q12_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major pneumoconioses","batch":1,"stem":"A 48-year-old jeweler using chalk molds develops acute onset dyspnea over 6 months with heavy silica exposure. Lung biopsy shows abundant lipoproteinaceous material filling alveoli. What pathologic entity has developed from this intense short-term silica exposure?","choices":{"A":"Chronic silicosis with whorled collagen nodules","B":"Acute silicosis with alveolar proteinosis-like pattern","C":"Progressive massive fibrosis with necrotic centers","D":"Simple coal workers' pneumoconiosis with macules","E":"Asbestosis with honeycombing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acute silicosis results from heavy silica exposure over months to a few years (unlike chronic silicosis requiring decades). It is characterized by accumulation of abundant lipoproteinaceous material within alveoli, morphologically identical to pulmonary alveolar proteinosis. This differs from chronic silicosis, which presents as slowly progressing nodular fibrosing pneumoconiosis with whorled collagen nodules. Acute silicosis has poor prognosis. Asbestosis is a slowly developing lower-lobe fibrosis from asbestos, not the result of months of heavy silica exposure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Heavy silica exposure over months to a few years can cause acute silicosis — alveoli filled with abundant lipoproteinaceous material, morphologically identical to alveolar proteinosis — instead of the nodular fibrosis that takes decades. COMLEX: Silicosis onset may be insidious (10–30 years, most common), accelerated (within 10 years), or rapid (weeks to months after intense exposure to fine silica dust), so exposure intensity shapes the presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 6 — pulmonary alveolar proteinosis, where Robbins lists acute silicosis among the secondary causes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q13_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate among the major pneumoconioses based on patient history, radiographic findings, and gross/microscopic morphology","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist examines lung tissue from three different occupational disease cases. Case A shows serpentine chrysotile fibers; Case B shows straight amphibole fibers; Case C shows coal dust. Which case is most likely to develop malignant mesothelioma?","choices":{"A":"Case A (chrysotile) due to greater industrial use","B":"Case B (amphibole) due to deeper lung penetration","C":"Case C (coal) due to contaminating silica content","D":"All three have equal mesothelioma risk","E":"None develop mesothelioma; only lung carcinoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amphibole asbestos fibers (straight, stiff) are more pathogenic than serpentine chrysotile, particularly for mesothelioma induction. Amphiboles align with airstreams and penetrate deeper into lungs, reaching the interstitium and mesothelial cells of the pleura. They are also less soluble than chrysotiles. Chrysotiles (flexible, curled) impact in upper airways and are removed by mucociliary elevator. Coal dust does not cause mesothelioma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Serpentine chrysotile (90% of industrial asbestos) is curled and flexible, impacts in the upper airways, is cleared by the mucociliary elevator, and leaches out because it is more soluble; straight, stiff amphiboles align with the airstream, reach the distal lung near the pleura, and are more pathogenic, especially for mesothelioma. COMLEX: Family members of asbestos workers have higher rates of lung carcinoma and mesothelioma, so household exposure belongs in the history.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 3 — malignant mesothelioma of the pleura","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Chrysotiles (flexible, curled)","C":"Coal dust does not cause mesot"}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q14_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate among the major pneumoconioses based on patient history, radiographic findings, and gross/microscopic morphology","batch":1,"stem":"A 54-year-old welder presents for occupational health screening. Chest X-ray shows increased radiodensity. Lung biopsy shows iron oxide particles. What is this condition called?","choices":{"A":"Anthracosis from urban air pollution","B":"Berylliosis with granuloma formation","C":"Baritosis from barium sulfate exposure","D":"Stannosis from tin oxide exposure","E":"Siderosis from iron oxide welding fumes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Robbins Table 15.6 (course Pulm SDL 25–27 PDF p49) lists iron oxide as the agent of siderosis, with welding as the exposure, so iron oxide particles in a welder point to siderosis. The same table lists barium sulfate as the cause of baritosis and tin oxide as the cause of stannosis, both from mining, which does not match iron oxide from welding fumes. Anthracosis is carbon pigment from coal dust, urban air, or smoking, not iron oxide, and beryllium causes acute berylliosis or beryllium granulomatosis rather than a harmless density.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Match the dust to the job — Robbins' mineral-dust table pairs iron oxide with siderosis (welding), barium sulfate with baritosis (mining), and tin oxide with stannosis (mining); Robbins describes only coal, silica, and asbestos in detail as the dusts that cause pulmonary fibrosis. COMLEX: Smoking worsens the effects of all inhaled mineral dusts, so a welder who smokes still needs cessation counseling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 1 — alveolar macrophages, the first-line defense that engulfs inhaled particles","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"The same table lists barium su"}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q15_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate among the major pneumoconioses based on patient history, radiographic findings, and gross/microscopic morphology","batch":1,"stem":"A 65-year-old former miner dies from respiratory failure. Autopsy reveals lungs with upper lobe predominance of scarring. Three pneumoconioses are being considered based on occupational history. Which microscopic feature would definitively distinguish silicosis from the others?","choices":{"A":"Ferruginous bodies with iron-protein coating on the fibers","B":"Coal macules with carbon-laden macrophages","C":"Central necrosis within massively fibrotic lesions","D":"Whorled collagen nodules, weakly birefringent particles","E":"Honeycombing with lower lobe predominance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Silicotic nodules show characteristic whorled collagen fibers with peripheral dust-laden macrophages. Polarized microscopy reveals weakly birefringent silicate particles, which is diagnostic. Coal workers' pneumoconiosis shows coal macules with carbon pigment (non-birefringent), while asbestosis shows asbestos bodies (ferruginous bodies) with lower lobe predominance. Central necrosis can occur in silicosis (from TB or ischemia) and in progressive massive fibrosis from coal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The silicotic nodule — a central area of whorled collagen with a peripheral zone of dust-laden macrophages — shows weakly birefringent silicate particles under polarized light; nodules may soften and cavitate from superimposed TB or ischemia. COMLEX: FA's rule — silica, coal, and beryllium come from the base but affect the roof — puts all three in the upper lobes, so distribution alone cannot separate them.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 49 → Obj 4 — reactivation TB, whose cavitation can complicate silicotic nodules","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Coal workers' pneumoconiosis s"}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q16_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphology of Langerhans cell histiocytosis and pulmonary alveolar proteinosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old male smoker with pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis achieves complete remission after smoking cessation alone. What does this clinical response suggest about the pathogenesis in this particular case?","choices":{"A":"Presence of BRAF mutation indicating neoplastic process","B":"Reactive inflammation, not neoplastic proliferation","C":"Autoimmune pathogenesis requiring immunosuppression","D":"Defective GM-CSF signaling requiring replacement therapy","E":"Hereditary genetic mutation in surfactant proteins"},"correct":"B","explanation":"While some cases of pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis have BRAF mutations (marking them as neoplasms), >90% of patients are young adult smokers, and about half improve after smoking cessation alone. This suggests that in many cases, the lesions represent a reactive inflammatory process rather than true neoplasia. The disease shows a spectrum from reactive to neoplastic, with smoking cessation responsive cases likely representing the reactive end. Autoimmune mechanisms and defective GM-CSF signaling belong to pulmonary alveolar proteinosis, not to Langerhans cell histiocytosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Among smokers with pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis, about half improve after stopping smoking, which Robbins reads as evidence that some lesions are a reactive inflammatory process, while BRAF-mutated cases are neoplasms. COMLEX: More than 90% of patients are young adult smokers or ex-smokers, so cessation is the first step; the disease usually has an excellent prognosis but progresses in some patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — DIP and the other smoking-related interstitial diseases that also improve after cessation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q17_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphology of Langerhans cell histiocytosis and pulmonary alveolar proteinosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 3-month-old infant presents with rapidly progressive respiratory failure. Genetic testing reveals autosomal recessive mutations in ABCA3. Electron microscopy of lung tissue would show which characteristic ultrastructural finding distinguishing this from autoimmune pulmonary alveolar proteinosis?","choices":{"A":"Small lamellae with dense cores in type II cells","B":"Normal surfactant lamellae in type II pneumocytes","C":"Intraalveolar PAS-positive proteinaceous material","D":"Langerhans cells with Birbeck granules (tennis rackets)","E":"Cholesterol clefts within alveolar spaces"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Surfactant dysfunction disorders (like ABCA3 mutations) show ABNORMAL type II pneumocytes with small surfactant lamellae containing electron-dense cores on electron microscopy. This contrasts with autoimmune PAP, where type II pneumocytes have NORMAL surfactant lamellae—the defect is in macrophage clearance, not production. ABCA3 encodes a transmembrane lipid transporter regulating surfactant homeostasis. ABCA3 mutations typically present in early infancy with rapidly progressive respiratory failure. Intraalveolar PAS-positive material and cholesterol clefts are features of alveolar proteinosis in general, so they do not separate ABCA3 disease from the autoimmune form, and Birbeck granules mark Langerhans cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ABCA3, a transmembrane lipid transporter that regulates surfactant homeostasis, is the gene most often mutated in surfactant dysfunction disorders; the autosomal recessive disease usually brings rapidly progressive respiratory failure in the first months of life, and small lamellar bodies with electron-dense cores are diagnostic. COMLEX: Surfactant protein B deficiency (also recessive) affects full-term newborns and is fatal by 3–6 months without lung transplantation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — the key events and timing of lung maturation, including surfactant production","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q18_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphology of Langerhans cell histiocytosis and pulmonary alveolar proteinosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old man with autoimmune pulmonary alveolar proteinosis undergoes GM-CSF replacement therapy with partial response. What is the primary mechanism by which GM-CSF deficiency leads to surfactant accumulation?","choices":{"A":"Impaired surfactant production by immature type II pneumocytes","B":"Increased surfactant secretion from lamellar bodies","C":"Macrophages fail to mature and cannot catabolize surfactant","D":"Direct cytotoxicity to type II pneumocytes","E":"Activation of Langerhans cell proliferation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In autoimmune PAP, anti-GM-CSF autoantibodies neutralize GM-CSF function. Loss of GM-CSF signaling blocks the terminal differentiation of alveolar macrophages, impairing their ability to catabolize (break down) surfactant. This leads to accumulation of surfactant in alveolar spaces. The defect is in clearance, not production—type II pneumocytes produce normal surfactant. GM-CSF knockout mice develop PAP and are \"cured\" by GM-CSF treatment (Robbins Ch 15), the rationale for GM-CSF therapy that restores macrophage function. Langerhans cell proliferation is the lesion of pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis and has nothing to do with GM-CSF loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loss of GM-CSF signaling blocks the terminal differentiation of alveolar macrophages, so they cannot catabolize surfactant; GM-CSF knockout mice develop PAP and are cured by GM-CSF treatment. COMLEX: Secondary PAP — with hematopoietic disorders, malignancies, or immunodeficiency — is thought to impair the same GM-CSF–dependent macrophage maturation, so therapy aimed at the underlying disorder may help.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 1 — alveolar macrophages in pulmonary host defense, whose failure explains PAP's secondary infections","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The defect is in clearance, no"}}],"27":[{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q01_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the major categories of pulmonary edema and list the underlying clinical etiologies associated with each","batch":1,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with a history of myocardial infarction presents with orthopnea and bilateral lower lobe crackles. Chest X-ray shows bilateral basal opacities. Lung biopsy reveals engorged alveolar capillaries with intraalveolar transudate appearing as finely granular pale pink material. What is the primary underlying mechanism of this pulmonary edema?","choices":{"A":"Increased alveolar capillary permeability from endothelial injury","B":"Decreased plasma oncotic pressure from hypoalbuminemia","C":"Increased hydrostatic pressure from left-sided heart failure","D":"Lymphatic obstruction from tumor compression","E":"Direct alveolar epithelial injury from inhaled toxins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient has hemodynamic (cardiogenic) pulmonary edema caused by increased hydrostatic pressure from left-sided congestive heart failure following MI. Edema accumulates initially in basal regions of lower lobes where hydrostatic pressure is greatest (dependent edema). Histologically, alveolar capillaries are engorged with intraalveolar transudate (protein-poor fluid) appearing as finely granular pale pink material. This contrasts with noncardiogenic edema from alveolar injury, which produces protein-rich inflammatory exudate. Lymphatic obstruction is a rare cause of edema, and nothing here suggests a tumor; the post-infarction heart failure and the transudate point to raised hydrostatic pressure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hemodynamic pulmonary edema from raised hydrostatic (pulmonary venous) pressure, most commonly left-sided heart failure, accumulates first in the basal regions of the lower lobes, where hydrostatic pressure is greatest (dependent edema). COMLEX: Volume overload and pulmonary vein obstruction raise hydrostatic pressure the same way, and Robbins stresses that therapy and outcome depend on the underlying cause.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 10 — the sequence of changes as left atrial pressure rises toward pulmonary edema","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"This contrasts with noncardiog","E":"This contrasts with noncardiog"}},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q02_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the major categories of pulmonary edema and list the underlying clinical etiologies associated with each","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with nephrotic syndrome and serum albumin of 2.0 g/dL develops progressive dyspnea. Imaging shows bilateral pulmonary infiltrates. What mechanism accounts for pulmonary edema formation in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Increased pulmonary venous pressure from mitral stenosis","B":"Neutrophil-mediated endothelial injury and increased permeability","C":"Direct pneumocyte injury from inhaled irritants","D":"Lymphatic obstruction reducing fluid clearance","E":"Decreased plasma oncotic pressure from hypoalbuminemia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient has hemodynamic pulmonary edema from decreased oncotic pressure due to severe hypoalbuminemia (nephrotic syndrome). With low plasma oncotic pressure, the balance of Starling forces favors fluid movement from capillaries into interstitium and alveoli. Other causes of decreased oncotic pressure include liver disease and protein-losing enteropathies. This is less common than increased hydrostatic pressure but still produces cardiogenic-type edema with transudate rather than inflammatory exudate. Neutrophil-mediated endothelial injury and direct pneumocyte injury from irritants belong to edema from alveolar wall injury, which produces an inflammatory exudate rather than the transudate of hypoalbuminemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Decreased plasma oncotic pressure — hypoalbuminemia from nephrotic syndrome, liver disease, or protein-losing enteropathy — is a less common hemodynamic cause of pulmonary edema than raised hydrostatic pressure (Robbins Table 15.1). COMLEX: Whatever the mechanism, pulmonary congestion and edema produce heavy, wet lungs, and treatment targets the underlying cause — here, the protein loss.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 13 — the Starling forces of pulmonary interstitial fluid that low oncotic pressure tips toward edema","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q03_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the major categories of pulmonary edema and list the underlying clinical etiologies associated with each","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man is hospitalized with severe pancreatitis and develops acute respiratory failure. Chest imaging shows bilateral pulmonary infiltrates. Arterial blood gas reveals severe hypoxemia despite supplemental oxygen. What category of pulmonary edema has this patient developed?","choices":{"A":"Hemodynamic edema from left ventricular failure","B":"Noncardiogenic edema from alveolar septal injury","C":"Hemodynamic edema from decreased oncotic pressure","D":"Lymphatic obstruction from mediastinal fibrosis","E":"Hemodynamic edema from pulmonary vein obstruction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient has developed noncardiogenic pulmonary edema from alveolar septal injury in the setting of severe pancreatitis, which triggers systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS). Circulating inflammatory mediators cause primary injury to vascular endothelium or alveolar epithelial cells, producing inflammatory exudate that leaks into interstitium and alveoli. This is a key feature of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS). The bilateral infiltrates and severe hypoxemia refractory to oxygen support the diagnosis of ARDS. Lymphatic obstruction is a rare hemodynamic cause and nothing here involves the mediastinum, whereas pancreatitis is on Robbins' list of indirect injuries that cause alveolar damage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Noncardiogenic edema follows injury to the alveolar septa — primary endothelial injury, or epithelial damage with secondary microvascular injury — producing an inflammatory exudate; systemic inflammatory response syndrome from sepsis, burns, pancreatitis, or extensive trauma injures the lung indirectly. COMLEX: Abrupt hypoxemia with bilateral pulmonary edema and no cardiac failure defines acute lung injury, and ARDS is its severe clinical form.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 13 — physiologic shunt, the perfused but unventilated alveoli behind refractory hypoxemia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q04_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the detailed pathogenesis of ARDS, focusing on the cascade of inflammation, endothelial activation, neutrophil involvement, and formation of intraalveolar exudate","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with gram-negative sepsis develops ARDS. What is the earliest event in the pathogenesis that sets in motion the vicious cycle of increasing inflammation and pulmonary damage?","choices":{"A":"Endothelial activation by circulating inflammatory mediators or pneumocyte injury","B":"Neutrophil degranulation releasing proteases and reactive oxygen species","C":"Accumulation of protein-rich edema fluid in alveolar spaces","D":"Formation of hyaline membranes from inspissated protein and cellular debris","E":"Type II pneumocyte necrosis with surfactant abnormalities"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Endothelial activation is an important early event initiating ARDS pathogenesis. In sepsis, circulating inflammatory mediators (e.g., TNF, cytokines) activate pulmonary endothelium directly. Alternatively, pneumocyte injury is sensed by resident alveolar macrophages that secrete TNF and other mediators acting on neighboring endothelium. Activated endothelial cells express increased adhesion molecules, procoagulant proteins, and chemokines, setting in motion the inflammatory cascade. Neutrophil recruitment, edema accumulation, and hyaline membrane formation are subsequent events.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ALI/ARDS starts with injury to pneumocytes and pulmonary endothelium; endothelial activation — set off directly by circulating mediators in sepsis or severe tissue injury, or indirectly by signals from resident alveolar macrophages after pneumocyte injury — is the key early event in the vicious cycle. COMLEX: Sepsis, diffuse pulmonary infection, gastric aspiration, and trauma head Robbins' list of ARDS triggers, and several are often present at once.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 9 — viral bronchiolitis and laryngotracheobronchitis, infections that can progress to ARDS","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Neutrophil recruitment, edema "}},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q05_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the detailed pathogenesis of ARDS, focusing on the cascade of inflammation, endothelial activation, neutrophil involvement, and formation of intraalveolar exudate","batch":1,"stem":"During ARDS pathogenesis, activated neutrophils migrate into alveoli and release multiple injurious mediators. What mechanism do neutrophils use that directly contributes to lung damage beyond traditional degranulation?","choices":{"A":"Production of surfactant to stabilize alveoli","B":"Release of neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs)","C":"Phagocytosis of bacterial pathogens and debris","D":"Secretion of growth factors promoting fibroblast proliferation","E":"Expression of adhesion molecules for endothelial binding"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Neutrophils adhering to activated endothelium migrate into interstitium and alveoli, where they degranulate releasing proteases, reactive oxygen species (ROS), and cytokines. Experimental evidence shows neutrophils also release neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs) that contribute directly to lung damage. NETs consist of chromatin decorated with antimicrobial proteins that can trap and kill pathogens but also injure host tissue. These neutrophil-mediated injuries create a feedback loop of inflammation and endothelial damage central to ARDS. Growth factors that drive fibroblast growth, such as TGF-β and PDGF, come mainly from macrophages in the later fibroproliferative phase, not from neutrophils injuring the lung.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neutrophils that adhere to activated endothelium migrate into the interstitium and alveoli and release proteases, reactive oxygen species, and cytokines; experimental evidence adds neutrophil extracellular traps as a direct cause of lung damage, closing the feedback loop of inflammation and endothelial injury. COMLEX: Robbins notes ARDS is more common and carries a worse prognosis in people with alcohol use disorder and in smokers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 5 — the neutrophil-rich exudate of the hepatization stages of lobar pneumonia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q06_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the detailed pathogenesis of ARDS, focusing on the cascade of inflammation, endothelial activation, neutrophil involvement, and formation of intraalveolar exudate","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with ARDS develops characteristic hyaline membranes lining the alveoli. What is the primary composition and pathophysiologic mechanism leading to hyaline membrane formation?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophil extracellular traps with chromatin and antimicrobial proteins","B":"Mucin secreted by hyperplastic goblet cells","C":"Fibrin-rich edema fluid mixed with debris of necrotic alveolar epithelial cells","D":"Proliferating type II pneumocytes attempting epithelial regeneration","E":"Collagen deposition from fibroblast activation and TGF-β signaling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hyaline membranes are the characteristic histologic feature of the acute (exudative) phase of diffuse alveolar damage in ARDS. Robbins describes them as fibrin-rich edema fluid mixed with the remnants of necrotic epithelial cells, lining the alveolar ducts (Ch 15): endothelial injury makes capillaries leaky so protein-rich exudate floods the alveoli, and necrosis of alveolar epithelium contributes the cellular debris. Similar membranes are seen in neonatal respiratory distress syndrome. They are not mucin from goblet cells, neutrophil extracellular traps, proliferating type II cells (a later reparative change) or collagen from fibroblasts (the organizing phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyaline membranes are fibrin-rich, protein-rich edema fluid inspissated with debris from necrotic alveolar epithelial cells; they are the hallmark of diffuse alveolar damage and look like the membranes of neonatal hyaline membrane disease. COMLEX: Early loss of functional surfactant stiffens the lungs, which is why ARDS patients need intubation and high ventilatory pressures.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — surfactant deficiency of the newborn, the other setting where hyaline membranes form","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q07_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the key morphological features of both chronic pulmonary congestion and the acute exudative stage of ARDS","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman with chronic mitral stenosis undergoes lung biopsy showing abundant macrophages containing golden-brown pigment within alveoli, along with thickened alveolar walls and interstitial fibrosis. The lungs appear firm and brown grossly. What are the pigment-laden cells and gross appearance called?","choices":{"A":"Ferruginous bodies and asbestosis","B":"Heart failure cells and brown induration","C":"Anthracotic pigment and coal workers' pneumoconiosis","D":"Cholesterol clefts and organizing pneumonia","E":"Carbon-laden macrophages and smoker's lung"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In long-standing pulmonary congestion (e.g., mitral stenosis), alveolar microhemorrhages lead to red blood cell extravasation. Alveolar macrophages phagocytose the red cells and accumulate hemosiderin (iron-containing breakdown product of hemoglobin), appearing golden-brown on histology. These are called \"heart failure cells.\" Hemosiderin-laden macrophages become abundant, and fibrosis and thickening of alveolar walls cause the soggy lungs to become firm and brown grossly—termed \"brown induration.\" This impairs respiratory function and predisposes to infection. Ferruginous (asbestos) bodies are iron-coated asbestos fibers of asbestosis, anthracotic pigment is black carbon, and organizing pneumonia fills airspaces with connective tissue plugs; none matches hemosiderin-laden macrophages from chronic congestion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Long-standing congestion, classically mitral stenosis, causes alveolar microhemorrhages; macrophages that ingest the red cells fill with hemosiderin ('heart failure' cells), and fibrosis with thickened alveolar walls turns the soggy lungs firm and brown (brown induration). COMLEX: These changes impair respiratory function and predispose to infection, so chronic congestion is not a benign finding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — diffuse alveolar hemorrhage syndromes, where hemosiderin-laden macrophages also mark old bleeding","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q08_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the key morphological features of both chronic pulmonary congestion and the acute exudative stage of ARDS","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old trauma patient develops ARDS. Lung biopsy in the acute phase reveals diffuse alveolar damage with characteristic eosinophilic hyaline membranes lining alveolar ducts. What additional morphologic features would be expected in the acute exudative stage?","choices":{"A":"Hyaline membranes, edema, capillary congestion, and type II cell necrosis","B":"Proliferating fibroblasts within alveolar walls and organizing collagen deposition","C":"Type II pneumocyte hyperplasia reconstituting alveolar lining epithelium","D":"Honeycombing with cystic fibrotic airspaces and architectural distortion","E":"Noncaseating granulomas with multinucleated giant cells and asteroid bodies"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The acute exudative stage of diffuse alveolar damage (DAD/ARDS) shows: (1) intraalveolar hyaline membranes (inspissated protein-rich edema and cellular debris) lining alveolar ducts, (2) interstitial and intraalveolar edema, (3) capillary congestion with microthrombi, and (4) type II pneumocyte necrosis. These lesions are not evenly distributed. Fibroblast proliferation and collagen deposition occur in the later organizing/fibroproliferative phase. Type II pneumocyte hyperplasia occurs during resolution. Honeycombing represents end-stage fibrosis. Noncaseating granulomas with asteroid bodies belong to sarcoidosis, not to diffuse alveolar damage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins' key concept for diffuse alveolar damage: hyaline membranes lining alveolar walls with edema, scattered neutrophils and macrophages, and epithelial cell necrosis, plus congestion and fibrin deposition in the acute exudative stage. COMLEX: Profound dyspnea and tachypnea herald ARDS, followed by diffuse bilateral infiltrates and hypoxemia that may be refractory to oxygen therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 6 — the layers of the respiratory membrane that edema and hyaline membranes thicken","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Honeycombing represents end-st"}},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q09_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the key morphological features of both chronic pulmonary congestion and the acute exudative stage of ARDS","batch":1,"stem":"A patient who survives the acute phase of ARDS enters the organizing/fibroproliferative phase. What cellular events and mediators drive this phase of injury?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophil degranulation with protease and reactive oxygen species release","B":"Type II pneumocyte necrosis with surfactant abnormalities","C":"Endothelial activation with increased adhesion molecule expression","D":"Macrophage removal of debris with TGF-β and PDGF release stimulating fibrosis","E":"Alveolar epithelial fluid clearance restoring normal gas exchange"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the organizing/fibroproliferative phase, if inflammatory triggers abate, macrophages remove intraalveolar debris and release fibrogenic cytokines including TGF-β (transforming growth factor-β) and PDGF (platelet-derived growth factor). These factors stimulate fibroblast growth and collagen deposition, leading to fibrosis of alveolar walls. Some patients enter extended resolution (months) with type II pneumocyte proliferation replacing type I cells and endothelial restoration. Others develop impaired cardiopulmonary function from persistent fibrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: If the inflammatory triggers abate, macrophages clear the intraalveolar debris and release fibrogenic TGF-β and platelet-derived growth factor, which stimulate fibroblast growth and collagen deposition in the alveolar walls. COMLEX: ARDS remains lethal despite supportive care — a 2016 study of ICUs in 50 countries found mortality of 35% for mild, 40% for moderate, and 46% for severe ARDS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — IPF, where profibrotic factors such as TGF-β likewise drive interstitial fibrosis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q10_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the major categories of pulmonary edema and list the underlying clinical etiologies associated with each","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old mountaineer develops dyspnea, cough, and hypoxemia at 4,500 meters altitude without evidence of heart failure or infection. Chest imaging shows bilateral pulmonary infiltrates. What category of pulmonary edema is this, and what is its underlying mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Hemodynamic edema from increased hydrostatic pressure","B":"Edema of undetermined origin (high-altitude edema)","C":"Noncardiogenic edema from bacterial pneumonia","D":"Hemodynamic edema from decreased oncotic pressure","E":"Edema from direct alveolar injury"},"correct":"B","explanation":"High-altitude pulmonary edema (HAPE) is classified as \"edema of undetermined origin\" as the exact mechanism remains incompletely understood. It occurs at high altitude in otherwise healthy individuals. Proposed mechanisms include hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction causing uneven perfusion and stress failure of capillaries, increased sympathetic activity, and altered fluid balance. HAPE is not purely hemodynamic or injury-related but represents a unique category. Treatment includes descent and oxygen administration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins files high-altitude and neurogenic (CNS trauma) pulmonary edema under 'edema of undetermined origin'; Guyton's proposed mechanism is uneven hypoxic arteriolar constriction that forces flow through fewer open vessels, raising capillary pressure there until local edema forms. COMLEX: Guyton notes that breathing oxygen usually reverses the process within hours.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 7 — HAPE pathophysiology and clinical findings","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"HAPE is not purely hemodynamic","C":"HAPE is not purely hemodynamic","D":"HAPE is not purely hemodynamic"}},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q11_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the major categories of pulmonary edema and list the underlying clinical etiologies associated with each","batch":1,"stem":"Robbins subdivides pulmonary edema due to alveolar wall injury into direct and indirect injury. Five ICU patients develop noncardiogenic pulmonary edema. In which patient does the edema arise from indirect rather than direct alveolar injury?","choices":{"A":"A woman who aspirated gastric contents","B":"A boy rescued after near-drowning","C":"A firefighter after heavy smoke inhalation","D":"A man after multiple blood transfusions","E":"A woman after thoracic radiation therapy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In Robbins Table 15.1, edema due to alveolar wall (microvascular or epithelial) injury is divided into direct injury, from bacterial pneumonia, inhaled gases such as high concentrations of oxygen or smoke, liquid aspiration of gastric contents or near-drowning, radiation, and lung trauma, and indirect injury, from systemic inflammatory response syndrome (sepsis, burns, pancreatitis, extensive trauma), blood transfusion, and drugs and chemicals such as bleomycin, methadone, amphotericin B, heroin, cocaine, kerosene, and paraquat. Transfusion-related edema is therefore the indirect injury. Gastric aspiration, near-drowning, smoke inhalation, and radiation all injure the alveolar wall directly from the airspace or the lung itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins divides injury edema into direct injury (bacterial pneumonia, inhaled high-concentration oxygen or smoke, aspiration of gastric contents or near-drowning, radiation, lung trauma) and indirect injury (SIRS from sepsis, burns, pancreatitis, or extensive trauma; transfusion; drugs such as bleomycin, heroin, cocaine, and paraquat). COMLEX: Transfusion-associated lung injury is on Robbins' list of ARDS triggers, so new hypoxemia during or after transfusion should raise it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 3 — oxygen toxicity, one of the direct inhaled-gas injuries in the same table","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Gastric aspiration, near-drown","E":"Gastric aspiration, near-drown"}},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q12_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the major categories of pulmonary edema and list the underlying clinical etiologies associated with each","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman with breast cancer develops progressive dyspnea. CT shows diffuse interstitial thickening and pleural effusions. Biopsy reveals tumor infiltration of pulmonary lymphatics. What mechanism explains the pulmonary edema?","choices":{"A":"Lymphatic obstruction preventing normal fluid clearance","B":"Increased hydrostatic pressure from tumor compression of pulmonary veins","C":"Direct tumor invasion causing alveolar epithelial injury","D":"Decreased oncotic pressure from cancer cachexia","E":"Tumor-secreted cytokines causing endothelial activation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lymphatic obstruction is a rare cause of pulmonary edema. Lymphatics normally clear excess interstitial fluid; when obstructed by tumor (lymphangitic carcinomatosis), sarcoidosis, or post-radiation fibrosis, fluid accumulates in the interstitium. Breast, gastric, and lung cancers commonly cause lymphangitic spread. Imaging shows interstitial thickening and Kerley B lines. Unlike hemodynamic edema, lymphatic obstruction produces chronic progressive symptoms. The edema is typically interstitial rather than alveolar.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lymphatic obstruction is the rare third hemodynamic cause of pulmonary edema in Robbins Table 15.1; normally the pulmonary lymphatics pump continually filtered fluid back to the circulation and keep interstitial pressure slightly negative so the alveoli stay dry (Guyton). COMLEX: When tumor blocks those lymphatics, the fluid that capillaries filter all the time has no exit, so edema can form without any rise in capillary pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 10 — metastatic disease of the lung, here spreading through the lymphatics","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q13_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the detailed pathogenesis of ARDS, focusing on the cascade of inflammation, endothelial activation, neutrophil involvement, and formation of intraalveolar exudate","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old man develops ARDS following aspiration of gastric contents. Which cytokine released by resident alveolar macrophages sensing pneumocyte injury acts on neighboring endothelium to initiate endothelial activation?","choices":{"A":"Interleukin-10 (anti-inflammatory cytokine)","B":"TGF-β (transforming growth factor-beta)","C":"TNF (tumor necrosis factor)","D":"GM-CSF (granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor)","E":"Surfactant protein-D"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When pneumocyte injury occurs (e.g., from aspiration), resident alveolar macrophages act as immune sentinels sensing the damage. They secrete tumor necrosis factor (TNF) and other mediators that act on neighboring pulmonary endothelium, initiating endothelial activation. TNF is a key early proinflammatory cytokine in ARDS pathogenesis. Activated endothelial cells then express adhesion molecules (E-selectin, ICAM-1, VCAM-1), procoagulant proteins, and chemokines that recruit neutrophils and amplify inflammation. TGF-β is a fibrogenic cytokine released later during the organizing phase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Resident alveolar macrophages act as sentinels that sense pneumocyte injury and secrete tumor necrosis factor onto the neighboring endothelium; Robbins' figure of the early injured alveolus also shows IL-1 and reactive oxygen species. COMLEX: Gastric aspiration is a direct lung injury in Robbins' classification and one of the conditions most often associated with ARDS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 2 — aspiration pneumonia as a clinical pneumonia syndrome","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"TGF-β is a fibrogenic cytokine"}},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q14_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the detailed pathogenesis of ARDS, focusing on the cascade of inflammation, endothelial activation, neutrophil involvement, and formation of intraalveolar exudate","batch":1,"stem":"During ARDS pathogenesis, activated endothelium expresses increased levels of specific molecules that facilitate neutrophil recruitment. What three major categories of molecules are upregulated on activated endothelium?","choices":{"A":"Surfactant proteins, phospholipids, and collectins","B":"Matrix metalloproteinases, collagenases, and elastases","C":"Immunoglobulins, complement components, and acute phase reactants","D":"Growth factors, angiogenic factors, and anti-apoptotic proteins","E":"Adhesion molecules, procoagulant proteins, and chemokines"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Endothelial activation in ARDS leads to expression of: (1) adhesion molecules (E-selectin, P-selectin, ICAM-1, VCAM-1) that mediate neutrophil rolling and firm adhesion; (2) procoagulant proteins (tissue factor) that promote microthrombi formation; and (3) chemokines (IL-8, MCP-1) that attract and activate neutrophils. Together, these changes facilitate neutrophil extravasation into alveoli and amplify the inflammatory cascade. Some mediators also directly injure endothelial cells, worsening capillary leak and edema formation. Proteases and elastases are released by the neutrophils after they arrive, not expressed by the endothelium, and immunoglobulins, complement, and acute phase reactants are not among the endothelial products Robbins lists.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cytokines induce activated endothelial cells to express more adhesion molecules, procoagulant proteins, and chemokines; neutrophils then adhere, migrate into the interstitium and alveoli, and degranulate. COMLEX: Disseminated intravascular coagulation appears in Robbins' list of conditions associated with ARDS, reflecting the procoagulant side of the injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 7 — the neutrophilic intra-alveolar inflammation of bacterial pneumonia","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Proteases and elastases are re"}},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q15_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the detailed pathogenesis of ARDS, focusing on the cascade of inflammation, endothelial activation, neutrophil involvement, and formation of intraalveolar exudate","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with ARDS develops worsening ventilation-perfusion mismatch contributing to severe hypoxemia. What anatomic distribution pattern of diffuse alveolar damage explains this physiologic derangement?","choices":{"A":"Uniform involvement of all lung regions with equal compliance","B":"Exclusively subpleural distribution with central sparing","C":"Exclusive involvement of upper lobes with lower lobe sparing","D":"Central airway involvement with peripheral airspace sparing","E":"Uneven: stiff, poorly aerated regions beside near-normal ones"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The lesions in ARDS/DAD are NOT evenly distributed throughout the lungs. There are typically areas that are stiff and poorly aerated (consolidated regions with edema, hyaline membranes, and atelectasis) alongside regions with nearly normal compliance and ventilation. Poorly aerated regions continue to be perfused (blood flow continues), creating ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch where perfusion exceeds ventilation. This mismatch exacerbates hypoxemia and cyanosis, explaining why supplemental oxygen alone often fails to correct hypoxemia in ARDS. Diffuse alveolar damage involves the alveoli in an uneven patchwork, not an upper-lobe-only pattern or the central airways.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ARDS lesions are not evenly distributed — stiff, poorly aerated regions sit beside regions of nearly normal compliance and ventilation — and because the poorly aerated areas stay perfused, ventilation–perfusion mismatch worsens hypoxemia and cyanosis. COMLEX: That mismatch is why ARDS hypoxemia may resist oxygen therapy, and respiratory acidosis can develop.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 12 — the V/Q ratio and its effect on alveolar PO2 and PCO2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q16_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the key morphological features of both chronic pulmonary congestion and the acute exudative stage of ARDS","batch":1,"stem":"A 74-year-old man dies of acute decompensated left-sided heart failure. Sections from the lower lobes show pulmonary edema. Which histologic description best fits the alveolar findings of this hemodynamic edema, as distinct from the injury-related edema of ARDS?","choices":{"A":"Waxy hyaline membranes lining distended alveolar ducts","B":"Neutrophil-rich exudate with fibrin filling alveoli","C":"Type II cell hyperplasia with granulation tissue","D":"Engorged capillaries with a fine, pale pink transudate","E":"Polypoid plugs of loose fibroblastic tissue in airspaces"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins describes hemodynamic pulmonary edema, most commonly from left-sided heart failure, as accumulating first in the basal regions of the lower lobes, where hydrostatic pressure is greatest (dependent edema); histologically the alveolar capillaries are engorged and the alveoli contain an intraalveolar transudate that appears as finely granular pale pink material, sometimes with microhemorrhages and hemosiderin-laden macrophages. Waxy hyaline membranes made of fibrin-rich edema fluid and necrotic epithelial remnants mark the acute exudative stage of ARDS (diffuse alveolar damage). A neutrophil-rich fibrinous exudate filling alveoli describes bacterial pneumonia. Type II pneumocyte proliferation with granulation tissue is the organizing stage of diffuse alveolar damage. Polypoid plugs of loose fibroblastic tissue in airspaces describe organizing pneumonia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hemodynamic edema shows engorged alveolar capillaries and a finely granular, pale pink intraalveolar transudate, sometimes with microhemorrhages; injury-related edema instead leaks a protein-rich exudate that organizes into hyaline membranes. COMLEX: The basal, dependent distribution of hemodynamic edema follows hydrostatic pressure, which is greatest in the lower lobes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 8 — the zone differences in pressure and blood flow down the upright lung","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q17_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the key morphological features of both chronic pulmonary congestion and the acute exudative stage of ARDS","batch":1,"stem":"A 47-year-old man dies on day 3 of ARDS after severe pancreatitis. At autopsy, before any sections are cut, which gross appearance of the lungs would the pathologist expect in this acute exudative stage?","choices":{"A":"Light, pale, voluminous lungs","B":"Firm, brown, indurated lungs","C":"Heavy, firm, red, boggy lungs","D":"Small lungs with honeycomb cysts","E":"Lungs with apical bullae and blebs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Robbins states that in the acute exudative stage of ARDS the lungs are heavy, firm, red, and boggy, reflecting congestion, interstitial and intraalveolar edema, inflammation, fibrin deposition, and diffuse alveolar damage with hyaline membranes; radiologically this appears as patchy ground-glass opacities with or without consolidation. Light, voluminous lungs that overlap the heart describe advanced emphysema. Firm, brown lungs with thickened alveolar walls are the brown induration of long-standing congestion, as in mitral stenosis. Small lungs with honeycomb cysts describe end-stage fibrosis such as UIP, which may follow ARDS in a minority of survivors but not on day 3. Apical bullae and subpleural blebs are features of emphysema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In the acute exudative stage of ARDS the lungs are heavy, firm, red, and boggy, reflecting congestion, interstitial and intraalveolar edema, inflammation, fibrin deposition, and diffuse alveolar damage; imaging shows patchy ground-glass opacities with or without consolidation. COMLEX: Robbins attributes most ARDS deaths to sepsis, multiorgan failure, or severe lung injury; survivors usually recover pulmonary function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — alveolar changes in pneumonia versus emphysema, other gross contrasts in lung weight and aeration","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q18_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the key morphological features of both chronic pulmonary congestion and the acute exudative stage of ARDS","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with ARDS who survives the acute phase develops progressive pulmonary fibrosis. Histology shows fibroblast proliferation within alveolar walls, collagen deposition, and type II pneumocyte hyperplasia. What phase of diffuse alveolar damage does this represent?","choices":{"A":"Organizing/fibroproliferative phase with attempted repair","B":"Acute exudative phase with hyaline membrane formation","C":"Resolution phase with complete restoration of normal architecture","D":"Chronic congestion phase with hemosiderin-laden macrophages","E":"Progressive massive fibrosis phase similar to complicated pneumoconiosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The organizing/fibroproliferative phase follows the acute phase if inflammatory triggers abate. Macrophages remove intraalveolar debris and release TGF-β and PDGF, stimulating fibroblast proliferation and collagen deposition within alveolar walls. Type II pneumocytes proliferate attempting to reconstitute the alveolar lining. Some patients enter extended resolution with eventual restoration; others develop persistent fibrosis with impaired function. This contrasts with the acute exudative phase (hyaline membranes, edema, necrosis) that precedes it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In the proliferative (organizing) stage of diffuse alveolar damage, type II pneumocytes proliferate and granulation tissue forms in alveolar walls and spaces; later, residual type II cells replace type I cells and uninjured capillary endothelium restores the vessels. COMLEX: Some survivors pass through a resolution phase lasting months, while a minority are left with interstitial fibrosis and chronic lung disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — type II pneumocytes, the progenitors that re-line injured alveoli","isHighYield":false}],"28":[{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q01_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — What are the clinical features of cystic fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy with cystic fibrosis presents with large, foul-smelling stools, abdominal distention, and failure to thrive. Laboratory studies reveal deficiencies of vitamins A, D, E, and K. What is the underlying pathophysiologic mechanism responsible for these clinical manifestations?","choices":{"A":"Chronic pulmonary infections with Pseudomonas aeruginosa","B":"Biliary cirrhosis with hepatic synthetic dysfunction","C":"Intestinal obstruction from meconium ileus","D":"Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency with fat malabsorption","E":"Endocrine pancreatic insufficiency with diabetes mellitus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency occurs in 85-90% of CF patients with \"severe\" CFTR mutations on both alleles (e.g., ΔF508/ΔF508). Thick viscous secretions obstruct pancreatic ducts, leading to protein and fat malabsorption. This causes steatorrhea (large foul-smelling stools), abdominal distention, and poor weight gain. Fat malabsorption leads to deficiency of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K), which can cause avitaminosis manifestations. Hypoproteinemia may be severe enough to cause generalized edema. Pseudomonas infection drives the lung disease, not steatorrhea, and the vitamin loss here comes from pancreatic fat malabsorption rather than from biliary cirrhosis, which affects only a minority of patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thick secretions plug the pancreatic ducts, causing exocrine atrophy and fibrosis; the resulting fat and protein malabsorption brings large foul-smelling stools, abdominal distention, poor weight gain, and deficiency of vitamins A, D, E, and K, often within the first year of life. COMLEX: FA lists pancreatic enzyme replacement (pancrelipase) for the pancreatic insufficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 9 — clinically differentiating obstructive lung diseases, where extrapulmonary clues point to CF","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Pseudomonas infection drives t","B":"Pseudomonas infection drives t"}},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q02_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — What are the clinical features of cystic fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 25-year-old man with cystic fibrosis and his wife present for infertility evaluation. Physical examination and semen analysis reveal obstructive azoospermia. What anatomic abnormality most commonly accounts for infertility in men with CF?","choices":{"A":"Congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens","B":"Testicular atrophy from chronic systemic illness","C":"Obstruction of seminal vesicles by viscous secretions","D":"Hypothalamic-pituitary dysfunction from malnutrition","E":"Epididymal fibrosis from recurrent infections"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Approximately 95% of males with CF are infertile due to obstructive azoospermia, most commonly from congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens (CBAVD). Robbins (Ch 10) calls it a frequent finding in these patients, caused by biallelic CFTR mutations. CBAVD can also occur as an isolated finding in males with CFTR mutations who have nonclassic/atypical CF without other manifestations. The vas deferens fails to develop properly due to abnormal CFTR function during embryogenesis. Robbins attributes male CF infertility to obstructive azoospermia from congenital absence of the vas deferens; testicular atrophy and hypothalamic-pituitary failure would cause nonobstructive azoospermia, and epididymal infection is not the described mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: About 95% of men with CF who reach adulthood are infertile from obstructive azoospermia, most often congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens, which is caused by biallelic CFTR mutations in 80% of cases. COMLEX: FA notes spermatogenesis may be unaffected in CF, while women are subfertile from amenorrhea and abnormally thick cervical mucus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 8 — bronchiectasis, including Kartagener syndrome, whose affected males are also infertile","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Robbins attributes male CF inf","D":"Robbins attributes male CF inf","E":"Robbins attributes male CF inf"}},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q03_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — What are the clinical features of cystic fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis presents with acute abdominal pain and jaundice. Ultrasound reveals common bile duct obstruction. Aside from acute biliary obstruction, what chronic hepatic complication develops in less than 10% of CF patients?","choices":{"A":"Primary sclerosing cholangitis with bile duct strictures","B":"Hepatocellular carcinoma from chronic inflammation","C":"Diffuse biliary cirrhosis from chronic obstruction","D":"Steatohepatitis from fat-soluble vitamin deficiency","E":"Portal vein thrombosis from hypercoagulability"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Significant liver disease occurs late in CF natural history and is the third most common cause of death after cardiopulmonary and transplantation-related complications. Diffuse biliary cirrhosis develops in <10% of CF patients from chronic bile duct obstruction by viscous secretions. Symptomatic or biochemical liver disease typically begins at puberty with prevalence ~15%. Asymptomatic hepatomegaly may be present in up to one-third of individuals. Acute obstruction of common bile duct can occur from stones or sludge. Hepatic steatosis is actually common in CF and is not a rare complication or one caused by vitamin deficiency, and portal vein thrombosis is not among the hepatic manifestations Robbins lists.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mucus plugs the bile canaliculi; focal biliary cirrhosis develops in about one-third of CF patients, but diffuse biliary cirrhosis with nodularity of the whole liver in fewer than 10%. COMLEX: After cardiopulmonary and transplantation-related complications, liver disease is the commonest cause of death in CF; it usually appears around puberty (prevalence about 15%), and stones or sludge in the common bile duct cause acute jaundice.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — lung transplantation, whose complications rank ahead of liver disease as a cause of CF deaths","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q04_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the knowledge of pathology, pathogenesis, and morphology to Cystic Fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A newborn with cystic fibrosis develops intestinal obstruction within the first 24 hours of life from thick, tenacious meconium. What is this clinical presentation called?","choices":{"A":"Distal intestinal obstruction syndrome of older patients","B":"Midgut volvulus from intestinal malrotation","C":"Rectal prolapse from chronic straining","D":"Meconium ileus from inspissated meconium","E":"Necrotizing enterocolitis of prematurity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Meconium ileus is intestinal obstruction in neonates caused by abnormally thick, viscous meconium that obstructs the small intestine (typically terminal ileum). It may be the presenting sign of CF (Robbins Ch 10). The thick meconium results from abnormal CFTR function in intestinal epithelium, causing decreased chloride and water secretion into the lumen. This produces dehydrated, inspissated intestinal contents. Meconium ileus is virtually pathognomonic for CF when it occurs. Rectal prolapse is a later complication of persistent diarrhea in children, and necrotizing enterocolitis is a hazard of prematurity, not an obstruction by thick meconium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meconium ileus — small-bowel obstruction by thick, viscid mucus plugs — brings 5–10% of CF cases to attention at birth or soon after; older patients can develop distal intestinal obstruction with recurrent right lower quadrant pain and a palpable mass. COMLEX: FA's newborn screen for CF measures immunoreactive trypsinogen, which rises when pancreatic ducts clog.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 8 — bronchiectasis, the lung consequence of the same viscid secretions","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q05_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the knowledge of pathology, pathogenesis, and morphology to Cystic Fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy with CF undergoes sweat chloride testing. The test reveals markedly elevated sweat chloride concentration. What is the pathophysiologic mechanism underlying hypertonic sweat production in cystic fibrosis?","choices":{"A":"Increased chloride secretion into sweat duct lumen","B":"Increased sodium reabsorption via hyperactive ENaC","C":"Decreased luminal bicarbonate secretion","D":"Impaired mucus secretion in sweat glands","E":"Decreased chloride reabsorption from sweat duct lumen"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In sweat gland ducts, the major function of CFTR is to reabsorb luminal chloride ions and augment sodium reabsorption via ENaC. Loss of CFTR function leads to DECREASED reabsorption of sodium chloride from the sweat duct lumen, resulting in production of hypertonic sweat with high NaCl content. This is the sine qua non of classic CF and forms the basis for the sweat chloride test (First Aid 2025: increased Cl⁻ concentration in pilocarpine-induced sweat). Parents often detect \"salty\" sweat in affected infants.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In sweat ducts CFTR reabsorbs luminal chloride and augments sodium reabsorption through ENaC, so loss of CFTR leaves NaCl in the duct and produces hypertonic, salty sweat — the sine qua non of classic CF and the basis of the sweat chloride test. COMLEX: FA notes salt and water lost in sweat can cause contraction alkalosis and hypokalemia, an effect like a loop diuretic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — the tissue-specific functions of CFTR and its mutation classes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q06_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the knowledge of pathology, pathogenesis, and morphology to Cystic Fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"An 18-year-old with CF has chronic pulmonary infections with Pseudomonas aeruginosa. What is the primary pathophysiologic mechanism in the airways that predisposes to these recurrent infections?","choices":{"A":"Isotonic, low-volume surface fluid; poor ciliary clearance","B":"Hypertonic surface fluid causing osmotic damage to epithelium","C":"Decreased chloride concentration in airway secretions","D":"Hypoalbuminemia causing decreased oncotic pressure","E":"Primary immunodeficiency with neutrophil dysfunction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In respiratory epithelium, CFTR is crucial for luminal chloride secretion. CFTR mutations cause: (1) loss/reduction of chloride secretion into lumen, and (2) increased luminal sodium absorption (loss of ENaC inhibition). These ion changes increase passive water reabsorption from lumen, lowering the water content of the surface fluid layer. Unlike sweat, there's NO difference in salt concentration—instead, CF airways have an isotonic but LOW-VOLUME surface fluid layer. This dehydration leads to defective mucociliary action and accumulation of viscid secretions that obstruct passages and predispose to infections. Plasma oncotic pressure governs edema, not mucus clearance, and CF is not a primary immunodeficiency: infections persist because mucociliary clearance fails, and the immune reactions that do occur cannot clear biofilm-protected Pseudomonas.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In airway epithelium CFTR loss cuts chloride secretion and frees ENaC from inhibition, so sodium and water are reabsorbed; the surface fluid stays isotonic but shrinks in volume, crippling mucociliary clearance. COMLEX: Static, hypoxic mucus favors alginate-producing mucoid P. aeruginosa that forms protective biofilms; FA lists chest physiotherapy, dornase alfa, and hypertonic saline to clear the mucus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 3 — P. aeruginosa among the key bacterial pneumonia organisms","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Unlike sweat, there's NO diffe","C":"Unlike sweat, there's NO diffe"}},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q07_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Detail the structure and function of the cystic fibrosis gene including normal structure and function and the mutational spectrum of the disease","batch":1,"stem":"The CFTR protein consists of multiple functional domains. What are the two cytoplasmic domains that bind and hydrolyze ATP, which is essential for opening and closing the chloride channel pore?","choices":{"A":"Transmembrane domains containing six α-helices each","B":"Regulatory domain containing protein kinase A phosphorylation sites","C":"Nucleotide-binding domains (NBDs)","D":"Anion selectivity filter regulating chloride conductance","E":"PDZ-binding motif for cytoskeletal anchoring"},"correct":"C","explanation":"CFTR structure includes: (1) two transmembrane domains (each with six α-helices) forming the chloride channel, (2) two cytoplasmic nucleotide-binding domains (NBDs) that bind and hydrolyze ATP—essential for opening/closing the channel pore, and (3) a regulatory domain (R domain) with protein kinase A phosphorylation sites. CFTR activation requires cAMP-induced protein kinase A phosphorylation of the R domain. ATP binding/hydrolysis at NBDs is enhanced by R domain phosphorylation and controls channel gating.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CFTR has two transmembrane domains of six α-helices that form the chloride pore, two cytoplasmic nucleotide-binding domains where ATP binding and hydrolysis open and close the channel, and an R domain that cAMP-activated protein kinase A phosphorylates. COMLEX: Gating (class III) mutations abolish this ATP-dependent activation, which is why potentiators that hold the gate open (ivacaftor in FA) help them.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 13 — β2-adrenergic bronchodilation, which works through the same cAMP second messenger","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q08_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Detail the structure and function of the cystic fibrosis gene including normal structure and function and the mutational spectrum of the disease","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with CF has the ΔF508 mutation, the most common CFTR mutation found in ~70% of Northern European patients. What class of CFTR mutation is ΔF508, and what is its effect on the CFTR protein?","choices":{"A":"Class I null mutation causing premature termination","B":"Class III gating mutation preventing ATP binding","C":"Class IV conduction mutation reducing chloride flow","D":"Class II processing mutation causing ER retention","E":"Class V production mutation reducing mRNA synthesis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ΔF508 (deletion of phenylalanine at position 508) is a Class II processing mutation. These mutations cause defective protein folding and impaired trafficking from endoplasmic reticulum to Golgi apparatus. The misfolded protein is recognized by ER quality control and degraded by proteasomes before reaching the cell surface. Class II mutations result in near-complete lack of CFTR protein at the apical epithelial surface. This is a \"severe\" mutation (<10% residual CFTR activity) associated with classic CF phenotype.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ΔF508 deletes three nucleotides coding for phenylalanine at position 508 — a class II processing mutation in which the misfolded protein is retained in the ER and degraded before reaching the apical membrane; it is found in about 70% of Northern European CF patients. COMLEX: Correctors (lumacaftor, tezacaftor in FA) help the protein fold and traffic to the surface, and corrector–potentiator combinations are approved for patients with two copies of ΔF508.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — panacinar emphysema from α1-antitrypsin deficiency, another lung disease of a misfolded protein","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q09_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Detail the structure and function of the cystic fibrosis gene including normal structure and function and the mutational spectrum of the disease","batch":1,"stem":"A CF patient has one \"severe\" CFTR mutation (ΔF508) and one \"mild\" mutation (R117H). Which organ system manifestation shows the most consistent genotype-phenotype correlation based on mutation severity?","choices":{"A":"Pulmonary disease severity and age of onset","B":"Pancreatic insufficiency and exocrine function","C":"Male fertility and vas deferens development","D":"Hepatic cirrhosis progression","E":"Sinonasal polyp formation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Genotype-phenotype correlation is MOST CONSISTENT for pancreatic disease. Two \"severe\" mutations (e.g., ΔF508/ΔF508) cause virtual absence of CFTR function and are associated with pancreatic insufficiency (85-90% of patients). However, presence of one \"mild\" mutation (e.g., ΔF508/R117H) can prevent pancreatic insufficiency, resulting in pancreas-sufficient phenotype (10-15%). In contrast, genotype-phenotype correlations are far LESS consistent for pulmonary disease due to secondary modifiers (environmental factors, modifier genes, infections). Pancreas-sufficient patients retain enough function to not require enzyme supplementation. Absent vas deferens occurs even in mild, nonclassic CF with no other features, so it does not track mutation severity, and Robbins names the pancreas, not the liver or sinuses, as the organ whose involvement follows the genotype.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Genotype predicts pancreatic status best: one mild allele with some residual CFTR activity can prevent the pancreatic insufficiency seen with two severe alleles, whereas lung disease correlates poorly because modifier genes (MBL2, TGFB1, IFRD1) and infections intervene. COMLEX: Patients who share a CFTR genotype can follow very different pulmonary courses, so lung prognosis cannot be read from the genotype alone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 1 — pulmonary host defenses, the arena in which CF's modifier genes act","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Absent vas deferens occurs eve","E":"Absent vas deferens occurs eve"}},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q10_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — What are the clinical features of cystic fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 3-year-old child with cystic fibrosis presents with chronic cough and sputum culture positive for Staphylococcus aureus. By what age do 80% of patients with classic CF harbor Pseudomonas aeruginosa?","choices":{"A":"6 years of age","B":"10 years of age","C":"18 years of age","D":"25 years of age","E":"35 years of age"},"correct":"C","explanation":"By 18 years of age, 80% of patients with classic cystic fibrosis harbor Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Cardiorespiratory complications, particularly persistent lung infections and obstructive pulmonary disease, are the most common cause of death in CF patients in the United States. P. aeruginosa colonization is associated with more severe pulmonary disease and faster decline in lung function. Additionally, 3.5% of patients harbor Burkholderia cepacia by age 18, which portends even worse prognosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins gives two figures — 80% of classic CF patients harbor P. aeruginosa by age 18 (clinical section), and approximately 80% are colonized by age 20 (pathogenesis section); S. aureus, H. influenzae, and P. aeruginosa are the commonest lung pathogens, and FA places S. aureus in infancy and early childhood and P. aeruginosa in adulthood. COMLEX: Burkholderia cepacia (3.5% by age 18) can cause fulminant 'cepacia syndrome' with higher mortality.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 6 — complications of bacterial pneumonia predicted by the causative organism","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q11_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — What are the clinical features of cystic fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman with CF develops glucose intolerance and diabetes mellitus requiring insulin therapy. What is the underlying mechanism for endocrine pancreatic dysfunction in adult CF patients?","choices":{"A":"Autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells","B":"Insulin resistance from chronic inflammation and malnutrition","C":"Pancreatic beta cell mutations in CFTR gene","D":"Ectopic ACTH production from pulmonary tumors","E":"Destruction of pancreatic parenchyma including the islets"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Endocrine pancreatic insufficiency (i.e., diabetes mellitus) occurs in up to 50% of adults with CF and is thought to be caused by destruction of pancreatic parenchyma including the islets. Chronic obstruction and inflammation lead to progressive fibrosis and atrophy of both exocrine and endocrine components. This is CF-related diabetes (CFRD), which differs from type 1 diabetes (not autoimmune) and type 2 diabetes (not primarily insulin resistance). CFRD is the most common comorbidity in adults with CF. Ectopic ACTH is a paraneoplastic product of lung cancer that causes Cushing syndrome, unrelated to CF diabetes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diabetes develops in up to 50% of adults with CF and is attributed to destruction of pancreatic parenchyma including the islets; in severe pancreatic disease the ducts plug completely, the exocrine glands atrophy, and fibrosis leaves islets within a fibrofatty stroma. COMLEX: FA describes CF pancreatic disease progressing from exocrine insufficiency to endocrine dysfunction (CF-related diabetes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — lung transplantation, a late therapy for end-stage CF lung disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q12_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — What are the clinical features of cystic fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy with CF develops recurrent nasal polyps requiring surgical removal. What percentage of CF patients develop recurrent sinonasal polyps?","choices":{"A":"5% of individuals with CF","B":"50% to 60% of individuals with CF","C":"10% to 25% of individuals with CF","D":"75% of individuals with CF","E":"Less than 1% of individuals with CF"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Recurrent sinonasal polyps occur in 10% to 25% of individuals with CF. Children who present with nasal polyps should be tested for CF, as this may be the presenting sign. The polyps result from chronic sinusitis and inflammation secondary to thick, viscid secretions obstructing the paranasal sinuses. Other upper respiratory manifestations include chronic rhinosinusitis and pansinusitis, which are nearly universal in CF patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recurrent sinonasal polyps occur in 10–25% of people with CF, so a child who presents with nasal polyps should be tested for CF. COMLEX: In an adult, nasal polyps with asthma and NSAID sensitivity point instead to aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (FA).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease, the asthma phenotype that is the other polyp-associated airway disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q13_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the knowledge of pathology, pathogenesis, and morphology to Cystic Fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 5-year-old with CF develops rectal prolapse. What is the underlying mechanism that predisposes CF patients to this gastrointestinal complication?","choices":{"A":"Chronic constipation from intestinal dysmotility","B":"Increased intraabdominal pressure from hepatosplenomegaly","C":"Weakness of pelvic floor muscles from malnutrition","D":"Persistent diarrhea from malabsorption and increased stool bulk","E":"Colonic polyps causing mechanical obstruction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Persistent diarrhea from fat and protein malabsorption may result in rectal prolapse in up to 10% of children with CF. The large volume of bulky, foul-smelling stools secondary to exocrine pancreatic insufficiency increases straining during defecation. This chronic straining, combined with malnutrition weakening supporting tissues, leads to rectal mucosa prolapsing through the anus. Treatment includes pancreatic enzyme replacement to reduce stool volume and manual reduction of the prolapse. Robbins ties rectal prolapse in CF to persistent diarrhea from malabsorption; hepatosplenomegaly and colonic polyps are not part of that mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Persistent diarrhea from fat and protein malabsorption causes rectal prolapse in up to 10% of children with CF, and hypoproteinemia can be severe enough to cause generalized edema. COMLEX: Pancreas-sufficient patients usually escape these GI complications and grow normally, so prolapse in a CF child signals pancreatic insufficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — the clinical features of CF, including pancreatic insufficiency and steatorrhea","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Robbins ties rectal prolapse i"}},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q14_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the knowledge of pathology, pathogenesis, and morphology to Cystic Fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A CF patient undergoes measurement of nasal transepithelial potential difference as part of diagnostic workup. What characteristic finding distinguishes CF from normal individuals?","choices":{"A":"Less negative baseline nasal potential difference","B":"More negative baseline nasal potential difference","C":"No difference in baseline nasal potential difference","D":"Positive baseline nasal potential difference","E":"Fluctuating nasal potential difference with respiration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Individuals with CF demonstrate a significantly MORE NEGATIVE baseline nasal potential difference than controls. This is due to increased sodium absorption (hyperactive ENaC without CFTR inhibition) and decreased chloride secretion through the nasal epithelium. Nasal potential difference measurement is a useful diagnostic adjunct, especially in patients with borderline or normal sweat chloride tests (those with at least one \"mild\" CFTR mutation). This test measures the voltage across the nasal epithelium in vivo.","boardPrep":"Step 1: With CFTR lost, ENaC absorbs more sodium and chloride secretion falls, so people with CF have a significantly more negative baseline nasal transepithelial potential difference than controls (Robbins, FA). COMLEX: Nasal potential difference is the adjunct when sweat chloride is normal or intermediate, as with at least one mild allele; CFTR sequencing is the diagnostic gold standard.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the ciliated respiratory epithelium in which this ion transport is measured","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q15_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the knowledge of pathology, pathogenesis, and morphology to Cystic Fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A newborn with CF is being evaluated for nutritional complications. The mother asks why her infant is at risk for bleeding disorders. What vitamin deficiency secondary to pancreatic insufficiency increases bleeding risk?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin A deficiency causing impaired clotting factor synthesis","B":"Vitamin D deficiency causing impaired calcium-dependent coagulation","C":"Vitamin K deficiency impairing synthesis of factors II, VII, IX, X","D":"Vitamin B12 deficiency causing megaloblastic changes","E":"Vitamin C deficiency causing capillary fragility"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fat malabsorption from exocrine pancreatic insufficiency leads to deficiency of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K). Vitamin K is essential for hepatic synthesis of clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X (the vitamin K-dependent factors), as well as protein C and protein S. Vitamin K deficiency manifests as prolonged PT/INR and increased bleeding risk. Other avitaminoses include: vitamin A (night blindness, xerophthalmia), vitamin D (rickets, osteomalacia), and vitamin E (hemolytic anemia, neuromuscular abnormalities).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pancreatic insufficiency causes fat malabsorption and deficiency of vitamins A, D, E, and K; vitamin K is needed for maturation of clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X and proteins C and S, so deficiency raises PT and aPTT and causes bleeding (FA). COMLEX: Robbins adds that vitamin A deficiency may contribute to squamous metaplasia of the pancreatic duct lining in CF.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — the clinical features of CF, including fat-soluble vitamin deficiency","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Other avitaminoses include: vi","B":"Other avitaminoses include: vi"}},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q16_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Detail the structure and function of the cystic fibrosis gene including normal structure and function and the mutational spectrum of the disease","batch":1,"stem":"CFTR regulates transport of which ion in addition to chloride that is particularly important in pancreatic duct function?","choices":{"A":"Potassium ions via inwardly rectifying channels","B":"Calcium ions via voltage-gated channels","C":"Magnesium ions via ATP-dependent transporters","D":"Bicarbonate ions via direct and SLC26 transport","E":"Phosphate ions via sodium-phosphate cotransporters"},"correct":"D","explanation":"CFTR regulates transport of bicarbonate ions both directly (the CFTR anion channel transports bicarbonate in addition to chloride) and indirectly through reciprocal interactions with SLC26 family anion exchangers co-expressed on the apical surface. In some CFTR mutants, chloride transport is preserved but bicarbonate transport is markedly abnormal. Acidic secretions (due to absent bicarbonate) increase mucin precipitation, duct plugging, and bacterial binding. Pancreatic insufficiency is virtually ALWAYS present when CFTR mutations have abnormal bicarbonate conductance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CFTR's anion channel conducts bicarbonate as well as chloride and also regulates the apical SLC26 anion exchangers; some mutants keep chloride transport but lose bicarbonate transport, secreting acidic fluid that precipitates mucin and plugs ducts. COMLEX: Pancreatic insufficiency is virtually always present when CFTR mutations impair bicarbonate conductance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — pancreatic insufficiency among the clinical features of CF","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q17_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Detail the structure and function of the cystic fibrosis gene including normal structure and function and the mutational spectrum of the disease","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher studies a CFTR mutation affecting intronic splice sites, resulting in reduced amounts of otherwise normal CFTR protein at the cell surface. What class of mutation is this?","choices":{"A":"Class V reduced protein abundance","B":"Class II abnormal protein folding","C":"Class III defective regulation","D":"Class IV decreased conductance","E":"Class I defective protein synthesis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Class V mutations are production mutations that affect intronic splice sites or the CFTR promoter, resulting in reduced amounts of normal CFTR protein. The protein that is produced is functional, but there's simply less of it. Robbins groups classes IV to VI as \"mild\" mutations with less than 20% residual CFTR activity (classes I to III, the \"severe\" ones, have less than 10%; course Pulm SDL 28 PDF p42). Class V mutations can benefit from \"potentiator\" therapy (which improves function of the reduced CFTR present) and \"amplifier\" therapy (which increases the amount of CFTR protein the cell makes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Class V (production) mutations hit intronic splice sites or the CFTR promoter, so less normal protein is made; Robbins groups classes I–III as severe (under 10% residual activity) and classes IV–VI as mild. COMLEX: Potentiators improve the function of the reduced CFTR present in class V, and amplifiers raise the amount of protein made.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 8 — bronchiectasis, where adult-onset idiopathic cases have been tied to CFTR mutations","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q18_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Detail the structure and function of the cystic fibrosis gene including normal structure and function and the mutational spectrum of the disease","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old man presents with recurrent acute pancreatitis but no other features of CF. Genetic testing reveals biallelic CFTR mutations (one \"mild,\" one \"severe\"). What category of CF does this represent?","choices":{"A":"Classic CF with typical multi-system involvement","B":"Nonclassic CF with single-organ involvement","C":"CF carrier state with no clinical manifestations","D":"Acquired CFTR dysfunction from environmental factors","E":"Modifier gene effects masking typical CF phenotype"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Nonclassic or atypical CF describes patients with biallelic CFTR mutations who present with isolated organ manifestations without other features of classic CF. These include: idiopathic chronic pancreatitis, late-onset chronic pulmonary disease, idiopathic bronchiectasis, and obstructive azoospermia from congenital bilateral absence of vas deferens (CBAVD). Most have one \"mild\" and one \"severe\" mutation. Identifying these patients is important for management and genetic counseling. They typically have normal or borderline sweat chloride tests.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nonclassic (atypical) CF means biallelic CFTR mutations — usually one mild and one severe — presenting as single-organ disease without other CF features: idiopathic chronic pancreatitis, late-onset chronic lung disease, idiopathic bronchiectasis, or obstructive azoospermia. COMLEX: Identifying these patients matters for management and for genetic counseling of the family.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 8 — bronchiectasis, where adult-onset idiopathic cases have been linked to CFTR mutations","isHighYield":false}]};
